/* ÇEVİRİ en · ekokuma — arac/ceviri.py ile YAZILDI, elle düzenleme: py arac/ceviri.py uygula.
   Kayıt: id → [metin, kaynak_ozet] (olayda alan başına). Tasarım: denetim/CEVIRI-1103/TASARIM.md */
Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"antlasma5-troyes-1420":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Troyes (1420): the King of England as heir to the French throne", "7e73f972"], "sebep.b": ["Henry V's victory at Agincourt and his seizure of Normandy; the Armagnac-Burgundian civil war", "e39c90bb"], "sonuc.b": ["Recognition of Henry V at Troyes as regent of France and heir to the throne", "b96fac90"], "bag": ["Significance: The moment in the Hundred Years' War when England came closest to the French crown. The treaty provided for uniting the two crowns in a single dynasty; but Henry V's early death in 1422, Joan of Arc's relief of Orléans (1429) and Burgundy's passage to the French side by the 1435 treaty of Arras brought this arrangement down.", "d6199637"], "surec": ["In the war that resumed after the deposition of Richard II, Henry V landed in Normandy when the truce of 1413 expired, won at Agincourt on 25 October 1415, and took Caen and part of Normandy. Meanwhile France was wearing itself out in the blood feud between the Armagnac and Burgundian parties. Between 1419 and 1425 the city of Troyes was the seat of the royal government, and the treaty was signed there.", "cb737df6"], "metin": ["The signing of the treaty was followed by the wedding of Henry V and Catherine in the church of Saint-Jean in Troyes. Henry V died at Vincennes in 1422 and left the throne to his son Henry VI; the regency in France passed to the Duke of Bedford. Charles VI died the same year. The dauphin, who proclaimed himself Charles VII, at first suffered one defeat after another (Cravant 1423, Verneuil 1424), but in 1429 Joan of Arc relieved Orléans and Charles was crowned at Reims. With the treaty of Arras in 1435 good relations were established between Charles VII and Duke Philip the Good of Burgundy; the 1911 Britannica describes this as the 'final blow' to the English. The war ended at Castillon in 1453.", "3dd85807"]},
"antlasma5-utrecht-1713":{"baslik": ["The Treaties of Utrecht (1713-1714): the end of the War of the Spanish Succession", "9f403874"], "sebep.b": ["The prolongation of the War of the Spanish Succession and the coming to power in England of the Tories, who favoured peace", "74b8769b"], "sonuc.b": ["The Anglo-French and then the Anglo-Spanish treaties at Utrecht", "3085e9d1"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the 1911 Britannica, no treaty is second to it in importance in English history: it became one of the most powerful factors in the expansion of England's colonial empire and commercial greatness. In Europe it established the principle that the French and Spanish thrones should remain separate; Spain's European possessions were divided up.", "09cb392f"], "surec": ["Driven into a corner by Marlborough's victories, Louis XIV offered peace in 1706 and 1709 and was refused; the Gertruydenberg talks of 1710 also came to nothing. In 1710-1711 the situation changed: the Tories, who favoured peace, came to power in England; in April 1711 Archduke Charles, the claimant to the Spanish throne, succeeded his brother Joseph I as ruler of Austria, and the allies did not want Spain to be united with Austria either; it had also become clear that it was impossible to drive Philip out of Spain. In October 1711 France and England signed the preliminary peace terms in London. The congress opened at Utrecht on 29 January 1712; England was represented by John Robinson, Bishop of Bristol, and Thomas Wentworth, Earl of Strafford. The Emperor sent a representative in February once he had received assurance that the preliminaries were not binding. After Philip renounced his rights to the French throne on 10 July 1712, the talks gathered pace.", "773903e5"], "metin": ["The war between France and the Empire continued for some time longer: Prince Eugene and Marshal Villars made peace at Rastatt on 7 March 1714, and the princes of the Empire at Baden on 7 September 1714; Baden is the last treaty of the Utrecht peace settlement, and France gained Landau. The Spanish-Dutch peace was signed on 26 June 1714. The 'barrier' fortresses that gave the Dutch protection against France were settled by the treaty of Antwerp in 1715. In England the Whigs sharply criticised the treaty; after the accession of George I, Bolingbroke, Oxford, Ormonde and Strafford were accused for having made this peace. Nor was it forgotten that the Catalans, who had supported Charles of Austria for the Spanish throne, were abandoned to the vengeance of Philip V despite England's promise of protection.", "08b22839"]},
"antlasma5-versay-1919":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Versailles (1919): the First World War peace with Germany", "8950094c"], "sebep.b": ["The First World War and Germany's laying down of arms on 11 November 1918", "1fb31a25"], "sonuc.b": ["Peace at Versailles between the Entente powers and Germany", "942c4b69"], "bag": ["Significance: The first of the treaties signed at the Paris Peace Conference. According to the TDV article 'almanya', political developments in interwar Germany were largely determined by the harsh provisions of this treaty; no German considered these provisions just, one of the main aims of the Nazi Party was the destruction of Versailles, and by 1938 Hitler had broken all the provisions that bound Germany.", "013a61e5"], "surec": ["Austria-Hungary laid down its arms on 3 November and Germany on 11 November 1918. The Peace Conference met in Paris on 18 January 1919. Treaties were signed with Germany at Versailles, with Austria at Saint-Germain (10 September), with Bulgaria at Neuilly (27 November), with Hungary at Trianon (4 June 1920) and with the Ottomans at Sèvres (10 August 1920). A republic had been proclaimed in Germany on 9 November 1918; the Weimar National Assembly ratified Versailles on 9 July 1919.", "b3043769"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'almanya', Germany lost all its colonies and some of its territory in Europe, was obliged to pay heavy war reparations, was drastically disarmed and was subjected to financial, commercial and economic restrictions. The parties of the right blamed the Social Democrats for accepting the harsh terms. Difficulties over reparation payments and the French occupation of the Ruhr (1923) intensified the reaction against the Versailles order. After coming to power (1933), Hitler rapidly rearmed the country, sent troops into the demilitarised Rhineland in 1936 and annexed Austria on 12 March 1938.", "b7e6e468"]},
"antlasma6-akabe-1906":{"baslik": ["The Rafah agreement (1906): the eight articles that closed the Aqaba question", "44cb0193"], "sebep.b": ["Britain's ultimatum of 3 May 1906 after Abdülhamid II had Aqaba, and Taba in Sinai, occupied", "91a9496e"], "sonuc.b": ["The fixing of a new frontier by an agreement of eight articles", "933a4c4b"], "bag": ["Significance: This line began as an administrative boundary drawn between the Ottoman state and Egypt and is the direct ancestor of today's Egyptian-Israeli frontier. The agreement settled the Aqaba question; but Ottoman-British rivalry in the region continued until the end of the First World War.", "9a5f215e"], "surec": ["As the Hejaz railway advanced southwards, the Sinai peninsula, and especially the fortress of Aqaba, grew in importance for the Ottomans. The British, for their part, wanted to bring the Arabian peninsula under control within the triangle of Aqaba, Kuwait and Sanaa, to secure the Suez Canal and to keep hold of the route to India; they planned to acquire, after Egypt, Aqaba too, which they had turned into a base of operations from 1882 onwards, by supporting the Egyptian garrison with their own forces. Abdülhamid II sent one of his aides-de-camp, Rüşdü Bey, to Aqaba with a military division and had it occupied; after entering Aqaba, Rüşdü Bey also took Taba in Sinai. Thereupon Britain, which was preparing a wider military operation and also had Europe's support, issued an ultimatum on 3 May 1906 demanding that the Sinai peninsula be evacuated within ten days. Abdülhamid II, who regarded Egypt as his own possession and rejected the British occupation, ensured through a resolute policy that the commission to determine the new frontier would be composed of Turks and Egyptians.", "4e4798a7"], "metin": ["The agreement was signed on 1 October 1906. The definition of the frontier as a 'straight line to be fixed by posts' was to lead, in the following century, to disputes over this line through the positions of the individual posts. The agreement settled the Aqaba question, but Ottoman-British rivalry in the region continued until the end of the First World War.", "f590454a"]},
"antlasma6-bukres-1913":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Bucharest (1913): the end of the war among the former allies", "269dc94b"], "sebep.b": ["The ending of the First Balkan War by the Treaty of London and the victors' quarrel over the spoils", "a5970a6f"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Bucharest between Bulgaria on one side and Serbia, Greece and Montenegro on the other", "e457c222"], "bag": ["Significance: The Ottoman state is not a party to this treaty — and that is precisely where its importance lies. When the victors of the First Balkan War fought among themselves, the Ottoman side gained the opportunity to recover Edirne, which it had lost; the reckoning of the Second Balkan War was settled among the victors at Bucharest, while separate treaties with the Ottoman state were signed in the following months.", "b501b612"], "surec": ["At the end of the First Balkan War the Treaty of London had been signed on 30 May 1913; by this arrangement Edirne, Thrace and Dedeağaç were left to Bulgaria; Salonica, southern Macedonia and Crete to Greece; northern and central Macedonia to Serbia; and Silistra to Romania. When the quarrel over the spoils set the victors against one another, Bulgaria found itself at war with four states at once. While the allies were advancing towards Sofia, the government of the Committee of Union and Progress took advantage of the opportunity and acted, and on 21 July Edirne, which Bulgaria had left without forces, was retaken from the Bulgarians without any resistance.", "243c0c7b"], "metin": ["The Ottoman state was not a party at Bucharest; it made its own peaces separately. The Ottoman-Bulgarian treaty was signed in Istanbul on 29 September 1913 and the Ottoman-Greek treaty in Athens on 14 November 1913. Under the twenty-article Treaty of Istanbul, Edirne remained with the Ottoman state. On 16 December 1913 the great powers communicated their decisions leaving Imbros (Gökçeada), Tenedos (Bozcaada) and Kastellorizo (Meis) to Turkey and giving the other islands into other hands. Bulgaria's cession of Western Thrace and the Aegean coast to Greece came about not through this treaty but six years later, through the Treaty of Neuilly (27 November 1919), which ended the First World War.", "c7674179"]},
"antlasma6-kibris-1489":{"baslik": ["The cession of Cyprus to Venice (1489): the end of the last Crusader state in the East", "26becf50"], "sebep.b": ["The appearance of an Ottoman fleet off Famagusta in 1488 and Venice's concern for the defence of the island", "e215af1c"], "sonuc.b": ["Queen Caterina Cornaro's abdication and the passage of the island's government to Venice", "fe963ad5"], "bag": ["Significance: The last Crusader state in the Eastern Mediterranean thus disappeared, and Cyprus ceased to be a dynastic kingdom and became a direct colony of a maritime state. The island was now the forward outpost of the Venetian navy in the Eastern Mediterranean; it came to lie at the centre of the naval rivalry between the Ottoman state and Venice, and this Venetian administration was the target of the Cyprus campaign of 1570-71.", "9bd33aa7"], "surec": ["From the fourteenth century the Kingdom of Cyprus had been wasting away under Genoese and Venetian economic influence: in 1374 the Genoese, by agreement with the King of Cyprus, secured economic dominance on the island for ninety years, and the strife between the two Italian republics ruined the island's economy. King James, who allied with the Venetians against the Genoese, supporters of the queen's side, married the Venetian Caterina Cornaro in 1472; Venice had thus made its way into the dynasty. The appearance of an Ottoman fleet off Famagusta (Magosa) in 1488 made Venice anxious about the defence of the island.", "48af028b"], "metin": ["In the legal order of the time Cyprus was a kingdom paying tribute to the Mamluk sultanate; Venice informed Cairo that it had taken over this obligation, and its envoy brought the sultan, together with gifts, 16,000 ducats as two years' tribute. In other words, the transfer did not break the island's legal tie — it only changed the hand that held that tie. When the Mamluk state was incorporated into the Ottoman state in 1517, this claim to tribute would also change hands and become one of the legal grounds of the Ottoman claim to Cyprus.", "efa697ad"]},
"antlasma6-neuilly-1919":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Neuilly (1919): the closing of Bulgaria's outlet to the Aegean", "6d3a4b46"], "sebep.b": ["Bulgaria's entry into the First World War on the side of the Central Powers and its withdrawal from the war in defeat by signing an armistice", "f5b28ac7"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of the Treaty of Neuilly, which ended the war", "4993bf93"], "bag": ["Significance: It is the treaty that closed Bulgaria's outlet to the Aegean Sea. The Western Thrace and Dedeağaç belt, taken from the Ottoman state in the Balkan Wars, passed to Greece by this treaty; the question of Bulgaria's 'outlet to the sea' remained one of the lasting themes of Balkan politics until the Second World War.", "985f70a9"], "surec": ["After declaring independence on 5 October 1908, Bulgaria, under the rule of Prince Ferdinand, who became king with the title of 'tsar', had played an important part in forming a Balkan alliance against the Ottoman state, and after the Balkan Wars it had entered the First World War on the side of the Central Powers. When the front collapsed, Bulgaria withdrew from the war by signing an armistice with the Entente Powers; because this meant that communications between Turkey and Germany were cut, it forced the Ottoman government too to seek an armistice, and the Armistice of Mudros was concluded on 30 October 1918.", "4cffdfbe"], "metin": ["The treaty has a sequel as regards the Muslim minority in Bulgaria: religious life and pious foundations in the country were laid down in three documents — the Istanbul protocol of 1909, the protocol of 1913 that amended it and introduced innovations, and the Regulation on the Administration and Organisation of the Religious Institutions of the Muslims of Bulgaria, adopted in 1919. In other words, the year in which the war was closed was the year in which, along with the frontiers, the law governing the community was also rewritten.", "846efd4c"]},
"antlasma6-rusya-1739":{"baslik": ["Peace with Russia (1739): Azov stays with the Russians but is not to be fortified", "d6674426"], "sebep.b": ["The war of 1736-1739, which began with the Russian attack on the fortress of Azov and march on the Crimea", "acdc35be"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of peace with Russia (11 Ramadan 1152)", "d929c350"], "bag": ["Significance: This peace of the reign of Mahmud I was the last major treaty with which the Ottoman state concluded a victorious war in the eighteenth century. Azov remained with Russia but could not be fortified; the Kabarda region was considered neutral. In other words, Russia failed to reach its goal of opening a way to the Black Sea and the Caucasus — this goal was put off for thirty-five years, until Küçük Kaynarca.", "e0929e4d"], "surec": ["The pretext for the war was a question of passage: Mahmud I had ordered the Crimean forces to cross the Kabarda region in the Caucasus in order to support the Ottoman forces on the Iranian frontier; the Russians protested, claiming that the region belonged to them, and although the order was withdrawn, they used it as a pretext to attack the fortress of Azov in March 1736 and marched on Or Kapı (Perekop). On 13 July 1736 the Russians captured the fortress of Azov, Gözleve, Or Kapı and Kinburn, and wrought destruction in Bakhchisaray and Akmescid. In 1738 the Russians who tried to cross the Dniester were repulsed, and the Russian fleet that sailed from Azov into the Black Sea was burned by the Ottoman fleet under the Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Süleyman Pasha. The objective of the army that set out from Istanbul in April 1739 under the command of İvaz Mehmed Pasha was Belgrade; in the battles between Belgrade and Hisarcık the Austrians were defeated and the city was recovered. The agreement made with Austria for twenty-seven years on 28 September 1739 ended the war, and the Austrians withdrew north of the Danube. Although Russia had taken Khotyn, it was forced to make peace, owing also in part to its ally's withdrawal from the war.", "1ea21083"], "metin": ["The date of the peace is 11 Ramadan 1152 / 12 December 1739. The agreement with Austria at Belgrade had been signed three months earlier, on 28 September 1739; together the two peaces closed the war of 1736-1739. By this peace the city of Belgrade was handed over to the Ottomans, and for the rest of the eighteenth century it remained the most advanced point of the Ottoman frontier.", "e4209722"]},
"antlasma6-saintgermain-1919":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Saint-Germain (1919): from empire to small republic", "d729085c"], "sebep.b": ["The ending of the First World War with the defeat of Austria-Hungary", "f4891e66"], "sonuc.b": ["Austria's signature of the Peace Treaty of Saint-Germain with the Allies", "a0f81c8d"], "bag": ["Significance: It is the legal liquidation of the Habsburg order, which for centuries had been the Ottoman state's closest rival on land. At the end of the same war the Ottoman state had signed the Armistice of Mudros (30 October 1918); the Ottoman part of the peace order imposed on the defeated states, together with Saint-Germain, Neuilly and Trianon, was to be Sèvres, and that order would not be accepted in Turkey.", "1a18b40a"], "surec": ["The elections held after the war, on 16 February 1919, produced a constituent assembly; the new constitution prepared by this assembly came into force on 1 October 1920. Austria signed the peace treaty with the Allies in the course of this process.", "1e97b058"], "metin": ["Efforts were made to relieve the burden that the reparations debt placed on the country's economy by means of economic aid obtained from abroad through the League of Nations; but the end of this aid in 1926 and the economic crisis of 1929 brought the country to the brink of bankruptcy. The fact that the treaty had barred rapprochement with Germany led Austria to develop relations with Italy, and in 1930 a treaty of friendship was signed between the two states.", "87386887"]},
"antlasma6-sudan-1899":{"baslik": ["The Sudan Condominium (1899): joint on paper, British rule in practice", "25416559"], "sebep.b": ["The destruction of the state founded by the Mahdist movement by an Anglo-Egyptian campaign", "683efb59"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of the agreement that formed the framework of government in the Sudan", "dc7d8ba3"], "bag": ["Significance: Egypt had been attached to the Ottoman state since 1517, and in the nineteenth century the Sudan was governed through the governorship of Egypt; the arrangement of 1899 set this tie aside in practice without breaking it in law. The name 'condominium' was chosen to take account of Egyptian influence in the region; but until 1920 the joint administration remained, in the broadest sense, in British hands, while the expenses were financed by Egypt.", "9efb3fbc"], "surec": ["The state founded by Muhammad Ahmad al-Mahdi managed to survive until 1899 despite major military campaigns by Anglo-Egyptian forces. Egyptian troops had already withdrawn from part of the region in 1883 because of these revolts. Lord Cromer, Britain's highest authority in Cairo, taking account of Egyptian influence in the Sudan, inaugurated on 19 January 1899 a new administration called a 'condominium' (the joint sovereignty of two states).", "795be46c"], "metin": ["The man who really established British rule in the Sudan was Francis Reginald Wingate; he stayed there from 1899 to 1916 and founded British colonialism. In the period of joint sovereignty railways were built between Wadi Halfa and Khartoum and between the Gezira and El Obeid, and a port was constructed on the Red Sea coast. The First World War brought some changes to the joint administration; by a new agreement made in 1945 Egypt's rights over the Sudan were recognised again.", "95c3319a"]},
"antlasma6-trianon-1920":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Trianon (1920): Hungary's loss of territory and people", "46e3e679"], "sebep.b": ["The defeat of Austria-Hungary in the First World War and the internal turmoil after the war", "7599fae2"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of the Treaty of Trianon", "237b54d4"], "bag": ["Significance: It is the treaty that drew the modern frontiers of the Hungarian land on which the Ottoman state had established provinces (Buda, Temesvár) in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. By this treaty Hungary suffered a loss of territory and people; between 1920 and 1938 efforts continued on the one hand to secure stability and on the other to have the Treaty of Trianon revised.", "93ef74bd"], "surec": ["After the war the country was dragged into internal conflict. The forces that gathered around Miklós Horthy, and in some places took revenge on the communists, were able to enter Budapest on 16 November 1919 with the approval of the Entente powers. On 1 March 1920 Horthy was made regent of the kingdom; the treaty was signed three months later.", "4b4cf73a"], "metin": ["The Ottoman period had left a deep mark on the history of Hungary: with the capture of Temesvár in 1552 a second Ottoman province was established on Hungarian soil, and the application of a foreign system on two fifths of the lands had disrupted the earlier natural course of development. Trianon is the document that reduced the multi-ethnic kingdom left by that long history to its present dimensions. Between 1920 and 1938 Hungary's foreign policy was shaped largely around the demand for revision of the treaty.", "5f4e137c"]},
"antlasma6-viyana-1815":{"baslik": ["The Final Act of the Congress of Vienna (1815): the map of post-Napoleonic Europe", "a7a7d507"], "sebep.b": ["The collapse of Napoleon's rule and the meeting in Vienna of the congress that was to reorder Europe", "5436d5b8"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of the Final Act, which brought all the separate treaties together", "eca98e10"], "bag": ["Significance: With the Congress of Vienna a period of conservative 'restoration' began in Europe, and Vienna became the political centre of this period. The order established by the congress lasted until the popular uprisings of 1848, which spread to almost every part of Europe. The Ottoman state was not a party to the congress; but the balance of the great powers that the congress established (the 'Concert of Europe') became the framework within which every crisis over Ottoman lands was resolved throughout the nineteenth century.", "bf223be8"], "surec": ["Owing to the visit of the allied sovereigns to England and other delays, the congress was postponed to the autumn of 1814; the representatives arrived then. The principal participants were Austria, Russia, Prussia, Great Britain and France, but all the states of Europe sent representatives. The thorniest subject of the talks was the question of Poland and Saxony.", "ae6b01f7"], "metin": ["The Final Act, gathering all the separately concluded treaties into a single document, was signed on 9 June 1815, a few days before the Battle of Waterloo. The order established by the congress lasted in Europe until 1848; the uprisings that broke out in March, May and especially October of that year ended the repressive restoration period that had begun with the Congress of Vienna, and Vienna was affected by these developments in the first degree.", "520cee68"]},
"antlasma6-yas-tasdik-1792":{"baslik": ["The exchange of the Jassy ratifications (1792): the peace becomes legally final", "76789e85"], "sebep.b": ["The talks conducted over fifteen sessions at Jassy resulting in a peace treaty (10 January 1792)", "db2e737f"], "sonuc.b": ["The ceremony for the exchange of ratifications held at the seventeenth meeting", "235c78f0"], "bag": ["Significance: The signature of a treaty and its entry into force are not the same thing — this entry is about precisely that difference. The treaty was signed on 15 Jumada al-Awwal 1206 (10 January 1792) and the grand vizier approved it on 23 Jumada al-Awwal (18 January), but since the sovereigns' ratifications were to be exchanged within five weeks, the delegates stayed where they were, and the peace became final only on 16 Jumada al-Akhir 1206 (10 February 1792). This is the closing date of the war of 1787-1792.", "cb8cfd1e"], "surec": ["By instructions arriving from Istanbul on 19 August 1791, the Ottoman delegation at Sistova had also been assigned to the talks at Jassy; after exchanging the ratifications of the peace made with Austria on 31 August 1791, it set out and joined the army at Silistra on 7 September. The Jassy talks began on 10 November 1791 and lasted fifteen sessions. The real crux was the acceptance of the river Dniester (Turla) as the frontier: Russia would not give this up, and when it also became clear that Prussia would not be able to fulfil the terms of its alliance, the grand vizier was given permission to make peace. Since the preliminary peace document (mukaddime-i sulhiyye) had accepted a new frontier by which the left bank of the Dniester was to belong to Russia and the right bank to the Ottoman state, no room was given at Jassy to requests for changes on this point.", "70f97256"], "metin": ["The exchange ceremony was performed at the seventeenth meeting, held on 16 Jumada al-Akhir 1206 (10 February 1792); the peace thus became final in international law as well. The outcome was grave for the Ottoman state: the frontier was drawn along the Dniester, territory was lost and the dream of recovering the Crimea came to an end. Selim III, who said that drawing the frontier along the Dniester was 'the same as accepting the Danube as the frontier', had been left with no choice but to decide for peace, despite his expectation of one last victory.", "a3a9fc9e"]},
"antlasma6-yergogu-1790":{"baslik": ["Armistice of Giurgiu (Yergöğü) (1790): the closing of the Austrian front", "1f354fd6"], "sebep.b": ["Prussia's march towards Silesia and the Reichenbach Convention, which forced Austria into peace", "d53839f5"], "sonuc.b": ["The signing of a nine-month armistice in the grand vizier's tent at Giurgiu", "2dc5c21b"], "bag": ["Significance: With Austria's entry in 1788 into the war declared on Russia in 1787, the Ottoman state was fighting on two fronts at once. The armistice of Giurgiu closed one of these fronts and cleared the way for the army to turn against Russia. It is also important from a procedural point of view: when compared with the armistice later made with the Russians at Galați, Giurgiu is an agreement that opened peace talks without any precondition. The peace of Sistova (1791) came by the road that this instrument opened.", "ae0c2154"], "surec": ["Emperor Joseph II wanted peace because of financial difficulties and internal revolts; his brother Leopold II was of the same view. In order to show the Sublime Porte that he was doing his part as an ally and to force Austria to accept the plan for an exchange of territories, the King of Prussia moved with his army towards Silesia in early June; on 18 June he set up headquarters in the village of Schönwalde near Reichenbach on the Bohemian frontier. Austria was forced to give way, and the Reichenbach Convention was signed on 27 July 1790. Selim III at first stipulated that a route be opened through Wallachia, which Austria held under occupation, so that the army marching against Russia could pass; the Prussian representative, Colonel Spiridion von Lusi, stated that this was not part of the Reichenbach understanding and would be possible only after the peace. On 6 September 1790 the consultative council that met in Istanbul decided to drop the preconditions and conclude an armistice speedily. The draft drawn up by Lusi was translated, partly corrected and sent to Prince Coburg; after corrections by both sides the text was signed.", "80d8192d"], "metin": ["The text was signed in the grand vizier's tent at Giurgiu on Sunday, 9 Muharram 1205 (18 September 1790), through the mediation of the Prussian representative Lusi, by Baron Ignaz von Stürmer, interpreter of the imperial court, on behalf of Prince Coburg, and by Grand Vizier Şerif Hasan Pasha; it was exchanged and carried by Stürmer to Prince Coburg. Peace talks began at Sistova on 23 December 1790 and lasted seven months; eighteen general sessions were held in all. The crux of the dispute was what the expression 'status quo' meant: the Ottoman delegates insisted that it meant a return to the situation before the war (status quo ante bellum), that is, the restitution of the captured fortresses, while Austria wanted the principle of the 'status quo strict'. The Treaty of Sistova was signed on 4 August 1791.", "0876d726"]},
"antlasma7-bahcesaray-1681":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Bakhchisaray (1681) — peace with Russia after the Chyhyryn campaigns", "8b9ae6c5"], "metin": ["According to TDV, after the Chyhyryn campaigns (1677-1678) the peace talks continued through the mediation of the Crimean Khanate and dragged on for two years, because Russia had not yet attained the privilege of dealing with Istanbul directly; when preparations for a new campaign in Ukraine began in 1680, the first official peace between the two states was concluded at Bakhchisaray as a twenty-year truce (TDV article on Mehmed IV: 13 February 1681; according to the TDV article on Kara Mustafa Pasha, Mehmed IV approved the terms fixed at Bakhchisaray on 11 February 1681). Russia had proposed, through the Crimean khan, that the river Dnieper (Özi) be accepted as the frontier. According to the TDV article 'Ukrayna', the treaty was approved by the Ottoman government, and Left-Bank Ukraine, including Kiev, was left to Moscow. Significance: it is the first official peace between the two states; the TDV article 'Kırım' says that until 1681 the khanate claimed the role of intermediary between the Ottomans and Russia. According to the TDV article 'Rusya', Russia joined the alliance formed against the Ottomans in the subsequent Great Turkish War (1683-1699) and took Azov on 6 August 1696. TDV does not give the provisions article by article.", "cebcd6a1"]},
"antlasma7-edirne-1568":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne) (1568) — the peace that ended the war with the Habsburgs", "ced8f6b7"], "metin": ["According to TDV, Selim II received the Habsburg ambassador while he was wintering in Edirne, and on 17 February 1568 the treaty ending the state of war with the Habsburgs was signed. The TDV article 'Macaristan' adds the following: when the last campaign of Suleiman the Lawgiver ended with the capture of Szigetvár, the Ottoman government realised that it would not be able to control the whole of Hungary, and the Treaty of Adrianople opened a period of relative peace lasting twenty-three years. The picture that the treaty froze was a Hungary divided into three: the part held by the Habsburgs (an estimated 120,000 km²), the part under Ottoman rule (about 120,000 km²) and the semi-independent Principality of Transylvania. The TDV articles used do not give article-by-article provisions on the duration of the peace, the amount of tribute or the frontier fortresses.", "13ba8b11"]},
"antlasma7-erzurum-1823":{"baslik": ["The First Treaty of Erzurum (1823) — peace with Iran", "7e7e5461"], "metin": ["According to TDV, in the autumn of 1821 the Crown Prince Abbas Mirza crossed the frontier with a large military force, took Kars and Bayazıt and besieged Erzurum; at the same time the Iranians attacked northern Iraq and the province of Baghdad, occupied Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad and besieged Kirkuk. Ottoman resistance and the cholera/epidemic that engulfed Azerbaijan forced the Iranians to withdraw. The war ended with the Treaty of Erzurum, signed on 28 July 1823: the Iranian forces were to withdraw within sixty days from the lands they had occupied, and the places taken were given back. According to the TDV article 'İran', the frontier clashes of 1833-1842 resulted in a new Ottoman-Iranian treaty at Erzurum in 1847. The TDV article on Erzurum adds that in this war there were not enough soldiers to defend the city and that the Janissaries had forgotten soldiering and were occupied with farming.", "87571475"]},
"antlasma7-galata-ahidname-1453":{"baslik": ["The Galata Ahidname (1453) — the guarantee given to the Genoese", "05960099"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Galata', immediately after the conquest of Istanbul, towards the end of Jumada al-Awwal 857 (early June 1453), the Conqueror granted an ahidname to the Genoese of Galata. Citing Halil İnalcık, TDV regards this document as being in the nature of a privilege and capitulation: it is the legal framework that determined what rights the Genoese would have and under which their colony would continue under Ottoman sovereignty. Its significance is that it was the basis for leaving within the Ottoman capital a Latin settlement that did not disappear with the conquest; the same article mentions that in the jizya register of 1455 the Latin community of Galata is recorded together with its churches. The TDV article on Galata does not give the provisions of the document article by article.", "08dddb73"]},
"antlasma7-hollanda-ahidname-1680":{"baslik": ["The Dutch Ahidname (1680) — renewal of trading privileges", "1df44273"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Hollanda', after Haga the Dutch Republic had sent no one to the Ottomans with the rank of ambassador; Colyer was given the rank of ambassador (1680), and the instructions given to him called above all for the renewal and improvement of the ahidname of 1612. At that time the Ottomans were weary in the face of Austria and France; opposition to France was the common ground for political cooperation between the two states, and the Venetian-Ottoman wars were also harming Dutch trade in İzmir. For this reason Mehmed IV granted the Dutch a new ahidname (1680; according to the TDV article on Kara Mustafa Pasha, a commercial treaty was made with the Netherlands on 15 September 1680). Provision: in content the ahidname was the same as that of 1612, and it shaped the Dutch presence in the Mediterranean until Lausanne in 1923, when the regime of capitulations came to an end; apart from this, two regulations were drawn up for the residence and trade of Dutch subjects.", "7fd62b3c"]},
"antlasma7-iskender-bey-1460-1463":{"baslik": ["Agreements with Skanderbeg (1460 and 1463)", "96fc2e6f"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İskender Bey', when King Alfonso V of Naples, patron of the Albanian lord Skanderbeg, died and Naples fell into turmoil, he too, like the other rebel Albanian lords, submitted to the Ottoman sultan; in 1460 he signed a peace agreement, undertaking to give the palace a fixed tax every year (in sheep) and to take part in the sultan's campaigns. Because he did not comply with the terms of the agreement, new units of akinji raiders were sent to Albania; when the sultan arrived the following spring with a large army, Skanderbeg made another agreement on 27 April 1463 (citing Âşıkpaşazâde). Significance: these are agreements of vassalage made with a local lord, binding him to obligations of tax and campaign service; when the Ottoman-Venetian war began, Skanderbeg came to an agreement with Venice on 20 August 1463. TDV does not give the remaining articles of the 1460 and 1463 agreements.", "1c3dea88"]},
"antlasma7-istanbul-1784":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Istanbul (1784) — recognition of the annexation of the Crimea", "4e6573ca"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Kırım', Russia made the Crimea a province on 8 April 1783; the Ottoman state, which was then in no position to open a new war, recognised the annexation of the Crimea, Taman and the Kuban by Russia in a treaty it signed in Istanbul on 8 January 1784; the river Kuban was taken as the frontier between the two sides. Significance: it is the Ottoman state's legal abandonment of the Crimean Khanate (1441-1783), which had been made independent by Küçük Kaynarca in 1774. TDV writes that no one could stomach the abandonment of a Muslim country such as the Crimea to the rule of the tsars, and that towards 1787 a new war began between the Ottomans and Russia. The other articles of the treaty are not given in the TDV article 'Kırım'.", "2e9eb63e"]},
"antlasma7-isvec-ahidname-1737":{"baslik": ["The Swedish Ahidname (1737) — trading privileges", "bd275bb6"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İsveç', by the first ahidname, dated 10 January 1737, Sweden obtained the right to trade in Ottoman lands and seas; Sweden renewed and updated this treaty in later years (the treaty of commerce and friendship of 1783). In this way Swedish trade in time established a consular network in Egypt (Rosetta, Alexandria) and in places and ports such as Crete (Candia), İzmir, Aleppo, Patras, Rhodes, Antioch, Cyprus, Salonica, Euboea and Sidon. In the same article TDV writes that a treaty of alliance was also concluded on 4 January 1740. TDV does not give the provisions of the ahidname article by article.", "4b9a0b0f"]},
"antlasma7-isvec-ittifak-1740":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman-Swedish Alliance (1740)", "556172bc"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İsveç', the talks held with Fredrik von Höpken and Karlson, who conducted relations, resulted in a commercial treaty on 10 January 1737 and a treaty of alliance on 4 January 1740. This alliance provided for financial aid to Sweden; but the debts of Charles XII had not been waived. The question of the debt was resolved after lengthy talks and numerous bribes: the claim was reduced to 19,000 muskets, and the bond signed by Charles XII was returned; when Colonel Sinclair, who was carrying the bond, was killed in Silesia on 17 June 1739, the bond fell into the hands of the Russians and was lost, then turned up in Hamburg and was brought to Sweden in April 1740. In the same year a Swedish ship came to Istanbul with 6000 muskets, and the remaining 13,000 muskets were waived in return for bribes. Significance: TDV states that the Russo-Swedish war of 1741-1743 went on without any contribution from this alliance and that Finland was lost (Peace of Åbo); that is, the war went on without the alliance contributing to it.", "15bf6223"]},
"antlasma7-isvec-ittifak-1789":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman-Swedish Alliance (1789) — against Russia", "cfdd0993"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İsveç', Russia's annexation of the Crimea in 1783 gave Gustav III the hope of fighting Russia jointly with the Ottoman state; the Ottoman-Russian (and Austrian) war that began in 1787 at times intersected with the Swedish-Russian war of 1788-1790 and developed alongside it. The struggle against the common enemy brought about a new treaty of alliance between the two states (11 July 1789). The treaty provided that the Ottoman state would give Sweden financial aid as a contribution to the war, and the parties were not to agree to a separate peace that would leave each other in the lurch. Outcome: according to TDV, the struggle against Russia did not achieve the desired success; by the Peace of Värälä (1790) Sweden preserved its independence against Russia.", "c3bd8c32"]},
"antlasma7-isvec-ticaret-1840-1862":{"baslik": ["The Swedish commercial treaties (1840 and 1862)", "f112b90d"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İsveç', in the nineteenth century relations between the two states continued through a series of mainly commercial treaties. By the commercial treaty of 31 January 1840 Sweden joined the ranks of the 'most favoured nations' and had the commercial rights it had acquired through earlier treaties confirmed; on 18 May 1851 a commercial tariff was agreed; on 5 March 1862 a new treaty of commerce and navigation was concluded. Among them is also Sweden's accession on 5 July 1842 to the London Straits Convention of 13 July 1841. Significance: as TDV describes them, these are texts that confirm and renew commercial rights; TDV does not give the provisions article by article.", "d0b6cd11"]},
"antlasma7-prusya-1761":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman-Prussian Treaty of Friendship and Commerce (1761)", "b111adda"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Prusya', the first official relations between Prussia and the Ottomans began with a letter in which Grand Vizier Nişancı Mehmed Pasha, before the Passarowitz talks of 1718, conveyed to Frederick William I the wish for friendship. In 1755 Frederick II sought an alliance through Rexin, whom he had sent secretly; while the war in Europe was going on, Mustafa III was also willing to make an alliance, but Grand Vizier Ragıb Pasha, knowing that Britain would not give a guarantee, blocked an alliance that would have meant entering the European war. In the end Rexin obtained a treaty of friendship and commerce (22 March 1761), with the door left open to a future alliance. Provisions: friendship and commerce between the two states; Rexin was officially recognised as the Prussian envoy, and the political relations that had until then been conducted in secret began to be conducted openly. The alliance was concluded only in 1790 (31 January), in the midst of the Ottoman-Russian and Ottoman-Austrian war.", "020bb8d8"]},
"antlasma7-saint-jean-1917":{"baslik": ["The Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne Agreement (1917) — the Anatolia promised to Italy", "894bbfc6"], "metin": ["According to TDV, on learning that Ottoman Asia was being secretly partitioned, Italy reminded its allies of the Treaty of London of 26 April 1915 and demanded the region stretching from Mersin to İzmir; the Italian demands clashed with French and Greek wishes, and the giving of İzmir to Italy clashed with Russia. After the February Revolution in Russia and the fall of the tsardom, the Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne Agreement was signed (17 April 1917) between Britain and France and an Italy that had stepped up its pressure. Provisions: the regions of Antalya, Konya, Aydın and İzmir, excluding Mersin, were to be given to Italy, and a wide sphere of influence north of İzmir was to be recognised; Britain and France would be able to establish a free port at İzmir, and Italy at Mersin, Alexandretta (İskenderun), Haifa and Acre. TDV counts this as the last in the chain of secret agreements among the Allies; the picture had already begun to change with the collapse of Tsarist Russia, and the real question was to what extent these partitions could be carried out.", "fa08e9fc"]},
"antlasma7-sykes-picot-1916":{"baslik": ["The Sykes-Picot Agreement (1916) — the plan for partitioning the Arab lands", "1c18fb23"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Sykes-Picot Antlaşması', the agreement was the product of talks that began in London in November 1915 between Mark Sykes (Britain) and François Georges Picot (France) (draft of 3 January 1916); Russia approved it on condition that it receive Trabzon, Erzurum, Bitlis, Muş, Siirt and the belt along the Turkish-Iranian frontier (23 May), and Sykes-Picot was signed on 16 May 1916 (confirmed by exchanges of letters on 9 and 16 May). Provisions: the coastal region of Syria together with Adana and Mersin to France; the provinces of Basra and Baghdad and the ports of Haifa and Acre to Britain; Alexandretta (İskenderun) a free port; Palestine an international zone. The Arabian lands were divided in two by the Acre-Kirkuk line, the northern part (including Mosul, as far as the Iranian frontier) being left to French influence and the southern part to British influence; the two states undertook to establish and protect Arab states in their own zones. Significance: according to TDV, this was an arrangement entirely different from what had been promised to Sharif Husayn; the Bolsheviks disclosed the secret treaties on 8 November 1917.", "47589feb"]},
"antlasma7-zuravno-1676":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Żurawno (1676) — peace with Poland", "00d63f93"], "metin": ["According to TDV, when the Polish Sejm found the 1672 Treaty of Buczacz too harsh and rejected it, the Ottoman-Polish wars resumed from 1673. After the fighting around the fortress of Żurawno (İzvança), the war ended with an agreement accepted by the King of Poland (TDV article on Mehmed IV: Sha'ban 1086 / November 1676). The TDV article on the Treaty of Buczacz writes that Buczacz was taken as the basis in the treaties made from time to time in 1676 and 1678; the article on Kamianets (Kamaniçe) says that the 1672 treaties of Buczacz and Żurawno and the agreements of 1676-1678 established Ottoman sovereignty over Podolia. In other words, Żurawno did not set up a new order; it confirmed the Podolian arrangement of Buczacz, which lasted until Karlowitz in 1699. TDV does not separately list the articles of the 1676 agreement itself.", "2b0ae38a"]},
"antlasma8-93-harbi-1877":{"baslik": ["The London Protocol and the outbreak of the War of '93 (1877)", "54a716e8"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Doksanüç Harbi', in 1877 the European states signed the London Protocol; the decisions of this protocol, which was not very different from the Istanbul Conference, were also rejected by the Sublime Porte. Thereupon Russia, claiming to defend the law of Europe, declared war on the Ottoman state on 24 April 1877. The war, known as the War of '93 because it fell in 1293 of the Rumi calendar, was waged, like earlier Ottoman-Russian wars, on two fronts, the Danube and Eastern Anatolia, and ended in the defeat of the Ottoman armies; the peace talks continued as the Russian headquarters moved to Yeşilköy (San Stefano), and the Treaty of San Stefano was signed on 3 March 1878. This entry is a declaration of war, not a treaty; the body of the TDV article Doksanüç Harbi does not mention that San Stefano was replaced by Berlin.", "3af912e0"]},
"antlasma8-bardo-1881":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Bardo (1881) — Tunisia enters the French protectorate", "d2ddc422"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Tunus', the French acted before the Italians: using a trumped-up quarrel on the Tunisian frontier as a pretext, they sent troops to Tunisia in March 1881, and by signing the Treaty of Bardo with the Bey of Tunis on 12 May 1881 they announced that they had taken the province under their protection. Tunisia formally came under French administration by the Convention of La Marsa of 8 June 1883. TDV also records that thereafter Tunisia appeared in the Ottoman yearbooks (salname) as a 'privileged province' (eyâlet-i mümtâze).", "1ec6c580"]},
"antlasma8-dagno-1448":{"baslik": ["Peace of Dagno (1448) — Skanderbeg and Venice", "388755b9"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İskender Bey', when the Albanian lord of Dagno was killed and the town was occupied by the Venetians, Skanderbeg fell out with Venice and besieged Dagno in 1447; in this attempt he received support from local Ottoman beys. Venice still regarded him as a vassal of the Ottomans. By the agreement he signed on 4 October 1448, Skanderbeg gave up his claims to Dagno. In the summer months of the same year Murad II came to Albania in person and took the castle of Kocacık. The agreement is between Skanderbeg and Venice; the Ottomans are not mentioned as a party.", "4758353d"]},
"antlasma8-ferhad-simon-1588":{"baslik": ["Ferhad Pasha and King Simon of Kartli (1588)", "c1119744"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Gürcistan', Shah Tahmasp, who wished to stir up disorder in the lands conquered in 1578-1584, released Simon, the former ruler of Kartli, whom he had held since 1569, and sent him to Kartli. At the end of the fighting an agreement was signed between the Serdar (commander-in-chief) Ferhad Pasha and Simon (1588): Simon, as ruler of Kartli, accepted Ottoman sovereignty as a vassal and was made to pay tribute. In 1589 Murad III approved Simon's administration of the other regions of Kartli, apart from the fortresses that remained under Ottoman administration. Simon later rebelled and seized the fortress of Gori.", "7267773d"]},
"antlasma8-gaeta-1451":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Gaeta (1451) — Skanderbeg's attachment to Naples", "e3468a24"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İskender Bey', King Alfonso V of Naples, who was trying to support the rebel lords in Albania and take them under his protection, gave his support to Skanderbeg. Under the agreement made on 26 March 1451, Skanderbeg became the king's vassal; in June the king's soldiers installed themselves in Krujë (Akçahisar), and Skanderbeg was to receive an allowance of 1500 ducats a year. This protection moved his former patron Venice and the Ottomans to act against him: both incited the influential families of Albania against Skanderbeg.", "f949a1d7"]},
"antlasma8-gence-1735":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Ganja (1735) — the lands Nader recovered", "3786f39b"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'İran', after taking Isfahan in 1730 and placing Tahmasp II on the throne, Nader recovered, through the wars he waged over five years, all the lands that had been lost to the Ottomans; although he could not capture Baghdad, which he besieged in 1733, he brought Ganja, Tiflis and Yerevan back into his territory. In the same way he also fought the Russians successfully and, by the Treaty of Ganja of 1735, recovered Derbent and Baku. These two chronology entries describe the same treaty from two sides (the return of Derbent and Baku; Russia's withdrawal from the Caspian shore).", "13de41bc"]},
"antlasma8-georgievsk-1783":{"baslik": ["Treaty of Georgievsk (1783) — King Erekle II of Kartli under Russian protection", "11abdcc2"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Gürcistan', when the region became the scene of a struggle for influence among the Ottoman state, Iran and Russia, King Erekle II of Kartli preferred Russian protection and signed an agreement with the Russians on condition that his political independence be recognised (1783). According to TDV, in the sequel, in 1795 the Shah of Iran, Agha Mohammad, led a campaign into Georgia and devastated Tiflis; the Russian Tsar Paul I abolished the kingdom of Kartli and Kakheti in 1800 and on 12 September 1801 proclaimed it a province of Russia.", "c4c23c73"]},
"antlasma8-vlaicu-murad-1373":{"baslik": ["Voivode Vlaicu of Wallachia and Murad I (1373)", "0a19bb50"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Eflak', Vlaicu (1364-1377), son of Nicolae Alexandru, had received land from King Louis of Hungary in the form of a zeamet (fief) and accepted his suzerainty; he fought against the Bulgarian Tsar Shishman, who had accepted the suzerainty of the Ottoman sultan Murad I. During this struggle Vlaicu's forces also fought Ottoman units and won minor successes (1368); TDV counts this as the first encounter between the Ottomans and the Romanians. Vlaicu, who understood the importance of the Turkish advance in the Balkans, signed an agreement with Murad I in 1373. The article does not list the terms (tribute, degree of vassalage); all that is recorded is that the agreement was made.", "1166e3c8"]},
"antlasma8-yemen-barisin-bozulmasi-1626":{"baslik": ["The breakdown of peace in Yemen (1626) — Sanaa is besieged", "8ceef4df"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Yemen', in 1036 (1626) the peace with Imam al-Mu'ayyad broke down; the Zaydis captured the northern part of Sanaa. Imam al-Mu'ayyad and his brothers besieged Sanaa and banned the entry of provisions into the city; thereupon the beylerbeyi Haydar Pasha left Sanaa with his army. Ahmed Pasha, appointed in place of Haydar Pasha, was killed on the way, poisoned at Jeddah at the instigation of the Sharif of Mecca; although Aydın Pasha, beylerbeyi of Habesh, was appointed beylerbeyi of Yemen in 1628, he was unable to enter the besieged city. In 1629 Haydar Pasha was forced by an agreement to leave Sanaa to Imam al-Mu'ayyad. This entry is not a treaty but the collapse of the ten-year peace of 1619; TDV gives 1626 as the year in which the peace of 1619 broke down.", "53c3ad01"]},
"antlasma8-yemen-kansu-1630":{"baslik": ["Kansu Pasha and Imam al-Mu'ayyad in Yemen (1630)", "e44ec8b3"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Yemen', Kansu Pasha, sent to Yemen in 1629 as vizier and serasker (commander-in-chief), arrived with more than 20,000 soldiers. When an army of 7-8000 men that his kethüda (steward) sent to Taiz was surrounded in Ramadan 1039 (April 1630) by about 100,000 Zaydis and suffered heavy losses, Kansu Pasha withdrew to Mocha and had its walls fortified; then in Muharram 1040 (August 1630) he made an agreement with Imam al-Mu'ayyad. The article does not give the terms of the agreement. The agreement broke down in 1043 (1633): Zabid surrendered, Kansu Pasha came to terms with Hasan b. al-Qasim and left Yemen, and Mocha too was evacuated on 22 October 1635.", "b16c20ac"]},
"antlasma8-yemen-kasim-1608":{"baslik": ["Peace in Yemen (1608) — Cafer Pasha and Imam al-Qasim", "a668e432"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Yemen', in the time of Cafer Pasha (1607-1612), who was appointed to Yemen from the beylerbeylik of Habesh, the dispute between the Ottoman administration and the Zaydi imam al-Qasim b. Muhammad was resolved by a ten-year agreement (1608). The article does not list the terms; the only provisions recorded are the ten-year duration and the calming of the dispute by this means. This arrangement did not last: it was upset by al-Qasim's renewed revolt in Saada between 1612 and 1616 and the struggle that lasted two years. The article gives no transfer of territory or frontier line.", "39165b8a"]},
"antlasma8-yemen-mehmed-pasa-1619":{"baslik": ["Peace in Yemen (1619) — Mehmed Pasha and the Zaydis", "30762d9f"], "metin": ["According to the TDV article 'Yemen', when the beylerbeyi of Yemen, Mehmed Pasha (1616-1622), came to Sanaa, fighting had broken out again because the 1608 agreement had run its term. Peace was finally secured, and an agreement intended to last ten years was signed between Mehmed Pasha and the Zaydis (1028/1619). The only concrete provision given is this: Ottoman troops were to withdraw from the lands that Imam al-Qasim had seized. The peace did not complete its planned ten years: in 1626 Imam al-Mu'ayyad broke the peace.", "09d65337"]},
"antlasma9-abd-1830":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman-American Treaty of Commerce and Navigation (1830): the first official tie and the rejected secret article", "2d2b07da"], "metin": ["Provisions. The treaty, signed in Istanbul on 7 May 1830 (14 Dhu al-Qadah 1245), consisted of a preamble, nine articles, a concluding section and a separate secret article. Under the public articles, the United States gained most-favoured-nation status in trade; each state would be able to open consulates in the other's country; American merchant ships would enjoy easy use of Ottoman ports and pass freely through the Straits; the trial of offences committed by American citizens was regulated; American envoys and consuls would not take Ottoman subjects under their protection; and the two sides would give each other help in cases of shipwreck. The secret article, for its part, provided that the Ottomans could, whenever they wished, have ships built in American shipyards and purchase ready-built ships and timber.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. The United States Senate ratified the treaty by a large majority but rejected the secret article by 27 votes to 18. Even so, the way to obtaining ships was not closed: Henry Eckford sold the Ottomans the ship United States, which he had brought over in 1831, and American craftsmen working at the shipyard established at Aynalıkavak gave the navy ships of a new type.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. American attempts to obtain a treaty, ongoing since 1799, had come to nothing, while in the meantime the number of American trading houses in Izmir had grown. In view of the increasing trade and Washington's persistence, the Sublime Porte permitted negotiations; the head of the delegation, Charles Rhind, arrived in Istanbul on 8 February 1830. The loss of most of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino in 1827 formed the background to the request for ships in the secret article; which side proposed the article, however, is disputed.", "ff3bda45"]},
"antlasma9-bahreyn-ingiltere-1861":{"baslik": ["The Bahraini-British treaty (1861): a link in the chain of protection in the Gulf", "c80d3024"], "metin": ["Provisions. According to the TDV article 'Bahreyn' (Bahrain), in May 1861 an agreement was signed between Britain and Ali, brother of Sheikh Muhammad bin Khalifa, representing Bahrain. The essence of it recorded by TDV is the prohibition of the slave trade and of piracy in the region; the other articles are not listed.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to the Encyclopaedia Iranica, Britain entered into treaty relations with the Al Khalifa in 1820, strengthened this tie with the treaties of 1847, 1856 and 1861 and with the executive agreements of 1880 and 1892, and established an 'exclusive' control over Bahrain's foreign relations. The agreement of 1861 is the link in the middle of this chain. In the following decades Britain treated Iran's claim to sovereignty over the island as invalid and described the sheikh as an independent ruler under British protection.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. According to TDV, from this date onwards Ottoman and British relations over Bahrain, Dammam and al-Ahsa in the Gulf were in constant conflict, and the two sides sent each other notes alleging violations. In 1869 Iran too sent Britain a note asserting that Bahrain was its own territory, but this claim was not taken very seriously. Thus the island remained within the British system of protection despite the claims of its two great neighbours.", "ce43f458"]},
"antlasma9-hewett-adua-1884":{"baslik": ["The Hewett (Adwa) Treaty (1884): the return of Bogos to Abyssinia", "b478b322"], "metin": ["Provisions. The treaty signed at Adwa on 3 June 1884 by Admiral William Hewett on behalf of Britain, by Mason Bey, governor of Massawa, on behalf of the Egyptian Khedive Tewfik, and by the Abyssinian ruler Yohannes IV consists of seven articles. ① All goods going to and coming from Abyssinia by way of Massawa, including arms and ammunition, were to pass freely under British protection. ② From 1 September 1884 the Bogos country was to be returned to the Abyssinian ruler; once the Egyptian troops had left the garrisons of Kassala, Amedib and Sanhit, the Egyptian buildings in Bogos and the ammunition in them would also pass to him. ③ Yohannes was to facilitate the withdrawal of these garrisons to Massawa through Abyssinia. ④ The Khedive was to give assistance with the appointment of a metropolitan to the Abyssinian church. ⑤ Fugitive criminals were to be extradited on a reciprocal basis. ⑥ Yohannes was to submit any disputes with the Khedive to British arbitration.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to TDV, the treaty was concluded between Egypt, Ethiopia and Britain because of the Mahdist movement in the Sudan. Since the deposition of Khedive Ismail in 1879, control of Egypt and the Red Sea ports had in practice been in British hands.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. Abyssinian support was needed to rescue the Sudanese garrisons besieged by the Mahdist forces; the price was Bogos and free transit. However, on 5 February 1885 the Italians entered Massawa, which Egypt had evacuated; despite the treaty they did not allow arms to pass to Abyssinia, and they did not recognise Egyptian sovereignty in the region. The tension led to an Italian-Abyssinian clash in 1887 and, with the Treaty of Wichale (Uccialli) of 2 May 1889, to the passing of the coastal lands to Italy.", "5ba54ac3"]},
"antlasma9-istanbul-protokolu-1913":{"baslik": ["The Istanbul Protocol (1913): the delimitation of the Ottoman-Iranian frontier", "9ce4c113"], "metin": ["Provisions. The protocol signed in Istanbul on 17 November 1913 determined the course of the Ottoman-Iranian frontier and entrusted the marking of the line on the ground to a mixed commission in which Britain and Russia held powers of arbitration. According to the Encyclopaedia Iranica, this line was essentially the product of Russo-Ottoman talks held before the protocol, and, insofar as it took as its basis the situation of 1905, just before the Ottomans began advancing into Iran in the northern border region, it was considered favourable to Iran.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to TDV, the commissioners of four states had begun to determine the frontier in 1849, but the work had dragged on for more than sixty years. The mixed commission of 1914 provided for by the protocol drew the frontier in detail along almost its entire length, from the Persian Gulf to Mount Ararat; according to TDV, the survey was completed shortly before the Ottoman Empire entered the First World War. Only on the Qotur region in the north could no decision be reached.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. This line later became the basis of both the Turkish-Iranian and the Iraqi-Iranian frontiers, but it did not remain undisputed. Turkey argued that the protocol had been imposed at a moment when the Ottoman state was too weak to resist, while Iran objected to the provisions concerning the Zuhab and Shatt al-Arab sectors. With the frontier agreements they signed on 9 April 1929, Turkey and Iran in effect confirmed the line of 1914, but they left sectors such as Qotur and Bulakbaşı to be examined by a new mixed commission.", "704db965"]},
"antlasma9-kutahya-1833":{"baslik": ["The Kütahya settlement (1833): Syria and Adana go to Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala", "98cf7e13"], "metin": ["Provisions. Mustafa Reşid Bey, the Âmedci (head of the grand vizierate's chancery office), whom the Sublime Porte sent with new instructions, met İbrahim Pasha in Kütahya at the end of March 1833 and, going beyond his instructions, agreed to leave Mehmed Ali Pasha all the places that had fallen into his hands, that is, Adana in addition to Aleppo and Damascus. Mahmud II at first refused, then accepted on 3 May 1833: Adana was given to İbrahim Pasha as a muhassıllık (a district held as revenue collector), and the places ceded were announced to the parties in a ferman dated 6 May. The Egyptian forces then withdrew from Kütahya.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to TDV, what European historians call the 'Treaty of Kütahya' was not a treaty signed and exchanged by both sides, but a set of offices conferred through the ordinary procedure of appointment; these appointments, which were renewed every year and could be revoked if necessary, left Mahmud II a door open to treating the settlement as temporary. Even so, the sultan was forced to leave to Mehmed Ali's administration lands that had been acquired in the conquests of 1516-1517.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. After defeating the Ottoman army at Konya on 21 December 1832 and taking the grand vizier prisoner, İbrahim Pasha reached Kütahya on 2 February 1833. The Sublime Porte appealed to Russia for help; the arrival of a second Russian fleet, which landed troops at Beykoz, together with pressure from Britain and France, pushed the sultan towards a settlement. Russia obtained its reward for this help with the alliance of Hünkâr İskelesi on 8 July 1833. Mistrust between the parties persisted, and on 24 June 1839 the matter turned into war again at Nizip.", "777f801e"]},
"antlasma9-petersburg-1723":{"baslik": ["The Treaty of St Petersburg (1723): the Caspian coastal provinces go to Russia", "79388d71"], "metin": ["Provisions. According to the Encyclopaedia Iranica, the Safavid ruler Tahmasp II was forced to accept a treaty signed on 23 September 1723 (22 Dhu al-Hijjah 1135): Derbent and Baku and the provinces of Gilan, Mazandaran and Astarabad were ceded to Russia. The TDV article 'İran' (Iran) summarises the same treaty as a document signed by the Iranian envoy in St Petersburg in the autumn of that year, which annexed to Russia the provinces on the western and southern shores of the Caspian and in return promised Russian Tsardom military aid to Tahmasp, who claimed the title of shah.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. In the midst of the Safavid collapse, the treaty gave Russia's gains on the Caspian coast a legal basis. According to TDV, this development caused considerable anxiety at the Ottoman court and, through the mediation of French diplomats, opened the way to the ahidname (treaty instrument) signed with Russia in Istanbul in 1724, which formalised the partition of the Iranian and Caucasian lands occupied by the two states (the Iranian partition treaty).\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. Peter I entered the Safavid lands, which were collapsing under the Afghan invasion, by taking Derbent in 1722; he established himself in Rasht in November 1722 and took Baku in July 1723, while the Ottoman army seized Tiflis in the spring of 1723. According to Iranica, however, Russia was never able actually to occupy Mazandaran and Astarabad and could not advance beyond Lahijan. With the rise of Nader, these two provinces were returned to Iran by the Treaty of Rasht of 1732.", "9dc0eba3"]},
"antlasma9-sofya-1915":{"baslik": ["The Sofia Convention (1915): the strip along the Maritsa (Meriç) ceded to Bulgaria", "4122409b"], "metin": ["Provisions. According to the TDV article 'Meriç' (Maritsa), the Ottoman-Bulgarian agreement signed in Sofia on 6 September 1915 provided that a strip 2 km wide along the left bank of the Maritsa would be given to Bulgaria; Enez, at the mouth of the river, was to remain in Ottoman territory. Thus the frontier that the 1913 Treaty of Istanbul had based on the Maritsa was shifted towards the other side of the river.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to TDV, in the First World War Bulgaria, unlike the other Balkan states, sided with Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire. The convention represents the Ottoman cession to its ally of a small part of Thrace, which it had recovered after the Balkan Wars, at a time when two states that had fought each other two years earlier found themselves in the same camp.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. TDV stresses that in all the frontier agreements made after the loss of the Balkans, the name of the Maritsa invariably came up. So it was after the war as well: at the Mudanya talks (11 October 1922) the Greek withdrawal 'to the west of the Maritsa' was left vaguely worded; exactly where in the river the frontier would run was settled only at Lausanne (24 July 1923). Turkey secured acceptance of the thalweg line instead of the left bank, and Karaağaç, which lay west of the thalweg, was also ceded to Turkey by a protocol.", "09a0e6a7"]},
"antlasma9-yanya-1430":{"baslik": ["The surrender of Ioannina (1430): Sinan Pasha and Murad II's charter of privileges", "836ed67f"], "metin": ["Provisions. According to the TDV article 'Yanya' (Ioannina), Ioannina, the centre of Epirus, surrendered without a fight to Sinan Pasha, the beylerbeyi (governor-general) of Rumelia, in October 1430, as recorded by the Byzantine historian Sphrantzes. In return for the surrender, Murad II granted religious and fiscal privileges to the city under Sinan Pasha's administration; the text of this charter has been published in Greek translation. An Ottoman military detachment was left in the citadel. TDV does not list the privileges one by one.\n\nSIGNIFICANCE. According to TDV, the rights in the charter remained in force for roughly two centuries. The transition of the city, which had grown used over half a century to living side by side with the Turks, from Frankish to Ottoman rule took place harmoniously. Over time Ioannina became an important sanjak (district) centre where Muslims, Christians and Jews lived together, and after the Tanzimat reforms it became the centre of a province covering the whole of Epirus.\n\nCAUSE AND EFFECT. When Carlo I Tocco, who held Ioannina, died in 1429, he was succeeded by his nephew Carlo II; Carlo I's illegitimate sons contested this, and the eldest of them, Hercules, asked Murad II for help in the spring of 1430. That same year, after taking Salonica from the Venetians, the sultan sent Sinan Pasha to Ioannina. After the surrender, Carlo II remained in a small principality as an Ottoman vassal until his death in 1448, while from Tocco's sons who became Muslims came the Karlızade family, which produced Ottoman commanders.", "9d6aec9e"]},
"avusturya-1736-1739-savasi-sebep-ozi":{"baslik": ["The Ottoman-Russian-Austrian War of 1736–1739: why did it begin, and how did Ochakov (Özi, Özü) fall?", "57667626"], "bag": ["The chronology did not explain the cause of the war at all. This card joins three links: the Kabarda pretext (TDV), the Black Sea objective (TDV `rusya`) and the lifting of the burden in the East (Iranica, Ganja 1735). This joining is the card's own synthesis. Missing and contradictory chronology items have been gathered in a separate patch proposal.", "316da46f"], "metin": ["First, the background. The Ottomans had been fighting in Iran since 1723. According to the TDV article `mahmud-i--osmanli` (Abdülkadir Özcan), after the defeat at Arpaçay (Yerevan) in June 1735 the sultan ordered the Crimean khan to march against Iran by way of the Caucasus. The fact that the Tatar forces were to pass through Kabarda caused a breach with Russia. The Russians protested, saying that these lands belonged to them. Although the order was withdrawn, the protest was used as a pretext for war.\n\nTHE real cause. According to the same article, Russia, together with its ally Austria, had concluded the War of the Polish Succession in its own favour. It then built fortresses on the frontier of Ukraine and Podolia in violation of the treaties and massed forces near Azov. TDV `rusya` (Kemal Beydilli) stresses that gaining access to the Black Sea was Russia's foremost objective. The two empires had been allies since 1726 (G. Hambly, EBSCO Research Starters). In March 1735 Russia signed a defensive alliance with Nader at Ganja and returned most of the Iranian lands it had taken in the 1720s (Encyclopaedia Iranica, \"Nāder Shah\"). It was thus freed of its burden in the east. The settlement of 1736 between the Ottomans and Iran, according to Iranica, was not a real peace treaty but a de facto ceasefire (see `antlasma4-istanbul-iran-1736`).\n\nTHE outbreak of the war (1736). According to TDV, in Dhu al-Qadah 1148 (March 1736) the Russians attacked Azov and marched on Or Kapı (Perekop). On 20 Dhu al-Hijjah 1148 (2 May 1736) the Ottoman imperial council decided on war; the article also mentions the encouragement of the French ambassador Villeneuve. Hambly dates Empress Anna's declaration of war to 12 April 1736, but does not state in which calendar. Azov fell that same summer; the day given by the sources differs (see the patch file). Marshal Münnich passed the Perekop lines and entered the Crimea. According to the TDV articles `kirim` and `bahcesaray`, Bakhchisarai was burned: about 2,000 houses and the Khan's Palace were damaged, and Selim Giray's library was destroyed.\n\nTHE fall of Ochakov (1737). Ochakov (Özü in TDV) was the fortress that held the mouth of the Dnieper and the western approach to the Crimea. According to TDV, in 1736 Yahya Pasha, governor of Trabzon, had been appointed to command its garrison. Münnich's army marched on the fortress in the summer of 1737. According to Hambly, the Russian success came largely from the explosion of the Ottoman powder magazine. TDV `abdullah-pasa-muhsinzade` (Feridun Emecen) writes that Abdullah Pasha, the serasker (commander-in-chief) at Bender, sent 3,000 soldiers to the fortress, but that because no other help arrived, Ochakov fell. According to the same article, on account of this loss Grand Vizier Osman Halisa Efendi was executed and the seal was given to Abdullah Pasha on 5 August 1737.\nThe day of the fall differs in the sources. TDV `ozu` and `mahmud-i--osmanli` say 11 July 1737. The war chronicle of the Russian naval archive (RGAVMF) says 2 July; the calendar is not stated, but if it is Old Style (Julian), this corresponds to 13 July New Style. Hambly also says 13 July. The card does not take sides.\n\nAFTER Ochakov. According to RGAVMF, in October of the same year an Ottoman army and 12 galleys tried to retake the fortress and were repulsed. In 1738, however, the Russians blew up and evacuated Ochakov and Kinburn because of an outbreak of plague. It was the peace of 1739 that legally returned the fortress to the Ottomans (TDV `ozu`). That is, the actual evacuation of the fortress and its formal restitution are separate events.\n\nTHE Austrian front. According to TDV, Austria attacked in three columns in June 1737; Hambly dates the emperor's official declaration of war to 14 July 1737. Seckendorf took Niš in July 1737; the city was retaken without a fight in October of the same year (TDV `nis`). In August 1737 peace talks began at Nemirov but came to nothing. Afterwards the Ottoman forces won successive victories: Banja Luka in 1737, Ada Kaleh on 17 August 1738 (TDV) and Grocka (Hisarcık) in 1739. The war ended with the Treaty of Belgrade of 1739; for the provisions of the treaty, see the card `antlasma-belgrad-1739`. According to TDV, Azov remained with Russia but was not to be fortified, while Kabarda was considered neutral.\n\nCONCLUSION. The pretext for the war lay in the Caucasus, its objective in the Black Sea. Russia took Ochakov and Azov, but because of epidemic disease and supply difficulties could not hold Ochakov. Austria's defeat also turned the peace in the Ottomans' favour.", "792073ac"]},
"avusturya-osmanli-askeri-geri-kalis-1683-1718":{"baslik": ["Where did the Ottoman army fall behind Austria? (Vienna 1683 – Passarowitz 1718)", "f5498966"], "metin": ["For the general framework, see first the card `dunya-savas-stili-karsilastirma` (1596–1699, the transformation of the Ottoman army in relation to Europe). This card narrows its focus to the Austrian front and the years 1683–1718. The disaster of the river crossing at Zenta is described in the cards `savas-zenta-1697` and `tartisma-ordularin-nehir-gecisi-riski`.\n\nTHE balance sheet. According to the TDV article `yeniceri` (Janissaries), of the major wars between 1593 and 1697 only two victories resulted: Mezőkeresztes (Haçova) in 1596 and Saint Gotthard in 1664. Against these there are twelve defeats. The same article writes that even at Mezőkeresztes the Austrians surpassed the Ottomans in the use of cannon and arquebuses. The chain of defeats after 1683 is a continuation of this older trend.\n\n① Drill and discipline. TDV `yeniceri`: the weekly musket drill that had been compulsory in 1552 was abandoned at the beginning of the 17th century; drill came to be carried out only before major campaigns. Gábor Ágoston's comparative article of 2011 lists the things İbrahim Müteferrika praised in 1732 in the \"Christian armies\": regular drill, discipline, a high proportion of officers, merit and quality of command. In other words, the shortcoming was also apparent to Ottoman intellectuals.\n\n② The army on paper. In response to Habsburg firepower, the number of Janissaries was increased and sekban and levend musketeers (irregular and mercenary troops) were recruited. According to TDV, this lowered the quality of the Janissaries. According to Ágoston, the number on the registers did not reflect the army in the field. In 1697 only about 30% (21,000) of the Janissaries on the pay register took part in the campaign; this proportion fell to 25% in 1701 and 17% in 1710.\n\n③ The collapse of the timar-holding sipahi (fief cavalry). Again according to Ágoston, in the Hungarian campaign of 1697–98 provincial troops made up only 11.62% of the army. In 1715–16, 14,800 sipahis failed to come on campaign. The army became increasingly dependent on the households of the pashas and on local power-holders.\n\n④ Artillery and munitions. TDV `merzifonlu-kara-mustafa-pasa` writes that the large siege guns were not taken to the siege of Vienna in 1683 and that the Austrians' artillery strength was greater. TDV `sehid-ali-pasa` states that before Petrovaradin in 1716 the army lacked cannon and munitions. According to the TDV articles `top` (cannon) and `humbaraci` (bombardiers), the artillery and bombardier corps also weakened in these years:\n• the number of artillerymen at the centre fell from 5,869 in 1694–95 to 3,437 in 1704;\n• the bombardiers lost much of their importance after 1689.\n\n⑤ Officers and schools. According to Ágoston, the Ottomans did not establish military-technical schools similar to those in Russia until the 1770s; the first attempt was in 1735. An officer class trained in the Western manner did not come into being until the Tanzimat.\n\n⑥ Supply. According to the TDV article `karlofca`, during the Karlowitz negotiations of 1698–99 the English ambassador Paget, who acted as mediator, drew attention to the supply difficulties of the Ottoman army. Transporting provisions overland was hard; if the allies also gained mastery of the Danube, transport would come to a complete halt.\n\nTHE opposing view: was this a \"collapse\"? The sources do not accept a picture of one-way decline:\n• TDV `yeniceri` writes that the diagnosis of \"decline\" arises largely from comparison with the previous century. According to the same article, even though the Ottomans fell behind in pitched battle, their superiority in siege warfare lasted longer.\n• Ágoston regards the concept of \"decline\" as a paradigm and points out that newer studies give prominence to the concept of \"transformation\". According to him, the Ottoman war industry did not seriously decline until the middle to end of the 18th century.\n• Ágoston also writes that in the army of 1697–98 the ratio of infantry to cavalry was 57 to 43, and that this was comparable to the Habsburg and Russian armies. He therefore objects to the generalisation of a \"cavalry-heavy Ottoman army\".\n• Technology alone is not an explanation. TDV `tufek` (musket) writes that in 1683 the Ottomans were using muskets with a combined firing mechanism like the Europeans; TDV `top` records that in 1686, 104 guns were cast at Tophane (the cannon foundry).\n• Nor were the defeats permanent. According to the TDV articles `pasarofca-antlasmasi` and `avusturya`, part of the places lost at Karlowitz were recovered in 1711, and the Morea in 1715. Austria lost the war of 1737–39, and Belgrade returned to the Ottomans in 1739.\n\nCONCLUSION. The picture that the sources draw together is one of a systemic difference rather than a gap in any single weapon: drill, the reality of the numbers, provincial cavalry, the artillery corps, the training of officers and supply. In 1683–1718 this difference produced grave results in pitched battles, but it was not an irreversible collapse.", "9dbeef00"], "kisa": ["Between 1683 and 1718 the Ottoman army was defeated again and again in pitched battles: Vienna, Zenta, Petrovaradin, Belgrade. The sources do not point to a single weapon. The main problems were the abandonment of drill, an army that remained on paper, the collapsing timar cavalry, shortages of cannon and munitions and the lack of schools to train officers. The same sources also object to the word \"collapse\".", "6a1f40f1"], "bag": ["The six headings are a synthesis compiled from TDV articles and Ágoston's article; no single source gives this list on its own. The order of the headings is not an order of importance. There is also a disagreement among the sources: TDV `merzifonlu-kara-mustafa-pasa` says that the siege of 1683 was unplanned and that the siege guns were not taken, whereas TDV `viyana` writes that the siege was undertaken with preparation. The card reports both views and does not take sides.", "7709ffbd"]},
"avusturya-prens-eugen-hafiza":{"baslik": ["Prince Eugene (Eugene of Savoy) in Austrian memory: the myth of the \"noble knight\" and its traces in Vienna", "1a16ba41"], "metin": ["For the man himself, see first the card `dunya3-prens-eugen-kimdir`. This card describes only how he was remembered afterwards.\n\nA song (1717). The soldiers' song \"Prinz Eugen, der edle Ritter\" (\"Prince Eugene, the Noble Knight\") tells of the siege of Belgrade of 1717 and the victory there. The Neue Deutsche Biographie (NDB) says that the song originated at the time of the victory at Belgrade and was probably written by a soldier from the Bavarian contingent in the army. According to the lexicon of the German Folk Song Archive in Freiburg, its oldest known written record is in a manuscript songbook begun in 1719. Thus he became a folk hero while still alive. The NDB also writes that he gained great fame in Germany too during his lifetime.\n\nTRACES in Vienna. The buildings Eugene left in the city are today part of Austria's cultural heritage:\n• Belvedere. The summer palace built by the architect Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt. Construction of the Lower Belvedere began in 1712; the Upper Belvedere began in 1717 and was completed in 1723. On a ceiling of the Lower Belvedere there is a fresco dated 1716, \"The Triumph of Prince Eugene\". After Eugene's death, the palace passed from his niece to Maria Theresa (1752).\n• Winter palace. He died in 1736 in this palace on the Himmelpfortgasse; the palace was later sold to the state.\n• Library. His library of about 16,000 volumes of books and manuscripts was bought by Emperor Charles VI and added to the court library. Today most of it stands in the State Hall (Prunksaal) of the Austrian National Library.\n• Tomb. He is buried in the Chapel of the Cross (Kreuzkapelle) in St Stephen's Cathedral (Stephansdom).\n• Heldenplatz statue. The equestrian statue in Vienna's \"Heroes' Square\" was made by Anton Dominik Fernkorn; it was unveiled on 18 October 1865. In the statue the horse is supported only on its hind legs and tail. According to the website \"Die Welt der Habsburger\", which tells the history of the Habsburgs, the statue's message was clear: to show the dynasty as the defender of Christendom against the Muslim Turks. There are also streets and squares in the city bearing his name.\nAn interesting detail: according to the Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie (ADB), during his lifetime Eugene had most firmly opposed a proposal to erect a statue to him.\n\nHISTORICAL writing: how the hero was constructed. The three-volume biography by the Austrian historian Alfred von Arneth (1858, 2nd edition 1864) exalted Eugene as commander, statesman and patron of the arts. Arneth also wrote the ADB article on Eugene himself. The same biography is among the sources of the TDV article `zenta`. Max Braubach's five-volume biography (Vienna 1963–65) and his NDB article of 1959 are more balanced: they also enumerate the \"noble knight's\" faults, such as jealousy and unwillingness to forgive his opponents.\n\nTHE shadow of the myth. The Vienna city administration's history wiki stresses two points together: Eugene became one of the best-loved figures of patriotic Austrian literature, but this literature did not fully reflect his personality. The Nazis too appropriated him on the grounds of the \"conquest of the East\". According to the German Folk Song Archive, in the Nazi period a warship and an SS division were named after him; in 1941 a radio fanfare was made from the melody of the song. For this reason the song was found disturbing in the second half of the 20th century. The website \"Die Welt der Habsburger\" also writes that the period 1683–1718 was later constructed as an \"age of heroes\". According to the same site, despite his bloody deeds, Eugene acquired the image of a \"clean hero\" and was exalted as the \"saviour of the West\".\n\nBEYOND Austria. According to an article in War Room, a journal of the US Army War College, Napoleon, in listing the great commanders worth studying, placed Eugene alongside Alexander, Hannibal, Caesar, Gustavus Adolphus, Turenne and Frederick.\n\nTHE view from the Ottoman side (TDV). TDV has no article devoted to Eugene; his name appears in the articles on wars and treaties:\n• `zenta`: it writes that this victory was one that \"even Eugene did not expect\".\n• `varadin`: it notes that the Ottoman chronicles referred to the defeat of 1716 as the \"year of Varadin\" (Petrovaradin).\n• `pasarofca-antlasmasi`: it presents Eugene as a pro-war statesman who magnified the Ottoman threat.\nThat is, the same person is a saviour myth in Vienna, while in the chronicles of Istanbul he is the name attached to a year of defeat.", "df1e8b8f"], "kisa": ["In the Ottoman chronicles, Eugene's name mostly appears next to a heavy defeat (the \"year of Varadin\"). In Austria, by contrast, he came to occupy the centre of a folk song, a palace, a statue on a city square and a two-century-long heroic narrative. This narrative was later also used by nationalist and Nazi propaganda.", "e6083d98"], "bag": ["This card answers the question \"what do Austrians say about Prince Eugene in their own sources\" in two layers. The first layer is the heroic narrative itself: the song, the palace, the statue and Arneth's biography. The second layer is the criticism of that same narrative by present-day Austrian institutions: the Vienna city administration's wiki and \"Die Welt der Habsburger\". Placing the two side by side is this card's own choice; it is not a single-sentence verdict of any of the sources.", "b61f5596"]},
"ayan-hangi-aile-nereyi-tutuyordu":{"baslik": ["Which Ayan Held Which Region? Families with a Documented Record and Their Territories", "39a6ce72"], "metin": ["The rule of the list. Each line below has a documented reference; no family without a source has been written. The backbone is the breakdown of families by region in the TDV article `âyan` (Özcan Mert, 1991); for those that have their own articles, that article's reference has also been given. Another backbone of the same list appears in the TDV article `Selim III`: Selim III “had to put up with powerful ayan such as Pazvandoğlu, Tirsiniklioğlu, Tepedelenli, İşkodralı, Canikli, Cezzar and Kavalalı.”\n\nRUMELIA. ① Tirsiniklioğlu İsmail Agha — ayan of Rusçuk (d. 1806; it is recorded that in his youth he worked as a water carrier, and that in 1792, at the head of a band of sixty men, he roamed the areas of Sistova, Nikopol, Tarnovo, Hezargrad, Pleven and Lovech; TDV `tirsiniklioglu-ismail-aga`). ② Alemdar Mustafa Pasha — ayan of Hezargrad, then of Rusçuk, “ayan of ayans”, governor of Silistra and commander-in-chief of the Danube; the only ayan in Ottoman history to rise to the grand vizierate (TDV `alemdar-mustafa-pasa`). ③ Pazvandoğlu Osman — in the TDV Encyclopaedia's own definition, “the rebel ayan of the Vidin and Northern Bulgaria region” (d. 1807; his family was of Tuzla-Bosnian origin; TDV `pazvandoglu-osman`). ④ Ali Pasha of Tepelena (Tepedelenli) — Ioannina and its surroundings, “governor of Ioannina” in the TDV heading (d. 1822; his date of birth is disputed: 1740 or 1744, and 1750-51 in sources of the period; TDV `tepedelenli-ali-pasa`). ⑤ İşkodralı (the Bushatis) — Shkodra; no separate TDV article could be found, and its reference is the `selim-iii` sentence above. ⑥ From among those who personally signed the Sened-i İttifak: İsmail Bey of Serres (Siroz) and Mustafa Bey, mutasarrıf (district governor) of Çirmen; among those who took part in the negotiations were Hacıahmedoğlu Seyyid İbrahim Agha, voivode of Bolu, Ahmed Agha, ayan of Şile, and Kalyoncu Mustafa Bey, ayan of Bilecik (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`; the Kalyoncuoğulları in Bilecik, `ayan`).\n\nANATOLIA. ⑦ The Karaosmanoğulları — Manisa and its surroundings, “an ayan family influential in the Western Anatolia region in the 18th century”; when the founder, Kara Osman Agha, died in 1706, he left behind a family that would take on a role in the administration of the region (TDV `karaosmanogullari`, Yuzo Nagata). The Sened-i İttifak was signed by Karaosmanoğlu Ömer Agha. ⑧ The Çapanoğulları (Cebbarzades) — “Central Anatolia, with Bozok as its center”; Ahmed Agha was voivode of the Mamalu Türkmens in 1732 and of Bozok in 1741, and when in 1755 he obtained the voivodeship of Bozok as a malikâne (life-term tax farm) he “became the undisputed master of the region” (TDV `capanogullari`, Özcan Mert). The Deed was signed by Çapanoğlu Süleyman Bey. ⑨ Canikli Hacı Ali Pasha and his sons — Samsun and its surroundings (`ayan`; `selim-iii`). ⑩ The Tuzcuoğulları — the eastern Black Sea, centered on Rize; the earliest record is from 1763, from the 1760s they took over the office of ayan of Rize, and in 1806 Memiş Agha became warden of the fortress of Faş (Poti) (TDV `tuzcuogullari`, Mehmet Hacısalihoğlu). ⑪ The Kozanoğulları — Kozan/Çukurova; their origin is traced to Türkmen tribes that migrated to Cilicia (TDV `kozanogullari`). ⑫ The others counted in the same sentence of the `âyan` article, with their regions: the Zennecizades in Kayseri, the Müderriszades in Ankara, Kanlızade in Balıkesir, the Katiboğulları in İzmir, the Yılanlıoğulları in Isparta, the Tekelioğulları in Antalya, and the Menemencioğulları in Çukurova.\n\nARAB provinces. ⑬ The Azmzades (al-Azm) — Syria; a family “originally from Konya which later settled in Syria”, which held the posts of governor, mutasarrıf and commander of the pilgrimage (emirü'l-hac) (TDV `azmzadeler`, Ali Karaca). ⑭ The Babanzades (Babans) — northern Iraq (TDV `ayan`). ⑮ Cezzar Ahmed Pasha — Acre/Sidon (TDV `cezzar-ahmed-pasa`; `selim-iii` counts him among the powerful ayan). ⑯ Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala — Egypt (TDV `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa`; in the same `selim-iii` sentence).\n\nTHE LIMITS of the list. This is not an exhaustive inventory but a set of families for which a documented reference exists: even the TDV article `âyan` opens its own list with “Among these… had won fame”, that is, it says that it is giving a sample. At the district (kaza) scale there were hundreds of ayan (the ayan of 41 kazas subject to Tirsiniklioğlu alone have been counted — Bayrak 2009, p. 64), and their names have not been compiled with references at this scale.", "4cca4231"], "bag": ["The geographical pattern is no coincidence: in Rumelia the great armed ayan stand out (Pazvandoğlu, Tepedelenli, Tirsiniklioğlu), and in Anatolia the families linked to the center through life-term tax farms and voivodeships (Karaosmanoğulları, Çapanoğulları). Nagehan Üstündağ Özdemir records this difference explicitly: “The stages of development of the ayan in the Balkans and in Anatolia show some minor differences”, and these differences explain why the Mountain Bandit (Dağlı) revolts that engulfed the Balkans in the 18th century and the “type of the mighty ayan” were more pronounced in that region.", "af319d2f"]},
"ayanlik-isyan-mi-ozerklik-mi":{"baslik": ["Was the ayan system rebellion, autonomy or corrupt provincial government — and who appointed the ayan to office?", "c4f4f44d"], "ozet": ["One and the same institution can be read as three different things: an armed force opposed to the centre, a recognised form of local autonomy, or a decayed form of state office. The sources do not choose one of these three readings — depending on which ayan, which region and which year you look at, all three find support. There is also a more concrete question: who put people into this position — the sultan, the people, or whoever could seize it?", "ff1fe36a"], "metin": ["First, a definition: what the ayan were not. The article `âyan` in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam (by Özcan Mert) says that the word was used very broadly: \"The local dynasties that appear in historical documents as voyvoda, mütesellim, muhassıl, mutasarrıf and governor are at the same time also referred to by the terms ayan, derebeyi (lord of the valley) or mütegallibe (usurper).\" In other words, the same person could be mentioned in a document sometimes by an official title and sometimes by an epithet meaning \"strongman\" — the term itself carries a judgement. In the classical period the influence of an ayan \"did not extend beyond the limits of the settlement in which he lived\"; the institution began to grow from the second half of the sixteenth century onwards.\n\nWho appointed them to OFFICE? The answer changes over time, and TDV dates it step by step: the chief ayan (reîs-i a'yân) was at first determined by election — according to the documents, \"for the first time in the 1680s\" — and the person chosen had to \"obtain a collective petition (mahzar) from the people, a written attestation (ilâm) from the kadi and a written order (buyruldu) from the governor\"; in return for the order a fee called \"âyâniye\" was paid. Then governors began \"to issue orders appointing ayan in return for bribes\", and the centre intervened: in April 1765 governors were forbidden to issue such orders and an investigation by the grand vizier was made a condition; in January 1769, because of the war, \"a system of appointing ayan by popular election was adopted\"; in April 1779 there was a return to the procedure of 1765; in May 1784 the rule that \"no one could become chief ayan without a letter from the grand vizier\" was renewed; in 1786 the Sublime Porte put an end to the office of ayan altogether and replaced it with that of town steward (şehir kethüdâsı); in November 1790 the old arrangement returned, and ayan were appointed to office by a \"sharia court certificate (hüccet-i şer'iyye)\". So all three answers are correct, but in different years: the sultan or central government gives approval, the people and local notables elect, and in practice the office often goes to \"whoever grabs it\". Hereditary succession, for its part, came through a separate channel: because after 1695 certain tax-revenue sources (mukataa) were farmed out for life under the \"malikâne\" system, the ayan gained \"the opportunity to keep their sources of revenue going by inheritance\"; by the middle of the eighteenth century \"great families with practically the character of a dynasty\" had emerged. The guarantees given by the Deed of Alliance (Sened-i İttifak) likewise applied to the ayan \"themselves and, after their death, their children and dynasties\" — that is, the state had put de facto heredity into writing.\n\nThe FIRST reading — rebellion. The epithet TDV uses to describe some ayan says this directly: the heading of the article on Pazvandoğlu Osman calls him \"the rebel ayan of Vidin and the region of northern Bulgaria\"; to suppress his revolt Selim III mobilised \"as if waging war on a foreign state\", and still could not get rid of him (`selim-iii`). In the Second Edirne Incident of 1806, the ayan of Rumelia took up arms against the establishment of the New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army in Rumelia and forced the sultan to back down (`ayan`, `kabakci-isyani`).\n\nThe SECOND reading — recognised autonomy or partnership. The same ayan were officials of the same state: they collected taxes, supplied the army with soldiers and grain, sent meat to Istanbul, procured saltpetre for the gunpowder works and caught bandits (`ayan`). Nagehan Üstündağ Özdemir describes this relationship as \"a conflict involving mutual dependence\": the centre depends on the ayan for taxes, public order and soldiers, while the ayan need the centre's approval \"to receive administrative authority and ranks, to protect their own spheres of control against rivals and to prove their legitimacy\". Ali Yaycıoğlu's Partners of the Empire (2016) takes this reading a step further: according to a review that reports the book's argument, Yaycıoğlu regards the Deed of Alliance neither as \"a late Magna Carta that was a step toward progressive constitutionalism\" nor as \"a sign of political centralisation\"; in his words, the deed envisaged \"a horizontal partnership of elites, bearing full responsibility, whose security was mutually guaranteed\" (as reported in Bostan 2017, pp. 157-158).\n\nThe THIRD reading — corrupt provincial government. The source says this too, in its own words: while carrying out their duties the ayan \"very often also pursued their own interests, rightly or wrongly\", and struggles over the office of ayan turned \"from time to time into bloody clashes\"; it was observed that after Çapanoğlu Mustafa acquired the office of voyvoda of Bozok as a lifetime tax farm, he \"at times attempted to oppress the people\" (`capanogullari`). The state's own view is also close to this third reading: an archive-based study describing the practice of confiscation records that the state was \"of the opinion that the ayan generally amassed their wealth by illegitimate means\" and for this reason tended to seize their property (Bayrak 2009, p. 69).\n\nThis card passes no VERDICT. All three readings are measurable faces of the same institution; which of them comes to the fore varies with the region (Rumelia or Anatolia), the year (before 1765, 1786, or after 1790) and the individual. The outcome, however, was written by the centre: Mahmud II established central authority \"by sending armies against rebellious ayan and sometimes by setting them against one another\"; against those who were obedient and powerful he did not use force; \"he waited for them to die and then reduced their influence in the provinces by appointing in their place not their heirs but representatives of the Sublime Porte\" (`ayan`).", "9727a98b"], "bag": ["View: The Deed of Alliance was a constitutional moment — a document resembling Magna Carta, by which the ayan obtained rights against the sultan. Counter-view (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`, Ali Akyıldız): the document is a text that \"arose under the pressure of historical events and circumstances\" and \"is not, as constitutional lawyers in particular have heavily emphasised, a document envisaging an advanced state order\"; whereas in the case of Magna Carta there was a strong social group defending its rights against the king, \"in the Deed of Alliance there is no such party that embraced the rights contained in the deed\" — most of the ayan did not sign it, and those who did sign did not stand by it afterwards. A third view (Yaycıoğlu 2016): it was neither — it was a horizontal partnership of elites. One detail also shifts the ground of this debate: the full text of the deed was found and published only in 1998; earlier assessments relied on the incomplete text in the History of Cevdet (Târîh-i Cevdet) and in Şânîzâde (TDV `sened-i-ittifak`).", "8eb614a8"]},
"bakis-arabistan":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Saudi Arabian eyes: the caliphate, Diriyah and Turkism", "3ec89e4d"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — In a thesis prepared at Marmara University in 2010, Zehra Gökdemir examined Saudi Arabia's primary, middle and high-school textbooks. According to her, the books cover the Ottoman state from its foundation to the deposition of Abdülhamid II (1909) and treat it as the ‘Islamic Caliphate’ after the Abbasids. The Ottoman caliphate and its centuries-long political supremacy are presented as a vital reality for Muslim unity. The books also stress that the Ottomans spread certain religious innovations (bid'a) in the region. But they also underline that the whole Arab world paid the price of the Ottoman collapse by being colonised. According to the chapter on Saudi Arabia in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, in the account of the Crusades the Turks are treated as a nation protecting the holy lands from the enemies of Islam, a ‘warrior nation’. It is also written that for four centuries the Ottoman ruler was the caliph of the Muslims.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — According to Gökdemir, nowhere in Saudi books are the words ‘occupier’, ‘tyrant’, ‘oppressor’, ‘exploiter’ or ‘despot’ used for the Ottomans. The negative information falls under four headings: İbrahim Pasha's failure to keep the agreement he made with Abdullah, leader of the Saud tribe, and his reduction of the city to ruins (Diriyah); the Turkification policy of the Committee of Union and Progress; Turkism and Turanism; and Mustafa Kemal Atatürk's severing of Arab–Turkish unity. According to the books, with the 31 March Incident (1909) a line was drawn under the history of Arab–Turkish brotherhood. By Gökdemir's count, the Turks appear on 208 (about 46%) of the 453 textbook pages in total that are relevant to the thesis; they feature in 70% of the history books.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — Gökdemir's study covers books up to 2010. Apart from press reports, no academic study could be found of the changes said to have been made after that date to the Ottoman narrative in the Saudi curriculum; for this reason they are not included here.", "3c920ee8"], "kisa": ["Saudi textbooks describe the Ottoman state as the Islamic caliphate after the Abbasids and consider it vital for Muslim unity. The words ‘occupier’ or ‘oppressor’ do not appear in the books. Criticism centres on four points: İbrahim Pasha's Diriyah campaign, the Committee of Union and Progress, Turkism, and the abolition of the caliphate.", "1c2d3922"]},
"bakis-arap":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Arab eyes (Syria and Jordan): the ‘occupying state’ and the Arab Revolt", "0fb55e4c"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: The sections on the Arab countries in the 38-country textbook study edited by Ahmet Şimşek and published in 2018 are summarised in Emel Akbaş's critique. According to this summary, the textbooks of the Arab states generally use the name ‘Turk’ in the sense of ‘Muslim’. What the books have in common is a negative judgement on the rule of the Committee of Union and Progress: the policy of this period is described as ‘racist’. In Syrian books the national narrative is built on secular Arab nationalism.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: SYRIA: Because the author of the section could not obtain current books owing to the civil war, he or she examined the books used in 2009-2010. The first information about the Turks is given in the account of the Abbasids; the Ayyubids, Seljuks and Mamluks are also mentioned. The Ottoman Empire, which takes up a large amount of space, is introduced to the pupil as an ‘occupying’ state and shown to be responsible for the backwardness of the Arab provinces. The Ottoman entry into Egypt is described as an ‘occupation’, whereas the French entry into Egypt is described as a ‘French intervention’. On the maps in the books, southeastern Turkey is shown within the borders of Syria. JORDAN: There are two courses, Arab and World History and History of Jordan, and only the single book distributed by the Ministry of Education is used. The pre-Ottoman Turks are not mentioned at all; the Ottoman state is described in broad outline. The Arab Revolt is treated separately because Sharif Hussein was born and lived in Jordanian lands. The revolt is described as a movement that rested on just causes and achieved success. The British occupation of Palestine is also presented as the fault of the Ottomans.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: The studies used here concern books from between 2009-2010 and 2018; no comparative study could be found for the periods before and after. For Egypt and Saudi Arabia, see the separate supplementary readings: within the same Arab world, Egyptian books portray the Ottomans entirely negatively, while Saudi books portray them largely positively.", "a60a857a"], "kisa": ["In Syrian and Jordanian textbooks, the Ottoman Empire is a domination lasting four centuries; in Syrian books it is openly an ‘occupying state’ and the cause of the backwardness of the Arab provinces. Jordanian books describe Sharif Hussein's revolt as a just and successful movement. In both countries the harshest criticism is directed at the Turkist policy of the Committee of Union and Progress.", "a27d04f1"]},
"bakis-arnavut":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Albanian eyes: Skanderbeg's resistance and the image of the ‘occupier’", "fcb1b3d2"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: According to Bülent Bilmez's study published in 2017, the most prominent image of the Ottomans in Albanian textbooks is that of the ‘occupying/foreign’ power that kept the Albanians under the yoke for centuries and exploited them. This emphasis on ‘exploitation’ carries the story of a long-exploited people attaining independence. The second image is that of the ‘reactionary’ Ottomans: the ‘national renaissance’ that Albania could have achieved was postponed because of the occupation. Bilmez lists other images as well: the Ottomans as representatives of an Islam regarded as an obstacle to modernisation, the Ottomans as outsiders, and an equation of Oriental-Ottoman-Muslim-Turk regarded as the source of all that is negative. One 11th-grade book refers to the Ottomans as a power that occupied the Balkans and Albania for 600 years. Bilmez objects to this by recalling the following: when the Ottomans arrived in the region there was no unified Albania, and these lands came together only under Ottoman rule.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: After the 2008 reform, history begins in the 6th grade of the nine-year school; world history is taught first, then Albanian history. Skanderbeg's resistance between 1443 and 1467 is given prominence in the 6th-, 9th- and 12th-grade books. Skanderbeg is treated as a ‘national hero’ representing all Albanians, while the enemy is the Ottoman occupier coming from outside. Ahmet Tuna Sürenoğlu and Sezai Öztaş examined the high school books printed in 2016 (grades 10, 11 and 12). The 10th-grade book describes the Ottoman expansion in the Balkans chronologically in its social, cultural and religious aspects and stresses that the policy of Islamisation was not oppressive; images of the Conqueror (Mehmed II) and Suleiman the Lawgiver also appear in the book. In the 11th-grade book, ‘anti-Ottoman resistance’ is a key concept. The book also describes the pashaliks established from the 18th century onwards and the Albanian Renaissance.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: According to Bilmez, the only noteworthy change seen in historical writing since the 1990s is this: liberal Eurocentric discourse took the place of Marxist-Leninist Eurocentric terminology. The format of the books changed through European Union projects, but there was no break as far as historians and institutions were concerned. Sürenoğlu and Öztaş write that adjectives such as ‘cruel’ and ‘destructive’, which Olsi Jazexhi found in the old books in 2009, do not appear in the current high school books. An old book related that the person who killed Murad I at Kosovo was an Albanian; this information is also absent from the current books. Atatürk, on the other hand, is still presented with sympathy today, as in the earlier books. Even so, the two authors point out that the image of the ‘occupier’ persists; in some extracts the Turks still appear as oppressors.", "ef6e77f1"], "kisa": ["Albania is the first Muslim-majority nation state to separate from the Ottoman Empire. Despite this, in school textbooks the Ottomans mostly appear as an ‘occupying and foreign’ power that kept the Albanians under the yoke for centuries. At the centre of the narrative stands the quarter-century resistance of Skanderbeg (Skënderbeu). In the latest books the harsh adjectives have become fewer, but the image of the ‘occupier’ persists.", "1970b7c9"]},
"bakis-avusturya":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Austrian eyes: the two sieges of Vienna and the ‘memory of the Turks’", "1793db66"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: In a thesis prepared at the University of Graz in 2015, Eva Maria Müller examined five history textbooks used in lower secondary schools (Neue Mittelschule) in Graz. Although the Ottoman-Habsburg conflict is not mentioned by name in the curriculum, it is an important part of lessons. The books focus on the two sieges of Vienna (1529, 1683) and the Habsburg campaigns that followed. According to Müller, the topics selected coincide with the national collective memory formed in the 19th century. The siege of 1683 and the rise of Austria as a great power take up the most space, because these events lend themselves to displaying national strength and resilience.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: All the written source extracts in the books relate to the siege of 1683. The illustrations likewise mostly concern 1683 or Prince Eugene of Savoy, and all are works by European painters. The events between the two sieges, that is, the struggle for Hungary and the ‘Long War’ (1593-1606) started by the Habsburgs, are not mentioned at all. Nor are the Habsburg raids into Ottoman territory described, while marriage policy is presented as a peaceful route to expansion. According to the Austria section of the 38-country study edited by Şimşek, the books have little text and lean heavily on questions. The same section notes that in the account of the Middle Ages it is written that the Turks treated Christians in Jerusalem mercilessly, and that the narrative concerning the Turks deepens with the Ottoman entry into the Balkans.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: According to Müller, of the five books examined only two still use ‘Turks’ as a synonym for ‘Ottomans’; one discusses the difference between the two concepts separately. Some authors try to move away from the old viewpoint by stating that Austrian soldiers also used violence and by pointing to the religious tolerance of the Ottoman rulers. Müller nevertheless writes that some explanations are biased and lag behind current research. According to the pupils she interviewed, lessons include trips to the ‘Turkish monuments’ in Graz; the belief that the Ottomans really came to Graz is also still widespread. According to the section in the volume edited by Şimşek, the curriculum updated in 2016 aims at an awareness of living together in a multicultural environment and stresses that present-day Turks should be kept distinct from the Ottomans.", "fc55825e"], "kisa": ["In Austrian school textbooks, the Ottomans are remembered above all for the two sieges of Vienna (1529 and 1683). The victory of 1683 is presented as the beginning of Austria's emergence as a great power. Newer books increasingly say ‘Ottomans’ instead of ‘Turks’; some also mention the violence of Habsburg soldiers and Ottoman religious tolerance.", "54eff56f"]},
"bakis-azeri":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Azerbaijani eyes: a brother state, or the Safavids' rival?", "3d2ca753"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — The Azerbaijani researchers Ilham Abbasov and Sergey Rumyantsev examined the textbooks on the history of Azerbaijan revised in the early 2000s. According to them, the unity of the Oghuz Turks is at the centre of the narrative in these books. The Sunni–Shi'ite split is presented as an intrigue of ‘old enemies’ that set ‘blood brothers’ against one another. The conflict between Sultan Selim and Shah Ismail Khatai is also told within this framework. The books refer to Shah Ismail's state as ‘Safavid Azerbaijan’, whereas European historians regard it as a dynasty ruling the Persian Empire. According to one book, Western European rulers succeeded in inciting Timur against Bayezid I the Thunderbolt, Uzun Hasan against Mehmed the Conqueror, Shah Ismail against Sultan Selim, and Shah Tahmasp against Suleiman the Lawgiver; thus the enmity of the great Turkish rulers towards one another weakened the power of the Turkish world.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — Salim Gökçen and Meriç Aybar compared Azerbaijan's 8th- and 9th-grade ‘History of Azerbaijan’ books with Turkey's 10th-grade book. According to them, the 8th-grade book, in describing the Safavid–Ottoman struggles, calls the Ottomans ‘occupiers’. The 9th-grade book, on the other hand, speaks of common ancestry in the Ottoman–Russian struggles and portrays the Ottomans as a state ever ready to rush to the aid of the Azerbaijani people. The authors see this as a contradiction in the books: when the Safavid period is described, pro-Safavid sympathy comes to the fore, whereas for the late Ottoman period it is pro-Ottoman sympathy. The cause of the Ottoman–Russian War of 1768–1774 also differs in the books of the two countries. Turkey's book points to the Polish refugees, while Azerbaijan's book points to the secret talks of the Azeri khanates with the Ottomans.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — According to the chapter on Azerbaijan in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, after independence in 1991 the ‘General History’ course was rewritten and a ‘History of Azerbaijan’ course was introduced. In the books, Dede Korkut is stressed as the most important legacy left by the Oghuz ancestors of the Azerbaijani people; the Turkish states in history are generally viewed positively. According to Abbasov and Rumyantsev, whereas in the Soviet period every possible reason was sought to stress the voluntary nature of joining the USSR, today everything is told in the opposite direction. In the account of the 18th-century khanates, too, the Ottomans are presented as the only friendly power that could help Azerbaijan.", "587077e5"], "kisa": ["The textbooks of independent Azerbaijan show two different faces of the Ottomans. When the Safavid–Ottoman wars are described, the Safavids' side is taken, and one book even calls the Ottomans ‘occupiers’. Against Russia, however, the Ottomans are a brother state of the same stock rushing to help. Chaldiran is described as the result of foreign intrigues that set the ‘Turkish brothers’ against one another.", "dbc313de"]},
"bakis-bosnak":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Bosniak eyes: a shared past, tolerance and estrangement after the 18th century", "5c5459e2"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: Ahmet Alibašić of the Faculty of Islamic Studies in Sarajevo examined the Bosnian-language primary school books printed between 2001 and 2005. These books try to tell Ottoman history from the viewpoint not of a single people but of all the peoples of Bosnia-Herzegovina. Alongside political and military history, they also give ample space to economic, social and cultural history. One book regards Islamisation as the most important change of this period and describes it as a ‘gradual and entirely voluntary’ process. Cited as evidence of tolerance are the Conqueror's ahidname (charter) of 1463, the re-establishment of the Patriarchate of Peć, the admission of the Jews coming from Spain, and the craft guilds with members of different faiths. The books also give prominence to the culture of ‘neighbourliness’.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: According to Alibašić, the books stress that the situation of the Muslim and Christian reaya (tax-paying subjects) was similar: Christians paid tribute, while Muslims went to war. From the 18th century onwards the account changes. The situation worsens because of lost wars, epidemics and the chiftlik (large estate) system; the Bosniaks lose their trust in the Sublime Porte and begin to see the Ottoman officials as foreigners who had come to enrich themselves. The narrative reaches its climax with the revolt of Husein Kapetan Gradaščević and with the feeling in 1878 that ‘the sultan had sold Bosnia to the Austrians’. Alibašić finds this picture too rosy in places. One book describes the devshirme (child levy) as a ‘privilege’ granted to the Bosnians; according to Alibašić, this ignores the suffering of the Christian families whose children were taken. The books mention the re-establishment of the Patriarchate of Peć but not its abolition.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: According to Bakir Ovčina's study, the books printed in Bosnia during the socialist Yugoslav period (1945-1990) described the Ottoman period as a dark age, even in Bosnia, where the Muslim population was large. The broadening of Muslims' political rights after the 1960s did not fundamentally change this picture either. The Bosnian-language books written after the 1990s, as Alibašić shows, depart markedly from this narrative. The Bosnia-Herzegovina section of the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek also finds the view of the Ottomans in Bosniak books positive, with some exceptions. In the books that Serbs and Croats in the same country use in their own schools, however, the Ottomans are still mostly the ‘occupier’.", "a42e4b1e"], "kisa": ["Bosnian-language textbooks describe the Ottoman period as a shared past in which the three peoples of today's Bosnia lived together. Islamisation is presented as ‘voluntary’ and Ottoman rule as ‘tolerant’. But it is also stressed that after the 18th century the Bosniaks lost their trust in Istanbul. Studies find this picture too idealised in places.", "b96e1162"]},
"bakis-bulgar":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Bulgarian eyes: from the ‘Turkish yoke’ to ‘delayed development’", "e8110313"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: According to the Polish historian Dariusz Kołodziejczyk, the concept of the ‘Turkish yoke’ took root in the Bulgarian popular imagination through Ivan Vazov's novel ‘Under the Yoke’ (Pod igoto), written at the end of the 19th century. The concept passed from political and literary language into academia as well. Kołodziejczyk lists the stereotypes tied to this concept as follows: the conquest destroyed states whose national consciousness had already taken shape; for five hundred years Christian subjects regarded the Ottomans as foreign and hostile, and cooperation with the state was considered ‘treason’; the Muslim culture that developed in the Balkans was deemed the work of ‘converts’ and was not admitted into the national heritage; Ottoman rule was held to be the cause of present-day backwardness; and brigands were portrayed as conscious national leaders.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: In 2002 the Bulgarian historians Aleksey Kalionski and Valeri Kolev summarised the textbooks of the time as follows: Ottoman rule (1354-1878; for Macedonia and Thrace, until 1913) interrupts the development of the Bulgarian people. The books do not portray the period as ‘completely dark’, but they treat it as a tragic departure from the main road of European history; the development of the Bulgarians is described as ‘delayed’. The Ottomans are scarcely discussed at all in the cultural sphere, the sole exception being Islam. The Orthodox Church, by contrast, is presented as the decisive force in the ‘survival’ of the people and their culture. The conversion of one section of the population to Islam is treated as a tragic division of the national body. The books distinguish between the Ottoman rulers and the ‘ordinary Turk’; they also describe how Christians, Turks and Muslims ‘suffered’ together from taxes and wars. According to Neriman Ersoy Hacısalihoğlu, history is taught in grades 5-12 using books entitled ‘History and Civilisation’. The devshirme (child levy) first appears in the 5th grade under the name ‘blood tax’ (krăven danăk), as part of forced Islamisation.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: Ersoy Hacısalihoğlu writes that pre-1990 textbooks described Ottoman rule under the heading ‘Under the Turkish yoke’ (pod tursko igo), and that Vazov's novel was also taught in schools as a core work. After the change of regime in 1990 the books were rewritten and some very negative expressions were toned down. According to the author, the expression ‘Ottoman slavery’ was largely abandoned in Balkan textbooks in general after 1990. But the stereotypes persist: the ‘blood tax’ is mentioned only briefly in the textbook, whereas in supplementary books and children's encyclopaedias it is presented with abundant illustrations as an example of ‘Turkish oppression’. In a survey the author conducted in Sofia and Varna in 2013, 89% of university students said that there had been forced conversion to Islam in the Ottoman period. In academia, meanwhile, Kołodziejczyk notes that in the work of Bulgarian historians the Islamic past is no longer taboo.", "e5b6f067"], "kisa": ["In the Bulgarian narrative, the Ottoman period is a long interruption between the fall of the medieval Bulgarian state and 1878. Until 1990, school textbooks presented this period under the heading ‘Under the Turkish yoke’. After 1990 the language softened, but the themes of the ‘blood tax’ and forced conversion to Islam persist in supplementary books.", "3fc1d1b5"]},
"bakis-cezayir":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Algerian eyes: not conquest but ‘voluntary union’", "8a8d7dc3"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — Zafer Çelik, Ayşenur İpek and Mehmet Sadık Gür examined 29 textbooks in Arabic, history, geography and civics taught in Algeria from primary school to high school. According to them, these books describe the Ottoman Empire not only as a conquering power but as a superior civilisation in science, architecture, culture and art, and as the protector of the Islamic world. The books stress voluntary union instead of conquest. The Ottoman–Algerian relationship is presented as one based on cooperation and mutual support. Istanbul is treated as a centre of science, culture and thought, that is, as an alternative to European civilisation.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — The 4th-grade history book describes the event as follows: Spain's dominance in the Mediterranean began to threaten Algiers. Oruç Reis and Hayreddin Barbarossa, who resisted European piracy in the Mediterranean and rescued Andalusians, were very famous at that time. When Spanish attacks increased, the people of Algiers asked them for help. After Oruç Reis was martyred in 1518, Hayreddin Barbarossa advised the people to place themselves under the protection of the Ottomans, with whom they shared the same religion. When the people of Algiers accepted this protection, Algeria became an Ottoman province. The books frequently stress that, unlike in other Arab countries, the Ottomans came to Algeria at the people's request. It is also written that Algeria was semi-independent, or even fully independent. As evidence of this they cite its being referred to as the ‘Republic of Algiers’ in correspondence with King Louis XIV of France, its direct diplomatic relations with European states, and its own land and naval forces. The textbooks also highlight the importance that Mehmed the Conqueror and Suleiman the Lawgiver attached to learning. The literature and history books include poems written to Istanbul, among them lines from Nabi's poem on Istanbul.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — Çelik, İpek and Gür link this narrative to Algeria's experience of French colonialism and to the politics of memory after independence. According to them, a positive Ottoman past serves as a historical support against the trauma created by colonialism and as a source of anti-colonial consciousness. The authors also say that this narrative is selective: basing the relationship on voluntariness and mutual consent renders invisible the power inequalities and political tensions of imperial rule. The authors compare this picture with the negative Ottoman narrative in the textbooks of Egypt, Lebanon and Macedonia. However, their study concerns the books taught today (some were printed before 2020). No comparative study measuring how the narrative has changed over time could be accessed.", "cef0779a"], "kisa": ["Unlike those of most former Ottoman lands, Algerian textbooks describe the Ottoman period in a largely positive way. According to this narrative, Algeria was not conquered; it voluntarily came under Ottoman protection by asking for the help of Oruç Reis and Hayreddin Barbarossa against Spanish attacks. The Ottomans are described as a just civilisation, and French colonialism as violence and oppression.", "23bcd479"]},
"bakis-ermeni":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Armenian eyes: a small nation facing great empires, and 1915", "0aa7c49f"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — The Armenian researchers Mikayel Zolyan and Tigran Zakaryan examined the textbooks on the history of Armenia taught in the 2000s. According to them, the main pattern of Armenian political history is the unequal struggle between a giant empire and a small, freedom-loving nation. The role of the enemy is mostly played by conquerors from outside the Caucasus: Persians, Romans, Byzantium, Arabs, Mongols and Turks. According to the authors, pagan Rome and Persia, Christian Byzantium, the Muslim Arab caliphate, the Safavids and the Ottoman Empire are viewed equally negatively; the books do not distinguish enemies by their religion. The sole exception is Russia: the incorporation of Eastern Armenia into Russia through the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828 is described as an unequivocal ‘liberation’. The books also deal with Israel Ori's search in the 18th century for allies for liberation from the ‘Turkish and Persian yoke’.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — According to Zolyan and Zakaryan, the events of 1915–16 in Ottoman Turkey are the key event of the Armenian national historical narrative and are described in the books as genocide. According to the authors, on this subject the books manage to avoid excessive emotionalism and ethnic stereotypes: it is not the whole Turkish nation but ‘the authorities’, ‘the Young Turks’ and those at the head of the Turkish government of the time who are shown as responsible. The Turkish researcher Yıldız Deveci Bozkuş, for her part, examined the books taught in the 2010–2011 school year. According to an article introducing her book, Bozkuş makes the following findings: the theme that Armenian lands were ‘occupied and invaded’ in the Seljuk and Ottoman periods recurs in the books of almost all grades. When the Turks are mentioned, words such as ‘murderer’, ‘executioner’ and ‘enemy’ are frequently used. The events of 1915 are treated in depth from the 8th grade onwards.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — According to Bozkuş, although the books still bear traces of Soviet-era textbooks, in recent times they have moved away from Marxism-Leninism and been prepared in line with the official state ideology. Zolyan and Zakaryan also note that post-independence textbooks reflect a dilemma in Armenian national historiography: should independence have been won with the help of the great powers, or solely by one's own strength? The preface to the 8th-grade book stresses that it is impossible to escape the yoke of the great empires by one's own strength alone. The same preface also says that the interests of the great powers cannot be relied upon and that national problems can be solved only by one's own strength. From other parts of the book, however, it is understood that joining Russia was far preferable to the ‘Turkish yoke’.", "1bbb7fd2"], "kisa": ["The main pattern of Armenian textbooks is the unequal struggle of a small, freedom-loving nation against giant empires. The Ottomans are one of these empires; the events of 1915–16 are the key event of the national narrative and are described in the books as ‘genocide’. Armenian and Turkish researchers have examined these books and reached different conclusions on how far the image of the enemy is ‘ethnic’.", "c66c50bc"]},
"bakis-gurcu":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Georgian eyes: a country caught between great empires", "05bb1bcb"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — Nino Chikovani and Ketevan Kakitelashvili of Tbilisi State University examined grade 7–12 textbooks written or revised after the education reform in Georgia. According to them, in these books Georgia, and the Caucasus in general, appear as an arena of struggle and conflict among outside powers. Some of these ‘others’ are unequivocally regarded as conquerors/invaders: Assyria, Urartu, the Scythians, the Cimmerians, the Sarmatians, Alexander the Great, Rome, the Parthians, the Achaemenid Persians, the Arabs, the Seljuks, the Mongols, the Ottoman Empire and late medieval Iran. Others are described sometimes as conquerors and sometimes as allies or protectors: the ancient Greeks, Byzantium, the Western Christian world, Russia and the European countries.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — According to the authors, Europe appears in the books as the only possible ally, yet it is still an ‘other’; it is not as distant as the Muslim world. In the Middle Ages some Georgian kings tried to win the support of Catholic Europe against Iran and Turkey, and for this they were even prepared to convert to Catholicism. Russia, for its part, is treated both as a co-religionist ally and protector and as the power that ‘occupied’ the country through its policy of Russification. The books also relate that Russia's violations of treaties led to punitive campaigns by Iran or Turkey.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — According to Chikovani and Kakitelashvili, derogatory adjectives are not used for the ‘others’ in the post-reform books. The only exceptions are in quotations taken from medieval sources, for example the expression ‘infidels’ for the Ottoman Turks. Phrases found in pre-reform books, such as ‘wild tribes began to descend from the mountains’, have been removed from the new texts. When such expressions are quoted from a source, the authors add an explanation. Some books prepared by authors from the pre-reform era, however, continue the old heroic language.", "78a4d29d"], "kisa": ["In Georgian textbooks Georgia and the Caucasus are an arena in which great empires contend. The Ottomans are among those empires unequivocally regarded as ‘conquerors/invaders’. In the post-reform books, however, derogatory adjectives have been largely cleared away.", "a2b68cd2"]},
"bakis-hirvat":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Croatian eyes: the ‘bulwark of Christendom’ and the ‘uninvited guest’", "d2f84842"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: Ahmet Alibašić examined the Croatian-language primary school books used by Croats in Bosnia-Herzegovina (Školska naklada, Mostar, 2001). According to him, the myth of being Christian Europe's ‘bulwark’ against Islam appears openly in these books. The headings foreground the Croatian viewpoint: one chapter is headed ‘The Turkish threat approaches Croatia and Bosnia’. The books repeatedly stress that the Catholics were the greatest losers under Ottoman rule. The Orthodox clergy, on the other hand, are said to have been ‘privileged’. According to the Croatia section of the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, the Ottomans are described as an ‘uninvited guest’ who came for a time and was watched with a hostility mingled with fear and respect.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: According to Alibašić, Croatian books give the Ottomans less space than Serbian and Bosniak books do: 19 of 158 pages in the 6th-grade book, and only 3 pages in the 7th-grade book. The account gives weight to military events. But these books also include positive statements about the early Ottomans. According to one book, in its first period Turkey was a state that Europe envied for its strength and attractive social order; thanks to its religious tolerance it was a refuge for those persecuted because of their religion. The same book writes that Christians voluntarily became Muslims in order to escape the heavy tribute and to move into the privileged class, whereas the devshirme was forced Islamisation. The books also portray the sultan as the protector of the reaya (tax-paying subjects) and the local ‘Turks’ as exploiters whom the sultan could not control. According to the section in the volume edited by Şimşek, textbooks in Croatia devote ample space to the structure of Ottoman society and to Ottoman-Croatian political relations.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: Alibašić's study belongs to the period of textbook revision that began in Bosnia-Herzegovina after the war of the 1990s. Under the agreements of 1998-2000, ‘offensive’ passages were blacked out or stamped; in 2005 common principles for multiperspective history teaching were adopted. According to Alibašić, this revision found more passages needing correction in the Serbian and Croatian books than in the Bosniak ones. No detailed study could be accessed on how the Ottoman narrative in Croatia's own school textbooks changed from the 1980s to the 2000s.", "a94678c3"], "kisa": ["In Croatian textbooks, the Ottoman period is described as an age of defence in which Croatia was the ‘bulwark of Christendom’ (antemurale Christianitatis). The Ottomans are an ‘uninvited guest’ watched with fear and respect but received with hostility. Even so, some books openly acknowledge the strength and religious tolerance of the early Ottomans.", "36b50eeb"]},
"bakis-iran":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Iranian eyes: a Sunni threat to Shi'ism", "a8b879ae"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — Hülya Çelik and M. Bilal Çelik examined Iranian middle-school history textbooks printed in 1384 (2007). According to them, the books cast Turks outside Iran's borders as the ‘other’, while the Turkish dynasties that ruled Iran are mentioned with praise. The Ghaznavids are presented under the heading ‘The first dynasty of Turkish origin in Iran’, and the Seljuks under ‘The Seljuks, who founded a powerful state in Iran’; Nizam al-Mulk is spoken of with praise. The Ottomans, by contrast, are seen as a threat to Shi'ism. According to the chapter on Iran in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, the books introduce the Turkish elements that founded states in Iran not by their Turkish identity but by their Shi'ite or Kizilbash identity.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — The Ottomans appear most in the section on the Safavids in the third year of middle school. The book describes Chaldiran as follows: after the birth of a Shi'ite and powerful Safavid state, Sultan Selim, who considered himself the ruler of all Muslims, attacked Iran. Despite their great efforts, the Iranians were defeated, because the Ottoman army used cannon and firearms, while the Iranian army fought with swords, arrows and shields. Tabriz was occupied, but in the face of the people's resistance the Ottoman forces withdrew. The peace concluded in the reign of Tahmasp is said to have brought order to the country. Shah Abbas's twenty-year war with the Ottomans, for its part, ends in an Iranian victory. According to Çelik and Çelik, the book does not mention Shah Ismail's efforts to convert Eastern Anatolia to Shi'ism. For the periods of the Afsharids and Zands, it is also related that Nader Shah defeated the Ottomans.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — According to the chapter on Iran in the Şimşek volume, the books changed continually with the education reforms after the 1979 Revolution. With the latest arrangements, history began to be taught as a separate subject only in high school, and eleven different history textbooks are in use in schools. According to the same chapter, the books present the Turks as a people who, like the Mongols, invaded Iranian lands. The era of Reza Shah is harshly criticised; the reforms of the Atatürk era are also criticised indirectly. No study comparing these with the textbooks of the pre-1979 Pahlavi period could be accessed.", "ea6c922a"], "kisa": ["Post-revolutionary Iranian textbooks speak with praise of the Turkish dynasties that ruled Iran (the Ghaznavids, the Seljuks), while the Ottomans appear as a Sunni threat standing against the Shi'ite Safavid state. The defeat at Chaldiran is explained by the superiority of Ottoman cannon. The long wars, however, end with Shah Abbas's victory.", "8f9efe57"]},
"bakis-kosova":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Kosovo Albanian eyes: the Battle of Kosovo, Skanderbeg and the image of the ‘occupier’", "25cec0bf"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — Fatih Fuat Tuncer examined the primary and secondary school history textbooks that were compulsory for Albanian students in Kosovo in the 2010–2011 school year. According to Tuncer, Kosovo's historiography is directly tied to that of Albania; the curricula of the two countries are almost identical. The most frequently used image of the Ottomans in the books is that of the ‘occupier’; the expression ‘period of occupation’ is used almost as a synonym for the Ottoman period. Some texts also contain the descriptions ‘practising terror’, ‘medieval darkness’, ‘feudal’, ‘tyrannical’, ‘intolerant’, ‘repressive’, ‘barbaric’, ‘forcing people to change their religion’, ‘taking children hostage’ and ‘assimilating’. The books frequently recall that before Islam the Albanians were mostly Catholic. For the period of separation, they relate that the sultan, the Young Turks and the Committee of Union and Progress deceived the Albanians and dragged them into turmoil. The chapter on Kosovo in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek also notes that Albanian-language high-school books use expressions such as ‘cruel’, ‘savage’, ‘terror state’, ‘police regime’ and ‘taker of the blood tax’ for Ottoman rule.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — The Battle of Kosovo is first covered in the 5th grade, in the unit ‘The Ottoman attack on the Arbër lands’, under the heading ‘The war of the Balkan peoples against the Ottomans’. The 7th-grade book describes the battle as ‘the joint resistance of the Balkan peoples against the Ottomans’ and highlights the Albanian lords in the coalition. The man who killed Murad I is referred to as ‘Milosh Kopiliq’, and his ethnic origin is said to be uncertain; Tuncer recalls that the name in Serbian and Western sources is Miloš Obilić, and that the 5th-grade book written for the Turks of Kosovo also uses this name. Skanderbeg is covered in the 5th, 7th and 11th grades, under headings such as ‘Skanderbeg, the Heroic Commander’ and ‘Skanderbeg's exemplary love of freedom’. The 7th-grade book presents him as the ‘guardian of European civilisation’ who held back the Ottoman armies for twenty-five years. The same book describes the devshirme (child levy) as a ‘barbaric’ system and a ‘blood tax’; it also adds that the children taken could rise to the highest offices of the state. Turkey, however, is not regarded as the heir of the Ottomans: the 9th-grade book describes Mustafa Kemal as ‘a patriotic, good and talented soldier, a democrat and an opponent of monarchy’.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — In August 2011 Turkey's Minister of National Education, Ömer Dinçer, who was visiting Kosovo, asked for certain negative statements to be removed from the textbooks; Foreign Minister Ahmet Davutoğlu also said that Kosovar historians needed to free themselves from ‘Cold War historiography’. Frashër Demaj, one of the textbook authors, retorted, ‘The Ottomans did not come to the Balkans on holiday; they occupied them for 523 years’; another author, Fehmi Rexhepi, said that there were no anti-Turkish statements in the books. Kosovo's Ministry of Education set up a commission to examine the disputed points. The minister said that the aim was not to change history but to remove defamatory and derogatory statements, and that the European Commission wanted this too. What the commission changed in the books is not covered in these studies. In a survey Tuncer conducted among Kosovo Albanian students, the word ‘Ottoman’ most often evoked ‘occupier’ and ‘enemy’. Tuncer also notes that in Kosovo Albanian and Turkish students read separate books, while Serbian students use books printed in Belgrade.", "7c8463ef"], "kisa": ["The history textbooks written for Albanian students in Kosovo follow the same line as the historiography in Albania: the Ottoman period is the ‘period of occupation’ and the Ottomans are the ‘occupier’. The Battle of Kosovo is described as the joint resistance of the Balkan peoples against the Ottomans, and Skanderbeg as the guardian of European civilisation. The Republic of Turkey and Atatürk, however, are kept separate from the Ottomans and portrayed positively. In 2011, following Turkey's objection, the books became the subject of a major controversy in Kosovo.", "32a349b0"]},
"bakis-makedon":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Macedonian eyes: the ‘long Ottoman domination’ and the liberation movements", "b603d953"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — In 2002 the historian Nikola Jordanovski, who had worked at the Institute of National History in Skopje, examined Macedonian school textbooks. According to him, the Ottoman state is clearly shown in the books from the outset as a Turkish state, and it remains unacceptable for centuries; since the narrative constantly shows uprisings and resistance, the conclusion that emerges is that the Christian subjects never embraced this state. The Ottomans are ‘conquerors’ who seized the Balkans through successive wars or by exploiting the quarrels of the small Christian kingdoms. The books describe Ottoman rule as inclined to change the fabric of the country through forced resettlement, Islamisation and the changing of place names. According to Jordanovski, the empire as a whole plays the role of a ‘giant anti-hero’ standing in the way of legitimate national aspirations. In the 19th century the Ottoman state almost vanishes from the narrative; all that remains is a ‘doomed’ structure from which one must be liberated.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — According to Emilija Simoska, history is not taught as a separate subject of national history but as part of world history; the books are divided into roughly four equal parts among the world, Europe, the Balkans and Macedonia. The Ottoman period is covered in the 6th grade in the unit ‘The Balkans and Macedonia in the Ottoman Empire’. The books of the minorities studying in Albanian and Turkish also have a separate section devoted to the history of their own mother countries. As Jordanovski reports, the books write that the ‘long Ottoman domination’ began with the fall of the small feudal states in the 14th century and of Salonica in 1430. The abolition of the Archbishopric of Ohrid in 1767 is described as the most important event of the Ottoman period, and it is attributed to the insistence of the Patriarchate of Istanbul and to the corrupt Ottoman administration. The two heroes of the early period are King Marko and Karposh, leader of the uprising of 1689; Karposh's execution on the Stone Bridge in Skopje is related in detail. The subheading of one unit is ‘The terror of the Kircalis and the Albanian marauding bands’. In high-school books Islam is defined as an ‘aggressive’ religion that preaches holy war against the infidels. The only trace of the Ottoman legacy that can be counted as positive is the ‘oriental appearance’ that Muslim architecture gave to Macedonian towns.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — In 2002 Jordanovski noted that the books did not foster lasting negative stereotypes of other Balkan peoples, and that Ottoman oppression too was described not as continuous terror but as confined to periods of crisis and uprising. But because there is no everyday life in the books, what remains is a series of hardships, uprisings and the atrocities that followed. Heike Karge of the Georg Eckert Institute writes in the same period that, compared with the 1992 edition, the new editions removed the sections devoted to common European developments and concentrated attention on the lands inhabited by ethnic Macedonians. The chapter on Macedonia in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek and published in 2018 draws a similar picture: the Ottomans are an ‘occupying’ power, while the conquest of Istanbul is described quite neutrally. Macedonia is presented as the first Balkan nation to recognise the Ottoman danger and begin the struggle against it. The devshirme is referred to as the ‘blood tax’, and quotations are given from a novel written on this subject; the settlement of Yörüks brought from Anatolia is described as ‘colonisation’. Mustafa Kemal's service in Macedonia, however, is mentioned in positive terms.", "fbfda189"], "kisa": ["In the textbooks of North Macedonia the Ottoman period is a long foreign domination that begins with the fall of Salonica in 1430. The books describe the period through uprisings and liberation movements; the Ottoman state appears as a great negative force standing in the way of ‘legitimate national aspirations’. According to a Macedonian historian, the books do not paint a picture of continuous terror, but because they do not describe everyday life, what remains is a series of disasters and uprisings.", "d3479ce9"]},
"bakis-misir":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Egyptian eyes: the cause of backwardness", "46f3c189"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — Ahmed Emin Osmanoğlu compared the social studies textbooks taught in Turkey and Egypt in the 2007–2008 school year. He found no positive statement at all about the Ottomans in the Egyptian books, and wrote that the Ottoman category was treated in an entirely negative way. One book describes the period as follows: the Ottomans isolated Egypt from the outside world, Egypt's condition declined, taxes rose, industry, agriculture and trade declined, security broke down, education was neglected, and superstitions spread. While Europe was taking great strides in civilisation, epidemics in Egypt were carrying people off. It is also written that Ottoman soldiers lived by plunder and destruction. According to an earlier study cited by Osmanoğlu (Yılmaz and Osmanoğlu 2009), Egyptian books present Ottoman rule as the sole cause of the country's social, political, economic, religious and military decline.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — The books tell the story of the Ottoman period as a two-sided tale of the people versus an oppressive government. This story is linked to a revolution scene in which the people rally around Ahmed Urabi, told through a teacher–student dialogue. The books also deal with the discrimination suffered by Egyptians in the Ottoman army because of their nationality. According to the chapter on Egypt in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, Ottoman history is told in Egyptian books in at least as much detail as in Turkish books; the Tulunids, however, are remembered with a positive image. The negative narrative is gathered under headings such as ‘the untrustworthy Turks’ and ‘the cause of the negative developments’. The occupation of Egypt by Western states is also attributed to bad Ottoman policy. The negative developments in the time of Mehmed Ali Pasha and the khedives are treated in detail.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — In the 2007–2008 book examined by Osmanoğlu, the event of 1517 appeared as ‘conquest’ in the heading and as ‘attack’ in the text. According to the study by Kurşun (2011) cited by Osmanoğlu, in the 2010–2011 edition the heading was also changed to ‘attack’, bringing the two parts into line. According to Osmanoğlu, there was no significant difference in the approach to the Ottomans in the following years. According to Zehra Gökdemir's thesis of 2010, in Egyptian books the history of the Pharaohs is taught every year from the fourth grade to the end of high school; the Mamluks are also criticised in harsh language along with the Ottomans.", "c33afe01"], "kisa": ["In Egypt's social studies textbooks the Ottoman period is described in an entirely negative way: Egypt was isolated from the world, taxes rose, and agriculture, industry and education declined. The same books refer to the conquest of 1517 as ‘conquest’ in the heading and ‘attack’ in the text; in later editions the heading was also changed to ‘attack’.", "99e95266"]},
"bakis-polonya":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Polish eyes: not the principal enemy, but a mixed image", "2621dae7"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative — The Polish historian Dariusz Kołodziejczyk writes that what Istanbul meant to the Greeks and Bulgarians, St Petersburg meant to the Poles. Polish national historiography was shaped largely in opposition to Russia and Germany, whereas the Bulgarians built their identity in the struggle against the Ottomans. According to Kołodziejczyk, the idea that Ottoman rule brought backwardness to the Balkans merged with the Marxist concept of the ‘Asiatic mode of production’. In the communist period this view was also adopted in the official historiographies of countries outside the Balkans, among them the Soviet Union, Hungary and Poland. Kołodziejczyk also draws a parallel: in the Balkans it is debated whether the brigands were conscious national leaders or ordinary bandits; Polish and Ukrainian historians debate the same question about the Cossacks.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOK — According to the chapter on Poland in the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek, the first accounts of the Turks in high-school books go back to the Seljuks, while the most detailed account concerns the Ottomans and focuses mainly on political events. Positive and negative statements are intertwined in the books. According to the author of the chapter, the influence of national historiography can be seen on matters that directly concern Poland, and quite negative expressions are used about the Turks. On the other hand, an effort is made to introduce students to the Ottoman state and its characteristics. Viewed as a whole, the narrative is Eurocentric.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS — In 2006 Kołodziejczyk wrote that European unification was a strong incentive to question the myths in national memories. He likens Norman Davies's ‘revisionist’ contribution to Polish historiography to Machiel Kiel's contribution in Bulgaria. No study measuring how the Ottoman narrative in Polish textbooks has changed over time could be accessed.", "b4dddc9e"], "kisa": ["Poland's national historiography took shape in opposition not to the Ottomans but to Russia and Germany. For this reason the image of the Ottomans in Polish textbooks is not as one-sided as in Balkan books. Positive and negative statements are intertwined, but on matters that directly concern Poland the harsh language of national historiography comes to the fore.", "250b6f15"]},
"bakis-romen":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Romanian eyes: resistance, autonomy and the ‘gate of Christendom’", "7490896c"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: In her 2002 study, the history teacher and researcher Codruţa Matei writes that the main theme in Romanian textbooks is resistance and autonomy. All the books stress that Wallachia and Moldavia, unlike the Balkan countries and Hungary, did not come under direct Ottoman administration but remained vassal states. One 6th-grade book contains a paragraph headed ‘The Romanian Principalities: the Gate of Christendom’. Some authors ask why the Ottomans did not turn the principalities into a pashalik and attribute this to the successful resistance of the Romanians, especially to the policies of Mircea and Stephen the Great. Matei also notes that in the books the Ottomans enter Romanian history as an element ‘from outside’. Yet Dobruja, southern Bessarabia, the Banat and some Danube ports were under direct Ottoman administration for a long time. According to Matei, this image does not come from the textbook alone: stories heard in the family, primary school readers and literary texts also dwell on Romanian heroism and the ‘wicked’ enemy.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: In the 6th grade, the organisation of the Ottoman state and army and the conquests are taught. Kosovo (1389), Nicopolis (1396), Ankara (1402), the conquest of Istanbul (1453), the siege of Vienna (1529) and Lepanto (1571) are mostly covered in factual, uncontroversial language. The books dwell insistently on the battles the Romanians won: Vaslui (1475) and Călugăreni (1595). One book proudly writes that the few defeats of Mehmed II were at the hands of the Romanian princes. According to Matei, what appears to have been forgotten is that in the campaign of 1462 the sultan won a political victory by replacing Vlad the Impaler with his brother Radu, and that in 1476 the only pitched battle, at Războieni (Akdere), was won by the Ottomans. In the 10th grade, the siege of Vienna of 1683 is presented as the beginning of decline, and the treaties from Karlowitz (1699) to Bucharest (1812) are covered. In most books the internal structure of the Ottoman state remains superficial, and the words ‘Ottoman’ and ‘Turk’ are used interchangeably. Matei writes that in the essays written by her pupils the image of ‘the Ottomans as those who conquer, destroy and oppress’ is dominant, whereas the same pupils' drawings mostly show scenes of daily life and the palace.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: Mirela-Luminiţa Murgescu describes textbook production after 1989 in three stages. First, the sections relating to the Communist Party were removed from the old books, and in 1991-92 a new series on Romanian history was published. After 1995 came ‘alternative’ books, co-financed by the World Bank and selected through a process of review and tendering; in lower secondary school no more than three books could be approved for each grade. According to Murgescu, some authors and publishers tried to avoid outright stereotypes and negative images, but not all did. Matei's study also concerns these new books: Ottoman events within world history were mostly described factually, but the one-sidedness in the accounts of the Romanian-Ottoman wars persisted.", "2c95bfda"], "kisa": ["Romanian textbooks describe the relationship with the Ottomans from a different standpoint from that of their Balkan neighbours: Wallachia and Moldavia never became an Ottoman pashalik but lived on as vassal states. The books attribute this to the successful resistance of the Romanians and give particular prominence to Romanian victories. The Ottomans, meanwhile, stand in Romanian history as an element ‘from outside’.", "99ea259b"]},
"bakis-rus":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Russian eyes: from ‘dangerous enemy’ to ‘instrument of the West’", "70d724a6"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: Evren Balta and Süheyla Demir examined Russian Federation history textbooks dated 2011. According to them, in these books Russian identity is built essentially on opposition to the West. The Ottomans/Turkey are not an independent actor in this narrative; they are a ‘tool’ that the West sets in motion and supports for its own interest. For the early period, the expressions most often used for the Ottomans are ‘warlike state’ and ‘dangerous enemy’. One 10th-grade book describes the Ottoman turn towards the Balkans with the words ‘Osman's successors stretched out their greedy hands to the Balkans’. The Ottomans are depicted as a power that carried out plundering raids and killed and enslaved Christians. The books use the names ‘Ottoman Turks’ and ‘Turks’ interchangeably; according to the authors, this is connected with a view that regards present-day Turkey as the direct continuation of the Ottoman Empire.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: The Turks appear mostly in the sections describing the Russo-Turkish wars. According to Balta and Demir, the 18th century is a turning point in the narrative: the Ottoman Empire is now a state that has lost its former strength and needs the support of the European states, while Russia goes from victory to victory. The Crimean War (1853-56) is told in terms of Russia's mission to protect the Orthodox in the Holy Land and the Balkans. The alliance between the West and the Ottomans and Russia's military inadequacy are presented as the most important causes of the defeat. The Russia section of the 38-country study edited by Ahmet Şimşek also notes positive elements: the contribution of Turkish scholars to science, and a portrait of an Ottoman state that treated its subjects justly in its founding period. The same section also gives negative examples: the account that the Hungarians were ‘massacred’ at Mohács, the expression ‘mass annihilation’ in connection with the Armenian question, and negative images considered unsuited to the pupils' age.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: Nigâr Maharramova Cengiz and Ahmet Şimşek examined textbooks of the Soviet period (1917-1991). In these books the Balkan peoples live in ‘Turkish captivity’; ‘backward feudal Turkey’ hinders their economic development and tries to destroy their national cultures. The Balkan uprisings are described as just liberation movements and the brigands as heroes; the Ottomans are called ‘occupiers’. The Soviet books portray Russia and the Russian revolutionary democrats as supporters of the oppressed Slav peoples. In the post-Soviet books examined by Balta and Demir, however, the emphasis has changed: it is the West, particularly Britain, rather than the Ottomans, that stands out as the main rival.", "ec3abfaa"], "kisa": ["In current Russian textbooks, the Ottomans in the early period are a ‘warlike state’ and a ‘dangerous enemy’. After the 18th century, however, they are described as a weakening state dependent on the West, against which Russia wins victories. According to the studies, it is not the Ottomans but the West that stands at the centre of this narrative: the Ottomans mostly appear as an instrument used by the West against Russia.", "c127522d"]},
"bakis-sirp":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Serbian eyes: textbooks and the ‘five-hundred-year yoke’", "f8bd166a"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: According to Miloš Todorović of the University of Belgrade, Serbian collective memory sees the Ottoman period in its entirety as a ‘dark age’ and a ‘five-hundred-year Turkish yoke’; the Ottomans are colonisers, oppressors and the ‘other’. Todorović says that this concept comes not from historical research but from a collective memory that regards the Ottomans as the enemy. The duration itself is also disputed: Kosovo (1389) or the fall of Smederevo (1459) is given as the starting point, and 1804, 1830, 1878 or 1912 as the end; if 1459-1830 is taken, the period does not reach 400 years. The narrative has two powerful symbols: the Battle of Kosovo and the ‘blood tax’, called ‘danak u krvi’ in Serbian, that is, the devshirme (child levy). In collective memory the devshirme lives on as a centuries-long abduction of children.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: According to Todorović, the formula of the ‘five-hundred-year yoke’ entered school textbooks in the 20th century and taught generations the idea that ‘they enslaved us and held us back’. Ahmet Alibašić examined the Serbian-language primary school books (editions of 2001-2005) used in Republika Srpska in Bosnia-Herzegovina. The 7th-grade book devotes three full pages to Kosovo and sends the pupil to the supplementary reader for four more pages. About two thirds of the text is devoted to the Kosovo legend, and Serbia is described as a bulwark protecting Christendom against the Turks. The 8th-grade book gives 25 pages to the uprisings in Serbia. The relationship between the Ottomans and their subjects is described with the words ‘enslavement’, ‘oppression’ and ‘exploitation’; one book calls this period ‘the worst of times’. Alibašić particularly criticises the repeated accounts of Serbs being impaled; he also points out that Bosnian Muslims too are mostly referred to in the books as ‘Islamised Serbs’. According to the same study, the 8th-grade book takes a positive view of the 19th-century Ottoman reforms, but explains that these reforms failed because of the resistance of the local aghas.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: The books of the socialist Yugoslav period (1945-1990) examined by Bakir Ovčina also portrayed the Ottoman period as dark. According to these books, ‘Turkish feudalism’ had halted the development of the Balkan peoples. In his 2024 article, Ovčina writes that Serbian textbooks still present the period as the ‘five-hundred-year yoke’. Change is seen more in academia: in 2011 the Serbian historian Srđan Milošević examined the mythical features of the ‘five-hundred-year Turkish yoke’. In 2021 Todorović too called this formula into question by recalling examples such as Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, who rose in society through the devshirme.", "11ab96c2"], "kisa": ["In the Serbian national narrative, the Ottoman period is remembered as the ‘five-hundred-year Turkish yoke’: a dark interlude that began at Kosovo and ended with uprisings. In textbooks the Kosovo epic, the ‘blood tax’ (devshirme) and the uprising of 1804 occupy a large place. Some Serbian historians now criticise this formula as a ‘myth’, but studies see no marked change in school textbooks.", "cb4213c5"]},
"bakis-yunan":{"baslik": ["The Ottomans through Greek eyes: Tourkokratia and ‘unbroken Hellenism’", "678712b8"], "metin": ["① The main theme of the narrative: According to Yasemin Nuhoğlu Soysal and Vasilia Lilian Antoniou, Greek textbooks construct national history as a cultural community unbroken through time: the ancient Hellenes, Byzantium's ‘Helleno-Christian tradition’ and the modern Greek state. This continuity can be preserved only by leaving out the Ottoman heritage. The taking of Istanbul in 1453 is described as the end of Byzantium's political life and as the moment when Greek culture temporarily came to a halt. The Ottoman period is the ‘dark period’ of Greek history, and the Ottoman Turk is the permanent ‘other’ of the national narrative. According to the books, during this period the Greeks preserved their traditions, remained distinct from ‘those who had enslaved them’ and did not lose their national consciousness. The 8th-grade book describes the Tourkokratia as a period in which, ‘in the darkness of slavery’, the Modern Greek consciousness came together and in 1821 astonished Europe.\n\n② ITS PLACE IN THE TEXTBOOKS: In the 8th-grade book of 1999 examined by Soysal and Antoniou, only two chapters are devoted to the Ottomans: the conquest of Istanbul and the founding of the Ottoman state. This amounts to about three per cent of the book. In the 9th-grade book, one chapter is devoted to life under Ottoman rule and another to the process of liberation. According to the authors, the period is counted as an important part of the ‘history of Greece’, but it is told as though it had nothing to do with the ‘history of the Greeks’. The old 6th-grade book examined by Betül Ayanoğlu was first printed in 1989 and was used for more than twenty years. About two thirds of this book were devoted to the hardships of the ‘enslaved’ Greeks under the ‘Ottoman yoke’ and to the War of Independence.\n\n③ CHANGE OVER THE LAST 30 YEARS: The new 6th-grade book written in 2006 by a team headed by Maria Repousi caused a great controversy and was withdrawn a year later. As Ayanoğlu reports, the criticisms were that the book used language that played down the ‘Ottoman yoke’ and belittled the role of the Patriarchate in the War of Independence. The same article notes that four history books have been withdrawn since 1984. In the new 6th-grade book of 2021, the title of the Ottoman section was changed from ‘Hellenism after the Fall’ to ‘The Greeks under Ottoman and Latin Rule’, and the account was also toned down. According to Ayanoğlu, the similarities nevertheless outnumber the differences: expressions such as Tourkokratia, ‘Hellas under Turkish occupation’ and ‘Turkey's Christian slaves’ persist, and the old patterns of othering live on in the source extracts at the ends of the chapters. In 2002 Soysal and Antoniou had written that, with the Greek-Turkish rapprochement, efforts had begun to rid the books of degrading portrayals.", "4658c20a"], "kisa": ["Greek textbooks tell national history as an unbroken line stretching from Ancient Greece to Byzantium and from there to the modern state. In this line, the Ottoman period (Tourkokratia, ‘Turkish rule’) is ‘captivity’ and a ‘dark period’. In 2006 a 6th-grade book that softened this language was withdrawn within a year following protests; the language of the book that replaced it in 2021 is more measured, but the basic stereotypes persist.", "b0573a9a"]},
"dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi":{"baslik": ["Mustafa IV: The Historians' Common Verdict and the TDV Encyclopaedia's Objection", "c0acc696"], "metin": ["One question that comes to mind was this: is Mustafa IV a figure whom historians like, or has he drawn their thunderbolts upon himself? The answer is both: for a long time he was condemned almost unanimously, while in recent literature this condemnation has been openly challenged. I give both sides with their references.\n\nTHE TRADITIONAL verdict — harsh epithets. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` enumerates one by one the descriptions used by “almost all historians in general” and gives their sources: “weak in judgment and intelligence, unaware of the requirements of the age, so greedy for the throne and so merciless as to collaborate with those preparing a revolt that would endanger the survival of the state” (M. Cavid Baysun, İslâm Ansiklopedisi, VIII, 713); and “a partisan of the old order, feeble-minded, ignorant, greedy, short-sighted, a troublemaker, thoughtless, naive, unaware of the general condition of the state, a fool, one who did whatever the rebels wanted, one who thought of nothing but reigning” (Çelebizâde Âsım, Târih, II, 65; Mustafa Nûri Paşa, IV, 60; Ahmed Cevdet Paşa, Târih, VIII, 185; Mufassal Osmanlı Tarihi, V; Enver Ziya Karal, V, 84; İsmail Hami Danişmend, Kronoloji, IV, 88). The weight of this list matters: it is a chain whose links are not independent of one another and which rests largely on the same contemporary chronicles.\n\n🟢 the opposing view — the TDV article itself. Kemal Beydilli, the author of the article, says that it is “clear” that these epithets “do not accord with reality” and regards the judgment as “a matter in need of correction”. His grounds are these: ① Mustafa IV was deposed before he was given the opportunity to take any action that would show his real thinking about innovations — so no definite answer can be given to the question of whether he would have followed in his uncle's footsteps or taken a reformist path like his successor Mahmud II. ② The harsh measures he took against immorality in the city, without regard to whether the offenders were Janissaries. ③ His mentioning certain statesmen of his uncle's period, saying that if they were alive he could appoint one of them grand vizier at once, and his lamenting that no capable men were left around him. ④ His considering stationing trained soldiers in the Artillery Barracks at Taksim. ⑤ His taking a close interest, during his short reign, in the condition of the Engineering School (mühendishane), attempting to reorganize it and having a new, detailed code of regulations drawn up. ⑥ Relying on Zinkeisen (VII, 552-553): that in his reign the idea of reviving the Nizam-i Cedid was not entirely abandoned, that the sultan supported it covertly, and even that he charged Süleyman Agha, who had served as an instructor to the drilled troops at Levend Farm, with this matter.\n\n⚖️ the same article also carries the other pan of the scales. Beydilli also writes that Mustafa IV had belonged to the opposition against Selim since his years as a prince, and that those close to him, including his sister Esma Sultan, worked among the Janissaries for his accession. And there is one indisputable fact: it was he who on 28 July 1808 had the palace gates shut and ordered the killing of his cousin Selim III and his brother Mahmud; Selim was murdered, and Mahmud escaped wounded. It is also related that Mustafa attacked Mahmud in person (Mustafa Necib, Târihçe, p. 92) — this is an anecdotal report, whereas the order is not.\n\n📌 to the reader: the two sides are answering not about two different people but two different questions about the same person. On the question “What did he do?” the scale is heavily weighted, and the TDV article does not lighten it either. On the question “What would he have done?”, however, the sources are not sufficient, and the fact that one historian says openly, against all his colleagues, that “this judgment is in need of correction” shows that the second question is still open. An answer given without knowing which question you are asking does justice to neither side.", "4d70ed72"]},
"hukum-alani-mesafe":{"baslik": ["How Far Can a Capital Rule? Post Stations, Day Marches and the Campaign Season", "072f6104"], "metin": ["How far away can a capital really rule over territory? The Ottomans answered this question not in kilometers but in hours — and the records they left behind can be measured today.\n\n■ THE MENZIL: THE SPEED OF NEWS\nThe empire had set up halting points called menzil (post stations) along its roads. These were not evenly spaced; the source says so plainly: menzils were established at distances of “from three hours up to twenty-eight hours”, “according to geographical conditions, security and the places where the needs of the post station could be supplied”. In other words, from the outset the measure was not a flat distance but the difficulty of the terrain.\n\n■ HOW MANY KILOMETERS IS AN “HOUR”?\nWe have the daily register of the Uyvar campaign of 1663: each of the sixty-four halts the army passed from Davutpaşa to Buda is recorded with its hours. When measured with modern coordinates, all seven separate stages give the same band: one Ottoman hour is 4.3 to 5.4 kilometers as the crow flies. Three independent sources converge on the same figure.\n\n■ THE SAME WORD, TWO DIFFERENT SCALES\nAnd here is a distinction that escapes most readers. A KONAK is one day's march of the army: 19 kilometers on average. A MENZIL, by contrast, is the interval between the courier's stations: 85 kilometers on average. There is a four-and-a-half-fold difference between them, and the reason is simple — at the menzil the courier CHANGES HORSES and rides on; the army walks and encamps. These are two different speeds on the same road network. The place an order reaches and the place an army arrives are not the same.\n\n■ THE BOUNDARY DRAWN BY LAW\nWhat is interesting is that Islamic law, too, gave an answer to the same question. The distance required for a person to count as a “traveler” and be allowed to shorten his prayers has been estimated in all four schools of law at between 83 and 99 kilometers. In other words, in law, anything beyond this is already SOMEWHERE ELSE. This figure also agrees with the boundaries of the historical kazas (judicial districts under a qadi).\n\n■ NESTED RINGS\nWhen all this is put together, a nested order emerges: a fortress or village could truly rule up to about 40 kilometers around it, a kaza center up to 90 kilometers, a sanjak center up to 150 kilometers, and a provincial (eyalet) center up to 270 kilometers. Each tier is roughly the square root of three times the one before it — the very pattern that geographers describe in central place theory.\n\n■ AND THE REAL ANSWER: VIENNA WAS NOT TOO FAR\nThe campaign of 1663 set out from Davutpaşa on 18 March and reached Buda on 17 July: 121 days. But within those 121 days there had been only 258 hours of marching. That is, almost the whole campaign had been spent not in marching but in encamping, supplying and waiting. The army's net progress was 8.8 kilometers a day.\n\nThe campaign season — the period between the melting of the snow and its return — was roughly 240 days, and the outward march, the fighting and the return HAD TO FIT into that time. By this measure:\n\n   Going to Belgrade and back takes 184 days — comfortable.\n   Going to Buda and back takes 243 days — the whole season; no days are left for fighting.\n   Going to Vienna and back takes 290 days — it does NOT FIT into the season.\n\nThis is the answer to why the empire's northwestern frontier stopped exactly there. Vienna was not too far; it was too LATE. What drew the frontier was not a distance but a CALENDAR — and the same reckoning also places Baghdad, Algiers and Mecca among the places that could not be fitted into a single campaign. Those lands were administered by other methods, through governors, garrison corps and vassal principalities.\n\n■ THE LIMITS OF THE MEASUREMENT\nThe daily speed used here is derived from the register of a single campaign; it is not an exact figure but an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE. Even if it varied by thirty percent, Vienna would still fall outside the season — this does not change the conclusion, but one should not ascribe precision to the number.", "0770eac2"], "kisa": ["Vienna was not too far — it was too late. An empire's frontier was drawn not by distance but by the calendar.", "6d8e95c7"]},
"kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti":{"sebep.b": ["The incitement claiming that the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts were to be brought into the Nizam-i Cedid (New Order) system and made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms; the killing of Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki", "cf38e5a2"], "sonuc.b": ["Selim III's abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions and practices, his deposition and the accession of Mustafa IV", "4e31054c"], "bag": ["The visible face of the revolt was Kabakçı Mustafa, a Janissary from Kastamonu; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that the “real architects behind the scenes” of the uprising were Köse Musa Pasha, the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam), and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Mehmed Efendi.", "8f1c1945"], "metin": ["The setting. The Nizam-i Cedid had been launched after the Treaty of Jassy of 1792 and had carried out important reforms, but from 1805 onward it began to be shaken by reactions and acts of protest. The Janissaries and members of the ulema who were dissatisfied with its measures found an opportunity to widen the opposition front by taking a common stand. The attempt to apply the Nizam-i Cedid in Rumelia as well came to nothing because of the resistance of the ayan (provincial notables) and armed clashes (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806) — the TDV article `selim-iii` regards this retreat as the turning point of Selim III's reign. The closure of the pilgrimage routes owing to the Wahhabi revolts and the arrival of the British fleet off Istanbul in February 1807 dealt a heavy blow to the sultan's authority; the fleet's blockade caused scarcity and high prices in the city, bringing the accumulated anger to the point of explosion.\n\nTHE SPARK. Because of the war with Russia, the army had left Istanbul on 12 April 1807. When the forts on the Bosphorus had to be reinforced against the Russian threat, the intention of bringing the yamaks there into the Nizam-i Cedid system became an opportunity for Köse Musa Pasha's covert agitation. The incitement to the effect that the yamaks would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms, and that Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, had been assigned to this task, resulted in the killing of these two men — this is the starting point of the revolt (25 May 1807).\n\nWHY it could not be suppressed. When the uprising became known in Istanbul, Köse Musa Pasha presented it as a mere outburst of indignation; the possibility of intervention by the drilled troops at Levent Farm and the initiative of the chief of the artillery were likewise eliminated by his schemes. Selim III was being given misleading information. The figure the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam underlines is this: the sultan, who failed to show the foresight to use the Nizam-i Cedid soldiers in Istanbul, who numbered more than 10,000, first sacrificed the officials demanded of him and then agreed to the abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid. Even this did not end the revolt; he himself was deposed, and on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807 Mustafa IV was placed on the throne.\n\nTHE AFTERMATH. On 31 May 1807 a “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (a sharia court deed) was drawn up: in return for the soldiers' undertaking not to interfere in affairs of state from then on, they would not be called to account for the revolt. The deed did not hold. After the revolt Kabakçı Mustafa obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne he was killed by Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar, sent by Alemdar Mustafa Pasha. The verdict of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is severe: with this revolt the era of renewal was closed until 1826, and the state's “great opportunity to reform itself in a way that would keep pace with the developments of the age” was thwarted.", "58445285"]},
"kimdir-dorduncu-mustafa":{"baslik": ["Who Was Mustafa IV? A Reign That Lasted Fourteen Months", "b59dbb07"], "metin": ["Birth and years as a prince. He was born on 26 Sha'ban 1193 / 8 September 1779. His father was Abdülhamid I, his mother Ayşe Sineperver Sultan. He was brought up in the palace with a classical education; together with his brother Mahmud he accompanied his father on his tours of inspection in the city. Because his cousin Selim III was childless, he received affection from him and led a comfortable life as a prince.\n\nACCESSION. When Selim III, having put an end to the Nizam-i Cedid, was deposed at the close of the Kabakçı Mustafa revolt, he became sultan on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807. He ratified the “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (sharia court deed) drawn up on 31 May 1807, stating that the rebels would not be called to account, and gave an assurance under oath. The document was of no use: those who had brought about the change of reign could not be prevented from interfering in affairs of state and forcing through whatever they wanted.\n\nTHE REIGN. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes these fourteen months as “such great turmoil, disorder and disintegration of the structure of the state that it would soon lead to regret over Selim's deposition”. The war with Russia that had begun in 1806 was continuing; the events in Istanbul spread to the front as well, the Janissaries revolted, the grand vizier was chased away, and the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, was killed by his own comrades. On 24 August 1807 a one-year armistice was signed at Slobozia, but this period was spent not in preparing for war but in settling internal scores. When, upon the resignation of the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha, Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, a mortal enemy of Selim III, was appointed in his place (November 1807), the Rusçuk Committee (Rusçuk yaranı), who wanted to put Selim back on the throne, gathered around Alemdar Mustafa Pasha.\n\nTHE FALL. Alemdar came to Istanbul with an army on 25 Jumada al-Ula 1223 / 19 July 1808; order was restored in the city, Kabakçı Mustafa was killed on his orders, and the Shaykh al-Islam Ataullah Mehmed Efendi was dismissed. When Alemdar stormed the Sublime Porte and headed for the palace to put Selim on the throne, Mustafa IV had the palace gates shut and ordered that his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud be killed. Selim III was murdered; Mahmud escaped wounded and, as the last surviving member of the dynasty, was placed on the throne on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 / 28 July 1808. It is related that Mustafa IV greeted the coup with the words “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has enthroned Mahmud?”, and that the sultan's imam Ahmed Kamil Efendi calmed him and saved him from Alemdar's wrath.\n\nDEATH. After being confined to his apartment in the palace, he did not refrain from sending word outside so that he might be restored to the throne. When about three months later Alemdar was attacked and killed and the rebels wished to march on the palace, he was strangled with the sash from his own waist on the order of his brother Mahmud II (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808). His body was buried at Bahçekapı in the mausoleum of his father Abdülhamid I. Rumors spread among the rebels that the coffin was empty.\n\nFor the point on which historians differ regarding his personality, see the card `dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi`.", "d41175c7"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"sebep-sonuc-amasya-1555":{"oneri": ["The Shah never gave battle", "670cbc9a"], "sebep.b": ["During the Nakhchivan Campaign, Shah Tahmasp's avoidance of a pitched battle and devastation of the army's route, Suleiman the Lawgiver's threat to Ardabil, held sacred by the Safavids, by wintering in Amasya, and the shah's request for a truce", "3fa28be9"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Amasya: the first treaty between the two states, in which the Safavids accepted Ottoman rule in Iraq and Eastern Anatolia", "dad38f4f"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this peace as the first treaty concluded between the Ottoman State and Iran. Although it had the appearance of a friendly exchange of letters, it made the other side accept Ottoman rule along the line stretching from Baghdad to Kars and kept the eastern frontier calm for a quarter of a century.", "338561c7"], "metin": ["The raids that the Safavids made into Eastern Anatolia after the Tabriz Campaign sent Suleiman the Lawgiver on campaign once again. Knowing that he could not withstand the great army, Tahmasp did not come out to face it; he burned the places the army was to pass through, had the water poisoned and moved the population away. Because of supply shortages and the approaching winter, the Ottoman army withdrew from Nakhchivan. While Suleiman the Lawgiver was in Erzurum, the shah's request for a truce was accepted (26 September 1554). The sultan's wintering in Amasya and his threat to destroy Ardabil alarmed Tahmasp: his envoy Ferruhzad Bey arrived in Amasya on 17 May 1555, was received in the divan on 22 May and presented the letter seeking peace.", "ac004014"]},
"sebep-sonuc-belgrad-1739":{"oneri": ["The 1739 Treaty of Belgrade — the recovery of lost Belgrade", "62d1d3de"], "sebep.b": ["Austria's defeat in the war of 1736–1739, which Russia and its ally Austria waged against the Ottomans on two fronts, and the operation carried out to retake Belgrade", "9da8e650"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Belgrade: Belgrade and Northern Serbia were recovered; Azov was to be demolished and become an area from which both sides would stay away", "3929618a"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, Austria's eastward expansion came to a halt and its frontier in the Balkans changed little over the following 140 years or so; Russia, for its part, was kept away from the Black Sea until the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 1774.", "951e406e"], "metin": ["The peace opened a long period of calm between the two empires. When Austria was hard pressed in the reign of Maria Theresa by the wars of succession and the wars with Prussia, the Ottomans chose not to take advantage of this weakness; they held aloof from the insistent offers of alliance made by King Frederick II of Prussia. Russia, having waged the war far more effectively than its ally, began to emerge as a serious rival to the Habsburgs in the Balkans. The price of the return to Belgrade was also heavy: according to the survey of 1741, 721 of the 1,546 villages in Northern Serbia had been abandoned. Belgrade Fortress was rebuilt and made the largest fortress in the country.", "76b0567f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-berlin-1878":{"oneri": ["Who undid San Stefano, and why did the Ottomans again leave the table with losses?", "ac172c9e"], "sebep.b": ["The objections of Britain, Austria and the Balkan states to the Greater Bulgaria that Russia had created at San Stefano", "d520e887"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Berlin: the division of Bulgaria into three, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania, Bosnia-Herzegovina handed over to Austrian occupation, and Kars, Ardahan and Batum ceded to Russia", "c00b2d63"], "bag": ["Significance: San Stefano was a treaty ending a war that concerned only two states; the Congress of Berlin made the question the common concern of Europe's great powers. According to TDV, the principle of Ottoman territorial integrity laid down in the 1856 Treaty of Paris was openly violated, and territory was given even to Iran and Greece, which had had nothing to do with the war. The congress, which was expected to secure a general peace, did not produce the desired result and became the source of later disputes.", "e1a4c38d"], "metin": ["San Stefano settled the Eastern Question solely in Russia's interest, without taking the European balance of power into account. Britain could not accept Russia's gaining so much influence over Ottoman territory, nor could Austria accept the birth of a large Bulgaria in the midst of its own Balkan designs. The new Balkan states were also discontented: Serbia wanted Bosnia-Herzegovina and part of Macedonia, and Romania, although it was to receive Dobruja, did not want to cede to Russia Bessarabia, where Romanians lived. When no European state would consent to Russia's reaching down to the Aegean and the Adriatic, Russia accepted the congress it had at first not wanted. Before the congress, Britain asked for Cyprus as a base in the Eastern Mediterranean against Russia and made it known that it would take the island by force if it were not given; the Sublime Porte, hoping for British support at the congress, handed over the island on 4 June 1878, and a supplementary convention dated 1 July stated that if Russia returned the places it had occupied in the east, Britain would withdraw, and that ownership belonged to the Ottomans. The congress met on 13 June under the presidency of Bismarck. The Ottoman delegation was headed by Field Marshal Mehmed Ali Pasha; with him were the Minister of Public Works, Karatodori Pasha, and the Ottoman ambassador in Berlin, Sadullah Bey. The Ottoman delegation expected the terms of San Stefano to be softened, but each state protected its own interest, and even the British representatives, from whom help was expected, expressed views against the Ottomans. The sixty-four-article treaty signed a month later also shaped the following years through articles not included on the provisions card: the application of the 1868 regulation in Crete, with Europe to be kept informed; the cession of Kotur to Iran; the closure of the Danube to warships while it remained open to merchant ships; reforms in the places inhabited by Armenians; and the retention of the status of the Straits laid down in the London treaty of 1841 and the Paris treaty of 1856.", "622cb3da"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bolge0073-misir-sudan-siyaseti":{"sebep.b": ["Mehmed Ali of Kavala's decision to create a new army on the European model, and that army's need for men and natural resources", "caae44b6"], "sonuc.b": ["Sudan's passing under Egyptian administration with the surrender of Sennar (the Funj), and the beginning of the sixty-one-year \"Turkiyya period\"", "01363b09"], "bag": ["Significance: The Sudan campaign was the first step in Egypt's transformation from an Ottoman province into a power pursuing its own policy of expansion. Sudan's present-day borders are likewise the borders drawn by the administration that began with this campaign.", "a126072b"], "metin": ["① The four causes listed by the source. The article on Sudan in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam lists one by one Mehmed Ali's aims in 1821: to gain possession of the gold and emerald deposits, to make greater use of the sources of the Nile, to employ Sudanese soldiers in the army, and to neutralise the last Mamluk remnants who had taken refuge in the region. For this purpose the campaign was directed at the Dongola and Funj regions. So, according to the source, the answer to the question \"soldiers, gold or trade route?\" is \"all of them\"; but there is an order among them. ② The real driving force: the new army. The TDV article on Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala links the cause directly to the army. After the Hejaz campaign, the pasha turned his eyes to Sudan in order to obtain the human and natural resources that would form the basis of the new army he was thinking of creating to preserve his authority and continue his expansionist policy. The campaign organised in 1820 lasted two years. According to the same article, Mehmed Ali, having seen the inadequacy of his army in the Hejaz and Sudan campaigns, decided to create a new army called the Nizam-i Cedid and entrusted this task to the French colonel Joseph Sève (Süleyman Pasha). In other words, Sudan had been conceived both as a reason for the new army and as its first source. ③ And this calculation did not work out. This is the most striking sentence in the source: \"Although the necessary manpower was initially obtained from Sudan, it was not sufficient, and Egypt and Syria thus came to the fore\" (TDV). When the idea of an army formed of Sudanese soldiers failed, Mehmed Ali was obliged to take his own Egyptian peasants under arms; compulsory military service became the greatest source of discontent in the following years. ④ The gold and the trade route really did exist. The source lists the foreign trade of the Funj Sultanate item by item: the main trade goods were slaves, ivory, tamarind, butter, cream, musk and gold ingots; in Cairo, Asyut, Esna, Daraw, Qus, Aswan and Naqada there were commercial agencies that organised the coming and going of the caravans, and Egyptian merchants bought large numbers of slaves from here in particular (TDV, Funj). Sennar was the nodal point of this trade, and Egypt seized this node directly. ⑤ The Mamluk question. At a banquet he gave in Cairo on 1 March 1811, Mehmed Ali had had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed; the survivors had fled south. According to the TDV article on the Funj, after the conquest the Mamluks of Sennar fled further south, left no trace and vanished from the stage of history. In this respect the campaign was as much the completion of an elimination as a territorial gain. ⑥ How long it lasted. From 1821 to 1882 Sudan remained a country governed by Mehmed Ali and his sons with the fullest powers in the name of the Ottoman State; in Sudanese history this period is called the \"Turkiyya\" (TDV). In 1841 Sudan was formally entrusted to Mehmed Ali by a firman, and the pasha was counted as governor of the provinces of Nubia, Sennar, Kordofan and Darfur. The period was completely brought down in 1885 by the movement that Muhammad Ahmad al-Mahdi launched in 1881. ⑦ So did Abyssinia, Djibouti, Eritrea and Somalia ever come onto the agenda? They did — but not in 1821, and none of them as the target of a campaign. For the time of Mehmed Ali, the source mentions no military plan for these regions; the Red Sea coast passed to Egypt by the sultan's firman. Massawa in present-day Eritrea and Sawakin on the Sudanese coast were given to Mehmed Ali by Sultan Abdülmecid in 1846 as a sâlyâne (a holding in return for a fixed annual payment); on the pasha's death (1849) Massawa returned to the control of the governor of the Hejaz (TDV, Massawa and the Province of Habesh). That is, twenty-five years after Sennar, and by appointment, not by conquest. ⑧ The Horn of Africa was opened a generation later. The real expansion came in the time of his grandson, Khedive Ismail Pasha: by a firman dated 27 May 1866, the district governorships (kaymakamlık) of Sawakin and Massawa were placed under the control of the governorship of Egypt; by another firman dated 1 June 1875, Zeila, on the coast of present-day Somaliland, and its environs were added, and Egypt agreed to send Istanbul 15,000 gold pieces for Zeila (TDV, Zeila). In 1874–1875 Ismail Pasha seized the coastal regions of Somalia in the name of the Ottoman State; his effort to spread into the interior was blocked by Britain, and in 1883 Egyptian troops withdrew from Somalia because of the Mahdist uprising (TDV, Somalia). ⑨ Abyssinia: war, yes; a plan, in 1821, no. According to the TDV article on Ethiopia, in 1872 Khedive Ismail Pasha, also encouraged by the British, wished to annex the sources of the Blue Nile to Egypt, but failed; after 1874 he organised three military campaigns against Ethiopia, which laid claim to Massawa, and in 1876 the fighting stopped when King Yohannes IV chose to come to an agreement. This was half a century after the surrender of Sennar. ⑩ Djibouti: the source does not speak of this. The TDV article on Djibouti mentions no Egyptian venture in the 19th-century history of the region; Obock there was bought by the French from a local chief in 1859 (TDV, Zeila and Somalia). This is an answer in itself: Djibouti never came onto Egypt's agenda at all. ⑪ The short answer. In 1821 Egypt's eyes were on the upper reaches of the Nile: soldiers, gold and the node of the Sennar trade. The African coast of the Red Sea — Massawa, Sawakin, then Zeila and the Somali shore — came into Egypt's hands a generation later and largely by Istanbul's firmans; the reckoning with Abyssinia belongs to the 1870s; Djibouti never came onto the agenda.", "56d31553"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bucas-1672":{"oneri": ["Why did the peace that won Podolia not last even a year?", "40c4c95f"], "sebep.b": ["Mehmed IV's Polish campaign: the capture of Kamianets (Kamaniçe) after a ten-day siege and the pressure put on Lviv (İlbav)", "c48f0660"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Buczacz: Podolia became an Ottoman province (eyalet), and Poland agreed to pay an annual pişkeş (tributary gift)", "74e7b1a2"], "bag": ["Significance: With Kamianets and Podolia, the Ottomans established themselves in a region regarded as the key to the Crimea; Ukraine under Doroshenko became an autonomous entity attached to the Ottomans, and the way to emigration was opened to the Lipka Tatars. But it was precisely this gain that was unacceptable to Poland: the Polish Diet found the treaty too harsh, rejected it and imposed new taxes. From 1673 the war began again and lasted until Karlowitz; the intervening treaties of 1676 and 1678 took Buczacz as their basis. Poland, unable to recover Kamianets by war, regained it only in 1699 through an article added at Karlowitz.", "affa5e12"], "metin": ["When Kamianets fell on 27 August 1672, the Ottoman side began to press Poland towards a lasting peace: on 4 September the Polish king was informed that the campaign would continue unless he accepted that Podolia belonged to the Ottomans, and on 6 September the army marched on Lviv, where the king was staying. On 19 September the king asked for peace; arguing that Lviv lay outside Podolia, he offered money in return for the lifting of the siege. When the army laid siege to the palanka of Buczacz on 3 October, the Polish envoys arrived and on 14–16 October held talks with a delegation headed by the kaymakam Kara Mustafa Pasha. The treaty was signed on 18 October; Fazıl Ahmed Pasha sealed the Turkish text and the Polish envoys the Latin one. At the envoys' request, the palanka of Buczacz, which lay outside Podolia, was returned to Poland, and that same evening a pardon (aman) for the Polish king was proclaimed by firman. However, Poland held on to some of the castles and palankas it had undertaken to return and turned to a hostile policy towards the Ottomans; the Ottoman government decided on a second campaign.", "8a5ad46f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bukres-1812":{"oneri": ["What was saved at the table while Napoleon marched on Moscow?", "bd02bd79"], "sebep.b": ["The breakdown of the Ottoman-Russian alliance formed against the French when the Russians incited the Greeks of the Morea and the islands to revolt, and the war that resumed in 1806", "76aa0d95"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Bucharest: Bessarabia passed to Russia, and the Serbs were granted partial autonomy", "f12f7097"], "bag": ["Significance: With the Prut frontier, Bessarabia entered a period of Russian rule that was to last 106 years. According to the TDV, together with the autonomy granted to the Serbs, a period began in which the Orthodox and Slav peoples were openly incited and supported against Ottoman rule.", "33a756ee"], "metin": ["The autonomy provision was not carried out at once: the revolt of the Serbs, who wanted broader rights, was suppressed in 1813, Belgrade was retaken, and the leader of the revolt, Karađorđe, fled to Austria. But in 1815 a second Serbian uprising broke out under the leadership of Miloš Obrenović; although it too was suppressed the same year, from then on the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be broadened, and the question came back to the table with the Akkerman Convention of 1826.", "4aa66331"]},
"sebep-sonuc-edirne-1829":{"oneri": ["With the Russian army in Edirne, was there any room left to bargain?", "4b1ee7cf"], "sebep.b": ["The intervention of Britain, France and Russia in the Morean revolt of 1821, the sinking of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino and Russia's declaration of war in 1828", "deab6cdf"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Adrianople: the independence of Greece was accepted, and the mouth of the Danube and the Caucasian fortresses passed to Russia", "d1dd5d41"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the Ottomans, unable to halt the Russian advance, could achieve no success in diplomacy either and were forced to accept the terms dictated by the victor; the payment of 11.5 million Hungarian gold pieces that they accepted was heavier than the treasury could bear.", "aeb7bf97"], "metin": ["On 22 August 1829 the Russian army entered Edirne without meeting resistance and also occupied Kırklareli and Lüleburgaz; while Russian ships attacked the Bosphorus, a squadron blockaded the Dardanelles. The Ottoman delegates who went to Edirne through Prussian mediation could not soften the Russian terms, apart from a small change in the commercial indemnity. The burden was later reduced: in 1830 the indemnity was lowered to 8 million Dutch gold pieces, and a further 1 million was written off on condition that the full independence of Greece be accepted; in January 1834 it was reduced to 5 million. The Russian withdrawal, which was tied to the indemnity, took place from Wallachia and Moldavia only in 1834, and from Silistra under the convention of 8 April 1836.", "6c6fcef5"]},
"sebep-sonuc-hunkar-iskelesi-1833":{"oneri": ["Why did a Russian fleet enter the Bosphorus while the Egyptian army was at Kütahya?", "64a31e5a"], "sebep.b": ["The forces of Mehmed Ali Pasha defeating the Ottoman army at Konya and threatening Istanbul; the acceptance of Russian help by the Sublime Porte, which found no support from France and Britain", "1dedc447"], "sonuc.b": ["The defensive alliance of Hünkâr İskelesi and the secret article providing for the closure of the Dardanelles in Russia's favour", "980eeac1"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the secret article turned the Egyptian question into an international question of the Straits; it caused a crisis in Europe, gave rise through Metternich's mediation to the Münchengrätz Agreement (18 September 1833) and, when the eight-year term expired, was replaced by the London Straits Convention of 1841.", "c64e9a2b"], "metin": ["When İbrahim Pasha's army defeated Grand Vizier Mehmed Reşid Pasha at Konya on 21 December 1832 and reached Kütahya on 2 February 1833, both the capital and the dynasty came under threat. While calling on Russia for help, the Sublime Porte also wished to use this invitation as a card to spur France and Britain into action; Russia, however, gave it no opportunity: it anchored its fleet at Büyükdere (20 February) and then landed its ground forces at Beykoz (5 April). Through the Kütahya talks, the provinces of Egypt and all Syria, together with Adana, were left to Mehmed Ali's side (firman of 6 May). The Russian forces still did not withdraw; the alliance proposal brought by Orlov was signed on 8 July as the heavy bill for the help, and two days later the Russian fleet and ground forces left the Bosphorus. The TDV also sees behind the indignation felt in Istanbul at the Russian alliance the propaganda that Mehmed Ali Pasha conducted in Europe.", "f9d73540"]},
"sebep-sonuc-karlofca-1699":{"oneri": ["How did the side that lost the war manage to sit bolt upright at the negotiating table?", "35286fc6"], "sebep.b": ["The sixteen-year war on many fronts that began with the 1683 defeat at Vienna: Venice's advance northward from the Morea, the fall of Azov in 1696 and the defeat at Zenta in 1697", "b6bdcf0e"], "sonuc.b": ["Karlowitz: Hungary and Transylvania ceded to Austria, Podolia to Poland and the Morea to Venice; with Russia, only a truce", "f248d009"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV regards Karlowitz as a turning point in Ottoman history — for the first time the state accepted by treaty a loss of territory on such a scale, the peace was made at the table of mediating powers (England and the Netherlands), and the Ottoman-Austrian equality that had been accepted at Zsitvatorok but could not be put into practice was written anew. Even so, according to TDV the Ottoman delegation showed none of the abjectness of a defeated party: by holding on to Temeşvar and to fortresses in Bosnia such as Bihać, it preserved the possibility of recovering the lost places in the future. Ottoman statesmen saw the treaty more as a truce than as a lasting peace — an outlook that found expression in 1711 at the Pruth and then in the war for the Morea declared on Venice.", "c5337a74"], "metin": ["On the road to peace both sides were weary. On the Ottoman side, the army had withdrawn to the Danube after Zenta; Venice, advancing northward from the Morea, had taken Athens and many fortresses in Dalmatia and Bosnia; and the Russians had captured Azov in 1696. Grand Vizier Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha persuaded Mustafa II, who would consent to peace only once part of the losses had been recovered; his arguments were that the people were being crushed under extraordinary taxes, that villages were emptying, that finding soldiers and munitions had become impossible, that public order had broken down in Anatolia and that there were revolts in Rumelia. On the other side, Austria wanted peace because of the question of the Spanish succession, which was expected to erupt; Venice fell in with it, but Poland and Russia, not having obtained what they wanted, favoured continuing the war. The preliminary protocol signed at Edirne on 27 January 1698 accepted the principle of 'alâ hâlihî' (each side keeps what it holds). The talks that began at Karlowitz on 13 November 1698 ran to thirty-six sessions over seventy-two days in a large tent with four doors. The reisülküttab (chief secretary) Rami Mehmed Efendi studied the texts of earlier treaties and obtained reports from experts; even the Austrian delegate Marsigli wrote that the Christian delegates were at times reduced to a pitiable state. The longest wrangle was with Venice: agreement was reached when Austria declared that if the Ottoman demands were not accepted, Venice would carry on the war alone. Because the Russian delegate did not recognise the protocol, only a two-year truce was made with Russia; peace was signed in Istanbul on 14 July 1700. The terms were ill received in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice. The fact that the second delegate, Mavrokordato, later received a decoration from the emperor gave rise to accusations that he had acted against Ottoman interests; TDV says these accusations must be treated with caution.", "8373db96"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasr-i-sirin-1639":{"oneri": ["How did three days of talks make a frontier for two centuries?", "e2bd586e"], "sebep.b": ["Murad IV's recapture of Baghdad, the withdrawal of the Safavid army to Qasr-e Shirin, and the exhaustion of both sides from the wars fought on the Azerbaijan and Iraq fronts since 1630", "4cf61a2e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin (Zuhab): Iraq remained with the Ottomans and Yerevan with the Safavids", "f48a09af"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it put a definitive end to the Ottoman-Safavid dispute and was taken as the basis in later centuries: the Treaty of Kerden of 1746 renewed it, and the Erzurum treaties of 1823 and 1847 settled frontier questions on its basis.", "81507167"], "metin": ["The peace also brought economic returns. Shah Abbas's transfer of his capital from Tabriz to Isfahan and his founding of New Julfa for the Armenian merchants had shifted the trade routes southward; in the climate of peace, Iranian silk and textiles were carried via Baghdad to Aleppo, and via Mosul to Diyarbekir and the cities of Anatolia. According to the figures reported by the TDV, Iran's silk exports rose by about half between 1640 and 1670. This balanced period lasted until Nader Shah, founder of the Afshar dynasty, came to power in 1736.", "35eec015"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kucuk-kaynarca-1774":{"oneri": ["Why was a peace signed in a besieged army camp misread for a century?", "9811fc1f"], "sebep.b": ["The war of 1768, declared without preparation: the occupation of the Crimea and of Wallachia and Moldavia, the raid at Çeşme, the collapse of the Bucharest talks and, after the rout at Kozluca in 1774, the encirclement of the army headquarters at Shumen (Şumnu)", "757943f7"], "sonuc.b": ["Küçük Kaynarca: the political separation of the Crimean Khanate from the Ottomans, freedom for Russian ships in the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, and an indemnity of 15,000 purses", "f5c01ade"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, this war both laid bare Ottoman weakness and testified to Russia's emergence as a great power. In the official French translation that Russia printed in 1775, the church to be built in Istanbul was rendered not as a 'Greek' (Rum) church but as a 'Russian' one; this falsification gave Russia a claim to a general protectorate over the Ottoman Orthodox and became one of the causes of the Crimean War. The claim was formally ended only by the Reform Edict of 1856 and the Treaty of Paris. On the other hand, Russia's recognition of the caliphal tie with the Crimea is counted as an important gain, even if it did not make up for the loss.", "b9b6b770"], "metin": ["The war was declared on account of Russia's interference in Poland's internal affairs and its attacks on the Crimean frontier — with just cause, but without any preparation. The Russians soon occupied the Crimea and Wallachia and Moldavia and crossed the Danube. In the Ottoman army, bad leadership, indiscipline, the collapsed menzil (post station) system and shortages of provisions heralded a general disintegration. The Russians were not at ease either; the Pugachev revolt that was shaking the country turned them towards peace talks — Frederick the Great had described this war as a 'war of the one-eyed against the blind'. In the talks held at Focșani and Bucharest in 1772-1773, granting independence to the Crimea was considered contrary to Islamic law, and the surrender of fortresses and an indemnity were also rejected amid the sharp reaction of the ulema in Istanbul; the war resumed in March 1773. In the spring of 1774 the Russians inflicted a heavy defeat on the Ottoman forces at Kozluca (on 25 June according to TDV) and besieged the headquarters at Shumen. Because even the interpreters had fled in the rout, Marshal Rumyantsev's peace letter went untranslated for several days. With the camp, reduced to 7-8,000 men, under siege, Rumyantsev insisted on negotiating while the war continued, without declaring a truce. The Ottoman delegates, Ahmed Resmi Efendi and Münib Efendi, held talks at Küçük Kaynarca on 16, 17 and 21 July; when the approval of the gravely ill grand vizier arrived, the treaty was signed on the evening of 21 July 1774. Catherine II found the terms more satisfactory than expected.", "494c3856"]},
"sebep-sonuc-mondros-1918":{"oneri": ["How did an armistice open the door to occupation?", "c3bb9cbb"], "sebep.b": ["The collapse of the Palestine–Syria front, and Bulgaria's signing of a ceasefire and withdrawal from the war, which left Thrace and Istanbul open to attack", "585fe0aa"], "sonuc.b": ["Armistice of Mudros: the disbanding of the army and navy, and the right of occupation given to the Entente powers by Articles 7 and 24", "26993965"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV regards Mudros as the herald of the end of the Ottoman State's six-century history; the occupations that began with the abuse of Articles 7 and 24 gave rise to the War of Independence led by Mustafa Kemal Pasha and to the founding of the Republic of Turkey.", "6091cf80"], "metin": ["The British offensive that began on the Palestine–Syria front on 19 September 1918 routed the Ottoman forces; with Bulgaria's withdrawal on 29 September, the land link with the allies was cut. Two peace offers made by the Ottomans through Spain (5 and 12 October) went unanswered; the government of Talat Pasha, having received a vote of no confidence, was replaced by that of Ahmed İzzet Pasha (14 October), and Admiral Calthorpe was reached through the captive British General Townshend. Trusting in Wilson's principles, the government sent its delegation in the expectation that there would be no interference in the administration and no landing of foreign troops in the country; the text signed, however, dismantled the Ottoman defence system almost entirely. The British occupation of Mosul within a few days on the basis of Article 7 confirmed the suspicions; the Committee of Union and Progress dissolved itself on 1 November, and Enver, Cemal and Talat Pashas secretly left the country.", "3df03dd8"]},
"sebep-sonuc-paris-1856":{"oneri": ["Admission to Europe on paper", "a9a49644"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's demand for an internationally valid right of protection over the Orthodox population in the Ottoman Empire, the dispute over the 'holy places' and the Crimean War, which began in 1853", "e7a6090e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Paris: the Ottomans were accepted as an equal member of the European states system, and the Black Sea was neutralised", "cbf265be"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it ended the superiority that the treaties of Küçük Kaynarca, Akkerman and Adrianople had given Russia, changed the Straits regime of 1841 and was interpreted as having checked Russia's expansion towards the Ottomans, which had been under way since Catherine II. Because of the Reform Edict, it also drew the state into an important constitutional change.", "afbe2766"], "metin": ["The guarantees given soon ceased to work. When France was defeated by Prussia in 1870–1871, Russia announced, before that war had ended, that it would no longer abide by the Black Sea clause; the TDV regards this as the first sign of the great Ottoman-Russian war that was approaching. The guarantee of territorial integrity could not prevent the great dismemberment in the war of 1877–1878. The burden of debt left by the war and the social conflicts that arose in the implementation of the Reform Edict shaped the period that followed. The TDV reports that at the Congress of Berlin (1878) Bismarck rebuked the Ottoman delegates, who were trying to defend their rights, by telling them that European law was not for them — a sign of how far the status won in 1856 had remained on paper.", "71cfb416"]},
"sebep-sonuc-pasarofca-1718":{"oneri": ["The 1718 Treaty of Passarowitz — the Morea stayed, Belgrade was lost", "809d732f"], "sebep.b": ["The desire to avenge Karlowitz: the war declared on Venice; Austria's alliance with Venice in 1716 and its entry into the war; the defeats at Petrovaradin and Belgrade", "bddbeceb"], "sonuc.b": ["Passarowitz: the Morea remained Ottoman; the Banat, Oltenia (Lesser Wallachia), northern Serbia including Belgrade, and northern Bosnia passed to Austria", "c5eb1153"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, the treaty served Austria more than Venice; with its great territorial losses the Ottoman state not only withdrew from Central Europe but also began to cease being a strong state in European eyes. The commercial treaty signed in the same days granted Austrian merchants free trade at a 3% customs duty and the right to maintain consuls and dragomans in all ports; in return the Ottomans too would be able to open consulates (şehbenderlik) in Austrian territory, and a consul (şehbender) was sent to Vienna. Belgrade and northern Serbia were recovered only by the 1739 Treaty of Belgrade.", "c503d80b"], "metin": ["Ottoman statesmen regarded Karlowitz as a truce. After halting Russia at the Pruth, they declared war on Venice, which they claimed had violated the treaty; the Ottoman army and navy had the upper hand in this war. Grand Vizier Damad Ali Pasha hoped Austria would remain neutral, but Prince Eugene favoured war, and on 13 April 1716 the Austrian-Venetian alliance was signed. In Istanbul some statesmen proposed placating Austria by returning a few unimportant islands to Venice; when the grand vizier threatened them with death they fell silent, and war was declared. Ali Pasha fell in battle at Petrovaradin, the Banat and Temeşvar passed to Austria, and in 1717 Belgrade fell as well. The question of peace set two poles against each other at court: at the head of the war party were Numan Pasha and the Köprülü family, and tradesmen who were profiting from high prices also wanted the war to go on; the people, however, were exhausted, and the sultan favoured peace. Charles VI's harsh terms, demanding Bosnia, Serbia and Wallachia, enraged Ahmed III, but through the mediation of the English and Dutch ambassadors it was agreed to negotiate on the basis of the existing situation. In the negotiations that began on 5 June 1718, Austria first made the surrender of Rákóczi and his men a condition; the Ottomans refused, and Austria backed down. In truth neither side was in a condition to fight: Austria was on the brink of a new conflict with Spain. The Ottomans asked for a fifteen-year peace; Austria's proposal of twenty-four years was accepted. After seventy days, the treaties with Austria and Venice were signed on 21 July 1718.", "ef9861d9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-sevr-1920":{"oneri": ["Debated for nineteen months at the partition table, forced on Istanbul for signature, never applied: what killed Sèvres…", "1720b20a"], "sebep.b": ["Paris Peace Conference: the victorious powers began to discuss the future of the Ottoman lands", "d3f8317e"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Sèvres was signed — the Ankara government did not recognise it, and the text could not be applied", "d11a8e42"], "bag": ["Significance: Sèvres was the written form of the solution Europe devised for the 'Eastern Question'. According to TDV, the 1923 Treaty of Lausanne preserved the distribution of the Arab mandates made at Sèvres, while introducing important changes in all matters concerning the new Turkish state.", "d797533f"], "metin": ["The decision-making body of the conference was the Council of Ten, composed of the prime ministers and foreign ministers of Britain, France, Italy, the USA and Japan; it determined the regions to be wholly separated from the Ottomans — Arabia, Syria, Mesopotamia, Palestine, Armenia and, on a British proposal, Kurdistan. The Allies could not agree: France and Italy wanted the secret wartime agreements to be implemented, whereas the USA wanted Ottoman rule to continue in regions with a Turkish majority, in accordance with Wilson's twelfth point. The Greeks, supported by Britain, France and the USA, landed at İzmir on 15 May 1919; this landing led to the rapid organisation of the national resistance in Anatolia. Faced with the demand of Indian Muslims that the caliph remain in Istanbul, the British government on 6 January 1920 rejected the plan to expel the Turks from Europe. On 16 March 1920 Istanbul was officially occupied; on 23 April the Grand National Assembly began its work in Ankara. The text was given its final form at San Remo (18-26 April 1920) and communicated to Tevfik Pasha on 11 May; Tevfik Pasha stated that he could not accept the terms. Lloyd George set the Greek army in motion on 22 June; after Bursa fell on 8 July and Thrace on 20 July, the Council of the Sultanate convened by the sultan on 22 July, in the face of reports that Istanbul would be occupied by the Greeks, voted in favour of signature, with the exception of Lieutenant General of Artillery Rıza Pasha. What followed came quickly: after Sakarya, France made an agreement with the Ankara government on 20 October 1921 and recognised the new state, and even Lloyd George declared on 10 August 1921 that Sèvres had now been torn up. The victory of 30 August 1922 was followed by the Armistice of Mudanya on 11 October, the abolition of the sultanate on 1 November and the Treaty of Lausanne on 24 July 1923.", "a078f4bf"]},
"sebep-sonuc-usi-1912":{"oneri": ["The last Ottoman province in Africa was given up at a table in Switzerland; but the war did not end there, and in the Aegean…", "4fa24612"], "sebep.b": ["Italy's declaration of war on the Ottoman State on the grounds of protecting its citizens in Tripoli", "8d96d53b"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Ouchy: the Ottoman State accepted that it had ceded Tripoli to Italy", "af6dab0c"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of the Ottoman State's last territory on the African continent. The treaty's loose end was tied up at Lausanne: Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which Italy was supposed to evacuate under the Treaty of Ouchy, remained in its hands, and Turkey formally renounced its rights over Tripoli.", "b90736c2"], "metin": ["As the colonial race in Africa gathered pace in the second half of the 19th century, Italy's interest in Tripoli grew. According to TDV, Italy declared war in September 1911 on the pretext of protecting its citizens living in the city from the Ottoman government, and this was the beginning of the province's transformation into an Italian colony; during the occupation a significant part of the city's population perished. By the treaty signed in 1912 at Ouchy in Lausanne, Switzerland, the Ottoman State accepted that it had abandoned to Italy this last province of its in Africa; the caliph, however, would continue to be regarded as the religious leader of the Muslim community there. The treaty did not in fact end the conflict: Ottoman soldiers carried on the resistance together with the local people and fought against Italy under the name 'Africa Groups Command' until the Armistice of Mudros in 1918. Tripoli remained under Italian occupation until 1943, then came under British control, and independence was proclaimed in 1951. The other end of the treaty was in the Aegean: according to TDV's Lausanne article, Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which the Italians kept under occupation although they were obliged to return them under the Treaty of Ouchy, were left to them in 1923 by Article 15 of Lausanne; by Article 22, the rights over Tripoli were also renounced.", "a97d4df9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-vasvar-1664":{"oneri": ["Heavy losses at Saint Gotthard, gains at the table", "e178b653"], "sebep.b": ["Habsburg intervention in Transylvania, Zrínyi's building of a fortress opposite Kanizsa and Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's campaign of 1663, in which he took Uyvar", "c54bfafe"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Vasvár: the withdrawal of both sides from Transylvania, the retention of Uyvar and Novigrad by the Ottomans, and a twenty-year peace", "739b5091"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this treaty as the text that defined the widest Ottoman frontiers in Hungary. It also carries an innovation in legal terms: whereas at Zsitvatorok the peace rested on the sultan's personal pledge, Mehmed IV's ahidname of ratification also bound the rulers who would descend from him — the principle of the continuity of the state. But the peace alienated the Hungarian nobles; this discontent led to the Thököly revolt and, in the second half of the 1670s, to the two empires confronting each other again.", "b98a8982"], "metin": ["The crisis began in Transylvania: after the death of George II Rákóczi, the Ottomans took Várad on 27 August 1660, while the Transylvanian nobles consented to the stationing of German troops in some castles. In the talks held for two years with the Habsburg envoy in Istanbul, Simon Reniger, the 1662 draft of the reisülküttab (chief of the chancery) Şamizade Mehmed Efendi already contained most of the articles later signed; when Vienna dragged its feet, Fazıl Ahmed Pasha set out on campaign in 1663 and took Uyvar after a six-week siege. In 1664, after the unexpected attacks of Zrínyi and Hohenlohe in January and April, the allies besieged Kanizsa at the end of April; the siege could be raised only at the beginning of June, and at the end of June the Ottomans took and burned Zrínyi's Yenikale. On 1 August 1664, Ottoman units attempting to cross the River Rába suffered heavy losses against Montecuccoli's army (Saint Gotthard). This defeat strengthened the desire for peace at the Ottoman headquarters; eight days later the texts were exchanged in Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's tent. Leopold I delayed ratification in the hope of better terms, but because of the condition of his army and the Imperial Diet's reluctance for war, he ratified on 9 September; the confirmation took place with a ceremony near Uyvar on 27 October 1664. A note on the date: the treaty was signed on 9 August 1664; because the date in the Turkish text was wrongly converted, it appears in Western historiography as 10 August.", "598b1e60"]},
"sebep-sonuc-yas-1792":{"oneri": ["The army refused to fight", "d48bcd0d"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's annexation of the Crimea in 1783 and the Ottoman declaration of war on Russia with the aim of recovering the Crimea; Austria's entry into the war on Russia's side as well", "fa10a91f"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Jassy: the Dniester (Turla) became the frontier, and the hope of recovering the Crimea came to an end", "17bec341"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the dream of recovering the Crimea was over, and with Russia's extension into Georgia the danger that would come from the Caucasus became apparent. The army's open declaration that it could not fight made the restructuring of the state in every field urgent; the peace was the starting point of the Nizam-i Cedid reforms.", "b7f66d88"], "metin": ["After the rout at Măcin (9 July 1791), the civil and military notables who gathered in the army camp acknowledged that the army no longer had the strength to fight and sealed a memorandum in favour of peace. The TDV describes this as a 'boycott', in the sense of the army's refusal to fight, without precedent in Ottoman history; Selim III, who wanted the war to continue, was left with no option but peace. Developments in Europe also played a part: Britain had opposed Ochakov (Özi) remaining with Russia with a firmness that went as far as the threat of war, and the danger that the French Revolution posed to monarchies had pushed Catherine II to end the Turkish war. The grand embassies sent by each side to the other after the peace were the last example of this tradition.", "1ecbc422"]},
"sebep-sonuc-zitvatorok-1606":{"oneri": ["Why did a title outweigh a fortress?", "95e3cb68"], "sebep.b": ["The financial straits into which the long war begun in 1593 had driven both sides; the Celali revolts in Anatolia, the Safavid counter-offensive in the east and the revolt of Prince Bocskay of Transylvania against the Habsburgs", "db194902"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Zsitvatorok: a peace in which the Habsburg ruler was regarded as the sultan's equal and the annual tribute was abolished", "b998d332"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, this was the first treaty in Habsburg-Ottoman relations to be decided by joint negotiation rather than depending on the sultan's unilateral goodwill; the two rulers dealt with each other as equal parties, and in diplomatic practice the Ottomans' superiority over their Christian rival came to an end.", "315f14fa"], "metin": ["Peace soundings had been made since 1596 in the winter months, outside the campaigning season, but had produced no result. In November 1604 the revolt of István Bocskay, Prince of Transylvania, against the Habsburgs interrupted the talks; the Hungarian nobles became the decisive party between the two great powers. Grand Vizier Lala Mehmed Pasha at first preferred to continue the war, but he died in June 1606 while preparing for the campaign. The treaty was later extended six times, and its terms remained in force for sixty years.", "2ac692fb"]},
"tabi-devlet-vassallik":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that the direct annexation of Serbia in 1439 and its conversion into a sanjak made distant lands beyond the Danube untenable in terms of the cost of defense and administration", "c33d4b54"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Wallachia, in the Treaty of Edirne-Szeged, in the status of a tributary (vassal) state governed by its own voivode — the model for Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and the Crimea over the following three centuries", "d4f4d91a"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Wallachia (Eflak), this treaty established 'a dual sovereignty': the voivode paid tribute and remained free in internal affairs, and the land was not divided into timars — the exact opposite of the solution applied to Serbia, which in the same period was directly annexed and made into a province.", "28922bc8"], "metin": ["A tributary (vassal) state was the form of government in which the Ottomans, without annexing the territory they had conquered, left the local dynasty at its head in return for tribute and troops. Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania, the Crimean Khanate and the Sharifate of the Hejaz were different forms of this model; what they had in common was that the land was not divided into timars (military fiefs) and that local administration and religion continued. Because it secured the same control more cheaply than direct provincial rule, it was preferred especially in frontier regions that were expensive to defend.", "3eed6869"]},
"tartisma-akdeniz-vehran-onemi":{"baslik": ["Oran (Vehrân): A Port Taken and Lost for Two Centuries — Why Was It Indispensable to the Regency of Algiers?", "b7f260c9"], "metin": ["① Geography itself defended the city. Oran was founded in 290 (903) by members of the Maghrawa tribe; its name means 'two lions' or 'lions' den' in the Tuareg language. The old city was built on both banks of the Ra's al-Ayn valley, and the 375-meter Aïdour hill to its west protected the harbor from westerly winds — in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 'its geographical structure has given the city a position that is sheltered against attacks from outside and strategic'. At a point very close to both Morocco and Spain, it was situated so as to control the sea route in the western Mediterranean. ② It was already a base in the Middle Ages. It suffered destruction repeatedly in the struggle between the Fatimids and the Andalusian Umayyads (297/910, 342/954), and passed successively into the hands of the Banu Ifran, the Zirids, the Almoravids (473/1080, Yusuf ibn Tashfin) and the Almohads; the Almohad ruler had a fleet built here and set out from here on his campaigns to take all of the Maghreb and al-Andalus. Under the Abd al-Wadids (Zayyanids) the city became the port of Tlemcen, the great trading center of the interior; for this reason the Marinids, who besieged Tlemcen in the 14th century, landed troops on the coast of Oran and had a fortress built here. When Granada fell to the Spaniards in 1492, Muslims and Jews from al-Andalus took refuge here and enlarged the city further. ③ 1509: the Spanish landing. On 17 May 1509 the Kingdom of Castile occupied Oran, killed most of its people and took the rest captive; no Muslims or Jews remained in the city. The Spaniards enlarged the walls and fortresses and turned it into a garrison town. ④ Five Ottoman attempts, four of them unsuccessful. Oruç Reis, master of Algiers, blockaded the city in 1516; but because the local rulers were uneasy at the Ottoman presence and cooperated with Spain, Oruç Reis and his brother İshak Reis were killed in 1518. Barbaros Hayreddin Pasha's attempts at recovery were not enough to retake the city either. In 1556, under Salih Pasha, beylerbey (governor-general) of Algiers, Piyale Pasha's expedition came to nothing because of the beylerbey's death. In 1563 Hasan Pasha besieged the city again but was forced to withdraw when help arrived from Spain. In the period up to 1708 the Spaniards had to contend with famine and plague as well as Berber attacks. ⑤ 1708: the first definitive Ottoman conquest — and its loss in 1732. After a long siege, the forces of Mustafa bin Yusuf Bey, who rebuilt Mascara, the center of the Western Beylik of Algiers, and who was popularly known as 'Bouchelaghem' (the big-mustached), received the surrender of Oran in 1708 in the name of the Ottomans; the news aroused great joy in Istanbul, and the bey moved his seat to the city. During the twenty-four years of Ottoman rule maritime trade revived. But in 1732 the city was retaken by Spain; with Spanish rule trade died down again and the population declined. ⑥ The earthquake of 1790 and 12 September 1792: the definitive conquest. When the great earthquake of 1790 devastated the town, the people founded a new settlement on the eastern side of the valley. On 12 September 1792 the Ottoman governor in Mascara, Kuloğlu Mehmed bin Osman, succeeded in finally retaking Oran from the Spaniards; this second conquest too was greeted with great satisfaction in Istanbul, and the people gave Mehmed Bey the title 'Mehmed (Muhammad) el-Kebir' (the Great). In total Spain held the city for 199 (1509-1708) + 60 (1732-1792) = 259 years; the 24-year Ottoman period in between falls outside this continuity. ⑦ Administrative and political importance: it became the capital of a beylik. Mehmed Bey made Oran the new center of the Western Beylik (one of the three sanjaks of the Regency of Algiers, the others being the Eastern/Constantine and Titteri/Médéa) — the TDV article on Algeria (Cezayir) records this as 'the West (center Mascara, Oran after 1792)'; moving the seat of the beylik from Mascara to Oran made the city no longer merely a port but an administrative capital. Mehmed Bey, who had the city rebuilt, settled the Muslim and Jewish population of the surrounding area here, restored to their original use the mosques that had been converted into churches, and built the mosques of Mustafa Pasha and of Mehmed Bey (Jami al-Basha), as well as a palace. ⑧ Economic importance: the gateway to Tlemcen, then the center of European trade. In the Ottoman period the city returned to its position as 'the port of Tlemcen', and maritime trade revived. After the French occupation (4 January 1831) it changed identity: while the Muslim population migrated to the interior, tens of thousands of French and other Europeans settled in the city, and Oran became the most important center of trade with Europe in Algeria. Today, too, its port competes with that of the capital, Algiers; according to 2009 data the population of the city is 852,000, and that of the province of the same name is 1,453,158.", "29bbef91"], "kisa": ["The city changed hands three times from 1509 to 1792 — behind Spain's persistence, amounting to 259 years in total, and the five separate campaigns the Ottomans undertook to win it back lay the same reason: the western gateway of the Maghreb.", "2f34ebac"], "bag": ["Its importance: because of its position, Oran was a gateway that was both easy to defend and valuable to seize — a port close to Morocco and Spain at the same time, controlling the sea route of the western Mediterranean and the trade of Tlemcen. That is why Spain held it at great expense for two and a half centuries, and the Ottomans tried to retake it in five separate campaigns. The definitive conquest of 1792 raised the city from a mere port to the capital of a beylik; only then was the coastline of the Regency of Algiers completed from end to end — and thirty-eight years later it became one of the first landing points of the French.", "aa3d7b1c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-1821-osmanli-ordusu-nerede":{"baslik": ["Where was the Ottoman army while the rebels were taking the towns one by one? The four simultaneous fronts of 1821–1822", "088eff3b"], "metin": ["① The measurable part of the question: how many fronts were there at the same time? Nauplion (Anabolu) fell on 12 December 1822. In that month the military situation of the Ottoman State was as follows. ② The eastern front — Erzurum under siege. According to the TDV article on Iran, using as a pretext the search by Ottoman officials of the caravan that included the shah's wife, who was going on pilgrimage, the Crown Prince Abbas Mirza crossed the border with a large military force in the autumn of 1821, seized the provinces of Kars and Bayazıt and laid siege to Erzurum. ③ The Iraqi front — Baghdad was occupied. According to the same source, Iranian troops simultaneously launched a large-scale attack on Northern Iraq and the province of Baghdad; Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad were occupied and Kirkuk was besieged. What ended the war was not an Ottoman victory: Ottoman resistance and an epidemic that engulfed Azerbaijan forced the Iranians to withdraw; the peace treaty concluded at Erzurum in 1823 stipulated that the Iranian forces would withdraw within sixty days. That is, in the two most critical years of the Greek revolt, the eastern half of the empire was a war zone. ④ The Albanian front — and the detail here is absolutely crucial. Mahmud II dismissed Ali Pasha of Tepelena, the governor of Ioannina, who was behaving semi-independently (March 1820), and sent forces against him. The commander entrusted with this task was Hurşid Ahmed Pasha — and, as the TDV records, Hurşid Pasha was at the same time the man who had been appointed governor of the Morea. That is, when the revolt broke out, the man who should have been governing the Morea was besieging a fortress at Ioannina. Ali Pasha's resistance at Ioannina lasted nearly two years, and in the words of the TDV, during this resistance the Greek revolt became generally widespread. Ali Pasha was killed on 14 February 1822, and his head was brought to Istanbul on 24 February. ⑤ The force he faced was no small one. Ali Pasha had an army of 30,000–40,000 men, 7,000 of them standing troops, and a small navy; the population under his rule is estimated at 1.5–2 million, and his annual revenue amounted to 4–5 million kuruş (TDV). This was not a hunt for bandits but a civil war. The TDV article on Greece states the outcome plainly: the rebellion of Ali Pasha, suppressed in 1822, made the Greek uprising easier and contributed to its success. ⑥ The army itself — it was still the Janissary corps. The Janissary corps stood until 1826, and according to the TDV article on the Janissaries, the series of wars that began in 1768 \"had laid bare that the Janissary army no longer had any military value\". The corps had become an obstacle standing in the way of renewal. The Nizam-i Cedid army founded by Selim III had been disbanded in 1807, and as the TDV records, such implacable enmity had developed between the two systems that even in times of need it had never been possible to use the two forces together. In 1821 the Ottoman State did not have an army trained on the European model. ⑦ And what about Arabia? The Wahhabi front was closed by this date, so it is not included in the list: Diriyah had been besieged on 6 April 1818 and was captured on 9 September 1818 by Egyptian forces under the command of İbrahim Pasha (TDV). But that campaign had been carried out not by the Ottoman army but by the Egyptian army — and this is the clue that leads to the real answer to the question. ⑧ The solution again came from Egypt. The island of Crete was given to Mehmed Ali Pasha so that he would suppress the revolts there, and Egyptian forces landed on Crete in June 1822. As the revolt continued in the Morea, in 1824 the sultan asked Mehmed Ali for help; the Egyptian fleet under the command of İbrahim Pasha landed in the Morea in 1825, made Modon its headquarters and harshly suppressed the revolt (TDV, Greece). The state had become dependent on the army of its own province. ⑨ The short answer: was it weak? According to the sources, the issue was not sheer strength but simultaneity and STRUCTURE. In 1821–1822 the Ottoman State was fighting a state in the east, fighting one of its own governors in the west, grappling with a revolt in the south, and doing all this with a corps which, by its own admission, no longer had any military value. The piling up of these three explains why the towns fell \"one by one\". And the fact that the sultan dared to destroy this corps in 1826, precisely at a time when the external threat was at its height, although it seemed ill-timed, is, in the words of the TDV, an indication of how well he followed developments.", "22e241d0"], "kisa": ["When Nauplion fell, the Ottoman State was occupied in four places at once: Erzurum was under Iranian siege, Baghdad had been occupied, the governor of the Morea was besieging a rebel pasha at Ioannina, and the army itself was still the Janissary corps that was to be abolished in 1826.", "b892b5e7"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of Nauplion was not merely the loss of a fortress; it was the result of a year in which the commander who should have been governing the Morea was tied down at Ioannina, and the eastern army at Erzurum and Baghdad. Shortly afterwards the town was to become the first capital of the new Greek state.", "30043a3c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-hurmuz-bogazi-el-degistirme":{"baslik": ["Whose was the Strait of Hormuz? Six changes of hands in five hundred years", "07d47cc0"], "metin": ["① First the city moved (1300). Hormuz, which gave the strait its name, was originally a port on the mainland. During the Mongol raids, the ruler Qutb al-Din Tahamtan moved the population to the island of Jarun north of the strait, and this place was called New Hormuz. The ruins of Old Hormuz are today 80 km from Minab. The new port soon became the keystone of the maritime trade route between the Persian Gulf and India, and kept this position until the 17th century (TDV). Ibn Battuta describes it as a large, beautiful and busy trading city; the Russian traveller Afanasy Nikitin, who saw the island in 1472, says that people and goods from every part of the world were to be found there. ② Portugal (1507–1622). Afonso de Albuquerque besieged the island in 1507 but turned back without taking it. When he besieged it again seven years later, taking advantage also of the fact that Shah Ismail, the sultan's overlord, was in those years engaged in a struggle with the Ottoman State, he seized the island and made the sultan, who was a vassal of Iran, subject to Portugal (1514). The Portuguese built a strong fortress surrounded by a moat at a commanding spot on the island and brought the whole strait, the entrance to the Gulf and the Iranian coast opposite under their control; they stopped ships bound for the Hejaz and tormented would-be pilgrims (TDV). But their influence was limited: they could not have much effect on the transit trade going from the coast into the interior of Iran, and had to content themselves with the trade of the island's port. ③ The Ottoman attempts — and neither succeeded. A fleet under the command of Piri Reis besieged the island in 1552 but could not take it. Two years later Seydi Ali Reis, while taking the Ottoman fleet at Basra to Suez, encountered Portuguese ships near Hormuz and defeated them. Another campaign was planned in 1570 but could not be carried out (TDV). The Ottoman State established itself in the Gulf by way of Basra and al-Ahsa, but it never got its hands on the key to the strait. ④ Safavid Iran (1622) — and the port was moved across the water. Hormuz was recovered in the time of Shah Abbas I, 108 years after the Portuguese occupation (23 April 1622); the operation was carried out with the support of the fleet of the English East India Company. By the shah's order the port installations on the island were demolished, Gombroon on the opposite shore was built up with their stones, and all commercial activity was shifted to this new town, named Bandar Abbas after the shah; the military installations were left untouched (TDV). From this date Hormuz declined rapidly and turned into today's small fishing town. The centre of gravity of the strait passed from the island to the mainland and never returned. ⑤ Ya'rubi Oman (from the 1620s). The expulsion of the Portuguese from Muscat and its environs took place in the time of Imam Nasir b. Murshid of the Omani Ya'rubi dynasty (1620); for some time longer the Portuguese continued to collect taxes from Hormuz, Julfar and Qalhat. In 1633 Nasir b. Murshid captured Julfar (present-day Ras al-Khaimah) (TDV, Ras al-Khaimah). The naval power of the Ya'rubis extended as far as East Africa: the colonies of Zanzibar and Mombasa attained autonomous administration in this period. ⑥ The Qawasim (18th century). The Qawasim tribe, which had played a major role in freeing the region from the Portuguese, settled on the Julfar coast. When the Ya'rubis went into decline, the tribal sheikh Rahma al-Matari began to act freely and declared his independence at Ras al-Khaimah, which he had founded beside old Julfar. Matari, who built up a strong navy and land army at Ras al-Khaimah and Sharjah, consolidated his control over the traffic of the Strait of Hormuz after Nader Shah was killed (1747) and Iran lost its influence in the Gulf; in the words of the TDV, this situation continued for about a century. At the beginning of the second half of the 18th century, some of the people of Hormuz, troubled by the Qawasim, migrated to Sharjah. ⑦ Busaidi Oman (after 1741) and the leasing of the island. After the death of Sayf b. Sultan II (1743), the period of the Al Bu Said dynasty began with the seizure of power by Imam Ahmad b. Said. In 1762 Ras al-Khaimah, the centre of the Qawasim, agreed by a treaty to submit to Imam Ahmad, and the two sides tried together to counter the Iranian threat (TDV, Oman). Omani interest in Hormuz began with Imam Ahmad; in 1793 his son Sultan b. Ahmad, by fighting, seized a 150-kilometre strip of the Iranian coast, including the islands of Qeshm and Hormuz. Under the treaty made with Iran, the island was leased to the Sultanate of Muscat and Oman from 1798 (TDV, Hormuz). ⑧ Britain (1798–1820) — not the one who held the lock, but the one who decided about the lock. Britain opened an agency in Muscat (18 January 1798), and Omani–British relations began with this agreement. From the first years of the 19th century it signed non-aggression treaties with the Arab emirates, which it brought to consent by using force through the East India Company; in 1819, in an attack, it seized Ras al-Khaimah, held it under occupation for a short time and destroyed the ships in the harbour. Thereupon Emir Sultan b. Saqr, who had withdrawn to Sharjah, was also obliged to sign a non-aggression treaty (1820, TDV Ras al-Khaimah). Both shores of the strait were now within the British maritime order. ⑨ Iran takes it back (1868). In 1855 the lease of Hormuz was taken up again and its term limited to twenty years. In 1868 Shah Nasir al-Din, who had been looking for an opportunity to break the treaty, withdrew the powers and privileges granted by earlier shahs concerning Bandar Abbas and Hormuz; Iranian rule was re-established on the island in place of the Sultanate of Oman (TDV). ⑩ And the last Ottoman claim. At the beginning of the 20th century the Ottoman State was asserting that Hormuz was among its own territories and was preventing Iran's attempt to build a quarantine station there (TDV). This was the very last Ottoman claim over a strait that it had never been able to seize in four hundred years. ⑪ A summary of five hundred years. The Strait of Hormuz changed hands not through the holding of a small island or port on its shore, but according to whose ships the trade passing over that water depended on. Portugal held it with a fortress; Iran took it by pulling down the fortress and moving the port to the opposite shore; Oman and the Qawasim held it with their fleets; and Britain bound them all by treaty without governing any of them.", "a0f318a2"], "kisa": ["The key to the strait never stayed in the same hands for long: the Kingdom of Hormuz, Portugal, Safavid Iran, Ya'rubi and Busaidi Oman, the Qawasim and finally Britain. Each change of hands came together with a change in the direction of a trade route.", "613f8e60"], "bag": ["Significance: This strait, the only outlet of the Persian Gulf, is the narrowest passage in the atlas where the colours of five different states appear in turn. The Ottoman State entered the Gulf from Basra but could never hold the strait; this is also the geographical logic of today's disputes over Hormuz.", "fbd322af"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-misir-osmanli-modernlesme":{"baslik": ["What did Egypt do that the Ottomans could not? The modernisation of a province and that of an empire", "3ded2070"], "metin": ["① First let us correct the wrong question. It is not that the Ottoman State \"could not\" modernise, but that it was able to do so only late. According to the TDV article on Mahmud II, the abolition of the Janissary corps (1826) opened the way for the reforms of the final period of the sultan's reign, which was to last thirteen years, and in this new period the old order and its institutions were granted no right to survive whatsoever. The real question is this: why was Mehmed Ali of Kavala able to do this fifteen years earlier? ② The elimination of the rival caste: 1811 and 1826. Mehmed Ali continued the struggle against the Mamluk beys, whom he saw as the greatest obstacle to his rule; in 1808–1810 he neutralised the beys in Upper Egypt, and on 1 March 1811, at a great banquet he gave in honour of his son Tosun, he had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed (TDV). Mahmud II was able to do the same only on 15 June 1826, and before that he had to spend years in preparation: he made careful appointments to the leading posts of the army, created a new drilled unit under the name of the Eşkinci corps (29 May 1826), and by announcing that these soldiers would begin drill in European-style uniforms on 11 June, he all but drove the Janissaries to revolt; in the words of the TDV, there is no doubt that the last Janissary revolt was not accidental and that it was the sultan's handiwork. ③ The composition of the resistance was different. The TDV describes the first period of Mahmud II as \"the domination of the anti-reform front, which saw its interest in the preservation of the traditional order, was represented chiefly by the military and learned (ilmiye) classes, and was supported by the ayan in Anatolia and Rumelia\". In other words, facing the sultan was a three-legged alliance: the Janissary corps, the ulema and the provincial notables. Nor was the Janissary body simply an army: according to a study based on the Surety Registers of 1791–1793, about 40 per cent of the 1,110 owners of businesses and shops in Istanbul had Janissary connections; the funds of the ortas (Janissary companies) lent money at interest to small tradesmen, and the transport and construction sectors were largely controlled by Janissaries (TDV, Janissaries). To disband an army meant at the same time to dismantle the credit and labour market of the capital. ④ Mehmed Ali also broke the ulema early. The pasha, who increased his control over land through the centralised economic system applied in Egypt, largely broke the power of the ulema, whom he saw as a danger to his rule, by subjecting endowment (waqf) lands to taxation (TDV). What is interesting is that Mahmud II used the same instrument in 1826: an attempt was made to finance the new army through the rational use of endowment wealth, and for this purpose the Ministry of Imperial Endowments was established and all endowment wealth was gathered under its administration. The same remedy, fifteen years apart. ⑤ Scale: a province and an empire. By 1821 Mehmed Ali had registered and taxed all the cultivable land of Lower and Upper Egypt; he abolished the tax-farming (iltizam) system and nationalised the endowment lands, and almost all the land of Egypt became state property (TDV). The Nile valley is a country stretching along a single river, which it is possible to survey and control. To carry out the same operation in an empire stretching from Rumelia to Baghdad, with a separate dynasty of ayan in each of its regions, was an entirely different task. The TDV sums this up as follows: although the case of Mehmed Ali remains the single exception where he failed, Mahmud II succeeded in making the weight of central authority felt over the Ottoman lands to a degree that had not been possible in earlier periods. ⑥ Finance: this is where the real difference lies. Mehmed Ali's system earned the state money — but by taking it from the people. Through the monopoly system, crops were bought very cheaply, and together with heavy taxes this order was \"enriching the administration while impoverishing the people\"; during the pasha's rule the cultivable land doubled, the variety of cotton was improved, and Egypt became one of the world's leading cotton producers (TDV). On the Ottoman side the picture is the reverse: according to the TDV, weakness in the financial sphere was the weakest side of the reign of Mahmud II. The war indemnity paid to Russia was 400 million kuruş — far above the state's total revenues for one year. As a result of devaluation carried out ten times, the silver content of the kuruş fell by 80 per cent. So one side was finding the money for reform, while the other in the same years was losing the value of its money. ⑦ Two programmes side by side. Egypt: in 1809 students began to be sent to Europe; in 1810 the foundation of the navy was laid at Bulaq; in 1816 a school of geometry; in 1820 the first military school in Aswan and the first printing press in Bulaq; in 1827 a medical school; in 1828 the newspaper Vekāyi-i Mısır (Egyptian Events); in 1834 an engineering school; in 1837 the Ministry of Education (TDV). The Ottomans: the Asâkir-i Mansûre-i Muhammediyye (Victorious Soldiers of Muhammad) army in 1826, the Ministry of Imperial Endowments in 1826, Takvîm-i Vekāyi‘ in 1831, the Mekteb-i Maârif-i Adliyye school, the Military Academy in 1835, the Redif (reserve) organisation, the Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances (Meclis-i Vâlâ-yı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye), quarantine (TDV). The list is almost the same; the dates are shifted by ten to fifteen years. ⑧ And the proof came on the battlefield. This is the TDV's clearest sentence: the fact that the regular Egyptian forces, trained by modern methods, brought under control in a short time a rebellion which Ottoman forces lacking any discipline and training had been unable to suppress for years had a significant psychological effect, especially on public opinion in Istanbul; the success of the trained Egyptian forces was perceived as a development that restored the reputation of Selim III and acknowledged that he had been right about reform. In other words, Egypt's success was not an obstacle to Ottoman reform but its trigger. ⑨ Mehmed Ali was not an Egyptian nationalist. The TDV is clear on this point: the pasha's cultural outlook was an Ottoman one, the language he spoke was Turkish, and his view of the world was a typically Ottoman view; to regard him as the forerunner of Egyptian nationalism is a mistaken approach. The two modernisations were not two rival civilisational projects but applications of the same tradition on two different scales. ⑩ The short answer. It was not a single thing that held the Ottomans back: ① the economic and social roots of the rival military caste in the capital, ② the alliance of the Janissary corps, the ulema and the ayan, ③ the impossibility of controlling land and taxation on the scale of an empire, ④ public finances that collapsed under war indemnity and currency depreciation, ⑤ and the fact of fighting on four separate fronts in the same years. What Egypt had was a single valley, a single ruler, and a rival whose elimination had been completed in 1811.", "93eb986f"], "kisa": ["The sources do not give a single cause. Mehmed Ali eliminated his rival military caste in 1811, whereas Mahmud II was able to do the same only in 1826 — and the moment he did so, he launched a reform programme very similar to Egypt's. The fifteen years in between are the difference in scale and resistance.", "72be9f4e"], "bag": ["Significance: Here lies the root of the Egyptian crisis stretching from Kütahya to Nizip. When an empire that disbanded its army in 1826 came face to face with a province that had done so in 1811, the fifteen-year gap came back in the form of the defeats at Konya in 1832 and Nizip in 1839.", "a05f091f"]},
"tartisma-kucuk-kaynarca-rivayetleri":{"baslik": ["Three stories told about Küçük Kaynarca — and the answer of the sources", "854b770e"], "metin": ["① 'The comfort of an elbow': It is told that the second delegate, İbrahim Münib Efendi, dozed off during the talks leaning on his elbow; that on waking and seeing everyone looking at him, he said, as though he had been following the discussion, 'let us come to the matter of the indemnity'; and that he thereby cost the state 4.5 million rubles in an indemnity the Russians had already given up. According to TDV, this has no basis beyond being an invented tale: the Russians never gave up the indemnity and had demanded 50,000 purses at Bucharest; the grand vizier had authorised the chief plenipotentiary to accept a demand of up to 20,000 purses without prolonging the matter and, if necessary, to go up to 40,000 purses. Since the indemnity was a separate article left to the very end, it was natural that it should be raised last.\n\n② 'Revenge for the Pruth': It is asserted that although the treaty was ready on 17 July, the Russians held it back until 21 July, the day on which the Treaty of the Pruth had been signed (Hammer, Zinkeisen, Jorga, Danişmend). According to TDV, the grand vizier's approval arrived on 21 July and the treaty was signed the same day; moreover, the Treaty of the Pruth too had been signed not on 21 but on 22 July and exchanged on the 23rd.\n\n③ 'In memory of Weissmann': The claim that the peace was made at Küçük Kaynarca in memory of the Russian general Weissmann, who had died there a year earlier, also appears only in Hammer; according to TDV, Rumyantsev's route and the fact that the peace could not have been known for certain in advance make such an intention implausible.\n\nNor is the account true that the treaty was 'dictated in a brief meeting of four to seven hours': talks were held for hours on three separate days, and the understandings previously reached at Bucharest were taken as the basis.", "71d21aab"], "kisa": ["A dozing delegate who brought up the indemnity, a date awaited to avenge the Pruth, a village chosen in memory of a general… TDV finds all three unfounded.", "51583f1f"], "not": ["The date of the treaty is also given differently in the sources: archival copies take 16 July, when the talks began, some primary sources take 17 July, and the Russian side takes 26 July, when the texts were exchanged. The date of signature is 21 July 1774.", "a379beab"], "bag": ["All three stories serve the same function: attributing a heavy defeat to an individual's lapse or to the enemy's symbolic calculation. TDV's objection, by contrast, rests on archival documents and contemporary Ottoman sources (Enveri, Vasıf, Ahmed Resmi).", "df4b9abe"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
